      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-21-verification-files-for-the-lean-proof-of-the-riemann-hypothe",
      "title": "Verification files for the Lean proof of the Riemann Hypothesis",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50590441\nPoints: 106\n# Comments: 37",
      "body": "Verification files for the Lean proof of the Riemann Hypothesis\nAnnounced at alpo.ge/remark.pdf on 20 November 2026.\nAndrew Sutherland (MIT), checks run with Claude Code. Page last updated 2026-11-22 04:35 UTC. Results below are as of that time and this page will be updated as the remaining checks finish.\nThe published Lean source compiles without errors, the five headline theorems depend only on the three standard axioms, every module has been replayed through the Lean kernel, two independently written kernels, nanoda and con-leche, accept the full exported proof, and lean4lean accepts every compiled module. The --compare modes of con-leche and of its Rust port con-ron, independent implementations of Comparator's statement matching, each confirm all five theorems against the challenge. An independent sandboxed lake build of the archive reproduces the export byte for byte. The lake comparator run and the Lean-kernel replay of the export are still in progress.\nAll 175,187 modules compile (Lean v4.33.1, Mathlib v4.33.1)passzero errors; finished 16:43:21 UTC\n#print axioms for the five theorems in Main.leanpasspropext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound only (audit.log)\nNo axioms declared outside Lean corepass15 axioms in the environment, all in Init\nMathlib files identical to the official cachepass8,322 Mathlib and 378 dependency .olean files, byte for byte\nIndependent rebuild with lake build Main in a sandbox (no network, read-only source), on a separate machinepassexit 0, zero errors; finished 18:07:21 UTC on 21 November (timeline, script)\nExport made inside that sandbox, compared with the published exportidentical61,570,595,788 bytes, sha256 5ea74b91…244e; finished 19:32:56 UTC\nCompiled files of the sandboxed lake build, compared with our manifest175,186 of 175,187 identicalthe one differing module compiles non-deterministically; see the note below (its manifest)\nleanchecker kernel replay of every modulepass175,187 of 175,187; zero rejections; finished about 18:17 UTC\nChallenge export equals the one published by Deicyde/riemann-hypothesis CImatchsha256 bd484ac9…b2b9\nnanoda (independent Rust kernel) on the full exportacceptsexit 0 at 19:05:45 UTC; 27 min 42 s on 80 threads (nanoda.log)\nLean default kernel and Lean paranoid kernel on the exportrunningsingle-threaded; no verdict yet\nlake comparator (Lean v4.36.0) end to endrunningstatement comparison and axiom check already passed in one run; kernels in progress\ncon-leche master (cd2eceab) on the full exportaccepts1,468,418 declarations accepted in --verified mode; exit 0 at 21:19:04 UTC; 3 min 15 s on 64 threads (log)\ncon-leche --compare (branch compare, 5dc36dc1): challenge against solutionacceptsall five theorems \"justified by the solution\"; 51,692 challenge declarations accepted; exit 0 at 21:19:12 UTC (log)\ncon-ron --compare (master, 3f22699b): challenge against solutionacceptsall five theorems \"justified by the solution\"; 1,468,418 solution and 51,692 challenge declarations accepted in --verified mode; exit 0 at 23:02:33 UTC on 21 November; 7 min 6 s on 64 threads (log)\nlean4lean (67f63b0c, built with Lean v4.33.1) on the compiled files, module by modulepass175,187 of 175,187; finished about 21:55 UTC. 175,182 passed with default settings. Five hit lean4lean's default step limit (\"deterministic timeout\") on decide +kernel computations and passed when rerun with the limit raised (timeouts, reruns with flags)\ncon-leche and con-ron as bundled with Lean v4.36.0declinedexit 2, not a rejection: those older binaries do not support one reflexive inductive type in the proof. con-leche and con-ron built from master, above, do. (log, log)\nlean4lean bundled with Lean v4.36.0not applicablecannot read a v4.33 core library (unknown constant 'Decidable.intro')\nFive theorems, proved in the submission's Main.lean. Their statements are below exactly as they appear in the challenge file, which imports only Mathlib. All the mathematical notions in them (riemannZeta, DirichletCharacter, DirichletCharacter.LFunction, IsPrimitive) are Mathlib's.\n1. The Riemann Hypothesis, using Mathlib's own definition (Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/RiemannZeta.lean): every zero of ζ other than the trivial zeros −2, −4, −6, … has real part 1/2. The condition s ≠ 1 is there because Mathlib assigns ζ a conventional value at its pole.\ndef RiemannHypothesis : Prop :=\n∀ (s : ℂ) (_ : riemannZeta s = 0) (_ : ¬∃ n : ℕ, s = -2 * (n + 1)) (_ : s ≠ 1), s.re = 1 / 2\n2. The same statement written out in the submission's own words. Lean accepts the two as equal by definition.\n∀ s : ℂ, riemannZeta s = 0 → (¬∃ n : ℕ, s = -2 * ((n : ℂ) + 1)) → s ≠ 1 → s.re = 1 / 2\n3. No zeros to the right of the critical line, for every Dirichlet L-function. For every modulus N ≥ 1 and every Dirichlet character χ modulo N, primitive or not, L(χ, s) ≠ 0 whenever Re s > 1/2. The one excluded case is the trivial character at s = 1, where the L-function has its pole.\n∀ (N : ℕ) [NeZero N] (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N) (s : ℂ),\n1 / 2 < s.re → (χ ≠ 1 ∨ s ≠ 1) → DirichletCharacter.LFunction χ s ≠ 0\n4. Zeros in the critical strip lie on the critical line, for every Dirichlet L-function.\n∀ (N : ℕ) [NeZero N] (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N) (s : ℂ),\n0 < s.re → s.re < 1 → DirichletCharacter.LFunction χ s = 0 → s.re = 1 / 2\n5. The same for primitive characters, the form in which the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis for Dirichlet L-functions is usually stated.\n∀ (N : ℕ) [NeZero N] (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N), χ.IsPrimitive →\n∀ s : ℂ, 0 < s.re → s.re < 1 → DirichletCharacter.LFunction χ s = 0 → s.re = 1 / 2\nScope. \"GRH\" here means Dirichlet L-functions: all moduli and all characters. Dedekind zeta functions, Hecke L-functions and automorphic L-functions in general are not among the statements that were checked.\nAs a check that does not depend on Mathlib's definition of the zeta function, the proof was combined with kimihiro64/Robin1984 (commit acab1a3), an independent formalization of Robin's and Lagarias's criteria on the same Lean and Mathlib versions. Importing both gives two theorems about the divisor sum, with no hypotheses and no mention of zeta (ArithSolution.lean; statements alone in ArithChallenge.lean, which imports only Mathlib):\n∀ n : ℕ, 5040 < n →\n((ArithmeticFunction.sigma 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) <\nReal.exp Real.eulerMascheroniConstant * (n : ℝ) * Real.log (Real.log (n : ℝ))\n∀ n : ℕ, 0 < n →\n((ArithmeticFunction.sigma 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) ≤\n(harmonic n : ℝ) + Real.exp (harmonic n : ℝ) * Real.log (harmonic n : ℝ)\nRobin1984 on its own (its three equivalences with RH)ResultDetail\nRobin1984 builds with lake buildpass4,435 jobs, exit 0\ncon-leche master and --compareaccepts69,293 declarations accepted; all three theorems justified against its own challenge (log)\nUnmodified Comparator v4.33.0 with the repository's own configpassnanoda accepts, Lean default kernel accepts, \"Your solution is okay!\"; exit 0 at 03:31:06 UTC (log summary)\nlake comparator (Lean v4.36.0) from exportspassLean paranoid kernel, nanoda and Lean default kernel all accept; \"Your solution is okay!\"; exit 0 at 04:16:52 UTC (log summary)\nThe two inequalities (RH proof combined with Robin1984)ResultDetail\nrobin_inequality and lagarias_inequality compile; #print axiomspasspropext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound only (audit)\ncon-leche master on the full combined exportaccepts1,472,825 declarations accepted in --verified mode; exit 0 at 02:29:56 UTC (log)\ncon-leche --compare against ArithChallengeacceptsboth theorems \"justified by the solution\"; exit 0 at 02:29:06 UTC (log)\ncon-ron --compare (master, 3f22699b) against ArithChallengeacceptsboth theorems \"justified by the solution\"; 1,472,825 solution declarations accepted; exit 0 at 23:15:58 UTC on 21 November (log)\nnanoda on the full combined exportacceptsexit 0 at 02:56:46 UTC; 33 min 46 s on 64 threads (log)\nlake comparator (Lean v4.36.0) on the combined exportrunningstatement comparison and axiom check passed; its Lean kernel stages are single-threaded on a 62 GB export and are expected to take many hours, as for the main proof\nThe combined export is 61,752,134,938 bytes, sha256 44651dcc482a6e06e8d626994221aff638e422fded189bf856e0c31176926f0d; its challenge export has sha256 088a57c9e7454fa5279a86a825e551828ac8431694855aadd1f6e6a6eb37bb1d. These statements still rely on Mathlib's definitions of the divisor sum, Euler's constant, harmonic numbers, exp and log, and on the Robin1984 proof, which is why that proof was put through the same checks.\nThe elaborated statement of the Lagarias inequality\nThe kernels check elaborated terms, not source text, so what was verified is the term that Lean's elaborator produces from the statement. For the Lagarias inequality that term is short enough to read in full. This is the statement with every coercion, instance and implicit argument shown, as elaborated against Mathlib alone (Lean v4.33.1, Mathlib 0df444a3), with none of the proof loaded:\ndef lagariasStmt : Prop :=\n@LT.lt Nat instLTNat (@OfNat.ofNat Nat (nat_lit 0) (instOfNatNat (nat_lit 0))) n →\n(@DFunLike.coe (@ArithmeticFunction Nat (@MulZeroClass.toZero Nat Nat.instMulZeroClass)) Nat (fun x => Nat)\n(@ArithmeticFunction.instFunLikeNat Nat (@MulZeroClass.toZero Nat Nat.instMulZeroClass))\n(ArithmeticFunction.sigma (@OfNat.ofNat Nat (nat_lit 1) (instOfNatNat (nat_lit 1)))) n))\n(@HAdd.hAdd Real Real Real (@instHAdd Real Real.instAdd) (@Rat.cast Real Real.instRatCast (harmonic n))\n(@HMul.hMul Real Real Real (@instHMul Real Real.instMul)\n(Real.exp (@Rat.cast Real Real.instRatCast (harmonic n)))\n(Real.log (@Rat.cast Real Real.instRatCast (harmonic n)))))\nIt names 27 constants directly: Nat, Real, the order and arithmetic instances on them, the casts from Nat and Rat to Real, ArithmeticFunction.sigma, harmonic, Real.exp and Real.log. Its meaning depends on 1,801 Mathlib and core declarations in all, counting definitions with their bodies and theorems by their statements only (a proof cannot change what a definition means). The corresponding count for Robin's inequality is 2,050; the difference is mostly the definition of Euler's constant.\nFiles: the script, its output (both statements), and the dependency lists for Lagarias and Robin.\nThe exported proof: output of lean4export on module Main. Unpacks to 61,570,595,788 bytes.\nThe exported challenge: the five statements with sorry proofs, importing only Mathlib. Unpacks to 350,862,970 bytes.\ncomparator.json: Comparator config naming the checkers that can process this proof\nSHA256SUMS: hashes of everything on this page\naudit.log and Audit.lean: the axiom audit and the file that produced it\nproject-oleans.sha256: sha256 of each of the 175,187 compiled files (29 MB). These hashes depend on the build directory; see the note below.\nnanoda.log: timing and exit status of the nanoda run\nsandbox-timeline.txt, sandbox-build.sh, sandbox-build-continue.sh and sandbox-project-oleans.sha256: the sandboxed lake build, its scripts and its per-file hashes (29 MB)\ncon-leche-master.log and con-leche-compare.log: the two con-leche runs\nWith Lean v4.36.0 installed, nothing needs to be built. You need roughly 100 GB of RAM and 70 GB of disk.\nzstd -d --long=30 solution.ndjson.zst      # --long=30 is required\nsha256sum solution.ndjson challenge.ndjson  # compare with the hashes above\nlake comparator --config comparator.json \\\nnanoda alone took about 28 minutes on 80 threads. The Lean kernels are single-threaded and take much longer.\nThe fastest independent check is con-leche built from source (Lean v4.36.0, about two minutes to build). It needs about 50 GB of RAM and took under four minutes on 64 threads:\ngit clone https://github.com/leanprover/con-leche && cd con-leche\ngit checkout compare        # 5dc36dc1; master (cd2eceab) for the plain check\nSource archivelean.tar.gz, the 14 parts from alpo.ge joined; 239,275,554 bytessha256 9631b4f020199e580ba86178cb56c6e9b09f690c2369a80dc4334aa93f49c7fc\nSource mirrorDeicyde/riemann-hypothesis, byte-identical to the archive apart from the Comparator files it adds\nLean / MathlibLean v4.33.1 (commit 819816b2); Mathlib tag v4.33.1 (0df444a3)\nExporterlean4export tag v4.33.0 (15f6055e), built with Lean v4.33.1; target list as in Deicyde's export-targets.txt\nJudge and extra kernelsLean v4.36.0: lake comparator, leanchecker, leanchecker-paranoid, nanoda_bin\nlean4leandigama0/lean4lean at 67f63b0c, built with Lean v4.33.1; run on the compiled .olean files, not the export\ncon-lecheleanprover/con-leche master at cd2eceab and branch compare at 5dc36dc1, each built with Lean v4.36.0\ncon-ronleanprover/con-ron master at 3f22699b, the Rust port of con-leche, built with cargo build --release\nManifest of all compiled filessha256 375cbee2fc3fc0f5088ef2e6dc9a5ac5530924310dc62422ec65aac9dc4c1d25specific to the build directory /home/ubuntu/grh/src/lean, and one file in it is not deterministic; see the note below\nBuild directorythe archive's lean/ directory was at /home/ubuntu/grh/src/lean on every build machine, so each source file was passed to Lean as /home/ubuntu/grh/src/lean/src/<Module/Path>.lean\nHow the original build was done, and the rebuild that checks it. The original build ran on cloud machines without a sandbox, and compiled each file by calling lean directly with the same inputs lake supplies (module name, package name and the lakefile's options), not through lake build. All the kernel checks above were run on the export from that build. We then repeated the build on a separate machine with lake build Main inside a bubblewrap sandbox: no network, the source tree read-only, only .lake writable, the archive downloaded afresh from the author's site, and Mathlib's files checked against the official cache first. The export made inside that sandbox is byte-identical to the published one, so every check on the published export applies to it.\nThe per-file .olean hashes depend on the build directory, and one module is not deterministic. In Lean v4.33.1 a compiled file stores each linter message together with the absolute path of its source file. Of our 175,187 compiled files, 98,219 contain the path /home/ubuntu/grh/src/lean/src/…; the other 76,968, including Main.olean, contain no path. So project-oleans.sha256 can be reproduced only by a build whose source tree is at /home/ubuntu/grh/src/lean; elsewhere, only the files of modules that emit no linter messages should match.\nThe sandboxed lake build was run at that same path, and 175,186 of its 175,187 files are byte-identical to our manifest. The exception is Formalism/LocalToGlobal/SiegelLatticeTheory/CokernelGLCounts/RankPointCounts/LocModel.olean: same size, different contents. Recompiling that one module six times on one machine with identical inputs gave one output four times and the other twice, and those are exactly the two hashes in the two manifests. The two outputs differ in 38 bytes: the name of one bound variable in BBtop.eq_1, an equation lemma that Lean generates automatically the first time a proof asks for it. Two adjacent theorems in the file both ask for it, Lean elaborates theorem bodies in parallel, and the lemma takes that variable's name from whichever theorem gets there first. Bound-variable names play no part in kernel checking, so the two files are logically the same. The export is not affected, and the export from the sandboxed build is byte-identical to ours: an export contains only the declarations that the five theorems depend on, and BBtop.eq_1 is not one of them, because the proofs that caused it to be generated do not refer to it. We checked that this name does not occur in the export. Had a proof on the dependency path referred to such a lemma by name, two builds could have produced exports with different hashes, although every check would still have passed on either. Compiled single-threaded (lean -j1), the module gave the same output six times out of six. A ten-line file with no imports reproduces the behaviour: on Lean v4.33.1 and v4.34.1 it compiles to two different outputs with -j32 and to one with -j1; on v4.36.0 it gave one output in 40 runs. The same race, with the first caller's options in place of its names, was reported as Lean issue #15498 and fixed by pull request #15499, first released in v4.36.0. That change also removes this behaviour: the file gives two outputs on the nightly builds of 7 and 8 October 2026 and one output in 40 runs on those of 9 and 10 October and on the toolchain built from the pull request alone. Because of this module, the sha256 of the whole manifest file is not reproducible even at the same path; compare file by file.\nA passing check shows that the formal statements, as written in Lean against Mathlib's definitions, follow from the three standard axioms according to the kernels used. It relies on those kernels and on Mathlib's definitions being what they are intended to be.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Bluestein",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T15:54:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/",
