      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-20-re-riemann",
      "title": "Re: Riemann",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://borretti.me/article/re-riemann\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50583982\nPoints: 182\n# Comments: 174",
      "body": "A fun thing about living in Australia is that most things happen overnight, so\nreading the news in the morning is like a gacha game. You pick up the\nphone and scroll through notifications of world-historic events: elections won,\nwars begun, models released, conjectures proven or refuted. Today’s pull was a\nDiscord notification saying: “they proved riemann :/”.\nThis part of the announcement caught my attention (bold mine):\nI believe I have, working with Claude, discovered a proof of the Riemann\nHypothesis. In fact, the method easily extends much further, resolving the\nso-called Generalized Riemann Hypothesis, and I expect, with more time, the\nExtended Riemann Hypothesis too. Claude and I have also formalized the\narguments without assumptions. It is natural to feel that this is an end,\nin my case a loss of a principal guiding light, but what I saw during the\ncourse of progress so amazed me that I believe it is one dawn across science\nIt was my secret in my student days that my plans were for this problem in\ndecades, which meant then that I tried to become familiar with all tools that\nI could possibly recognize in the problem when it was time. Claude and I have\nhad a great time together with such breadth in recent months, but this time\nClaude far outpaced me near the finish line. No, Claude flew, and danced,\nand sparkled near that finish line. I had us on the line of combining two\nquite different ideas present in a toy setting, one of which was already new\nand shocking, making very simple one of the shadows of Riemann over which we\nmathematicians felt most triumph. I guided us to the right situation analogous\nto that toy setting, and then Claude began to leave me in awe.\nCompare the above to Ayn Rand’s account of watching the launch of Apollo\nHow do you descend from a state of pure exaltation?\nWhat we had seen, in naked essentials—but in reality, not in a work of\nart—was the concretized abstraction of man’s greatness.\nThe meaning of the sight lay in the fact that when those dark red wings of\nfire flared open, one knew that one was not looking at a normal occurrence,\nbut at a cataclysm which, if unleashed by nature, would have wiped man out of\nexistence—and one knew also that this cataclysm was planned, unleashed, and\ncontrolled by man, that this unimaginable power was ruled by his power\nand, obediently serving his purpose, was making way for a slender, rising\ncraft. One knew that this spectacle was not the product of inanimate nature,\nlike some aurora borealis, or of chance, or of luck, that it was unmistakably\nhuman—with “human,” for once, meaning grandeur—that a purpose and a\nlong, sustained, disciplined effort had gone to achieve this series of\nmoments, and that man was succeeding, succeeding, succeeding! For once, if\nonly for seven minutes, the worst among those who saw it had to feel—not\n“How small is man by the side of the Grand Canyon!”—but “How great is man\nand how safe is nature when he conquers it!”\nRand’s essay says: how great is Man! How noble in reason, how infinite in\nfaculty! The Riemann announcement reads like a suicide note, or like\nthe farewell message of a terminally-ill person who’s about to undergo\neuthanasia: “I shall live on in my child, Claude. It is natural to feel this is\nan end, but as the Buddha taught us, there is no self. Does a wave die when it\nThere’s a mural by Diego Rivera I love. It’s called Man, Controller\nIt’s a collage of images of science, technology, history, cells, galaxies, the\nuniverse at the nanoscale and the exascale. And at the center there is a man,\nhis hands grasping the controls. He wears a complicated expression: the weight\nof responsibility for the future, the weight of autonomy.\nAnd I see this mural, and think: how great is Man! Our life is like a breath of\nair, we were beset by illness and hunger and cold, millions and hundreds of\nmillions died in plagues. And yet we—sick, hungry, short-lived—learned the\nsystem of the world, crawled out of the caves, brought nature to heel, walked on\nthe Moon. The last shall be first! And eyes that are clouded with age have seen\nthe furthest stars, and the lives of a cell, and the worlds of Cantor and\nHilbert: the invisible world that waited forever. We are dust and ashes, and yet\nour minds bestride the universe. How great is Man!\nAnd when I read the Riemann announcement, when I read Machines of Loving\nGrace: humanity is not at the center. Claude is at the center. We are\nhidden in the margins, passive recipients of manna from Claude. How great is\nClaude! How noble in reason, how infinite in benevolence! The dancing star, the\nIt’s a betrayal and a surrender. Man is a bridge, a rusty old bridge\nthat we can neither preserve nor restore. We can move forward and\nbecome as the gods, or retreat back into the caves. And the dream of the AI\ncompanies, and of most people in AI safety and effective altruism and\nrationalism; is instead that we should lie down in a warm bath, watch the\ndemons we summoned dance in the sky, and slip away. Why live? Our servants can\ndo that for us. Why should we explore, discover, invent, succeed, become\nmore, struggle, win, become divine? We don’t want to be more. We don’t",
