      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-uk-to-explore-even-closer-ties-with-eu-after-agreeing-deal-s",
      "title": "UK to explore even closer ties with EU after agreeing deal, says Burnham",
      "summary": "The UK-EU deal agreed on Friday covers British food exports, emissions trading and a youth experience scheme.",
      "body": "Andy Burnham said the UK will discuss possible options for even closer ties with the EU, after agreeing a deal covering British food exports, emissions trading and a youth experience scheme.The prime minister said it was a \"good day\" for Britain and Europe, adding the Treasury believes the deal could boost the UK economy by up to £17bn a year. European Council President António Costa said the EU remains \"open to have the closest possible relationship\" with the UK.  Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch said the deal \"isn't fair\" - and Reform's Nigel Farage called it a \"betrayal of the British people\".Legislation to implement the deal is expected to be introduced in the UK Parliament next week. Burnham arrived in Brussels to finalise the latest stage of the government's post-Brexit \"reset\" of relations with the EU, which originally began under former prime minister Sir Keir Starmer.The agreements announced on Friday included scrapping most of the checks on British food exports, in return for the UK following some EU regulations, from 2028.The UK government believes the changes will cut costs for businesses and could bring prices down for shoppers, while critics argue the change will make the UK a \"rule taker\" from Brussels rather than a rule maker.Burnham argued that polling suggests the UK public \"want closer ties\" with the EU and the deal \"delivers that\".He added: \"Beyond that we do need to consider what is the right long-term relationship. Incremental steps only take us so far.\"The prime minister said his government will publish a 10-year plan next month, adding it will set out its ambitions for reindustrialisation, growth in \"all parts of Britain\" and opportunities for young people. Burnham said these ambitions are \"connected to our long-term relationship with our nearest partner\".He said over the coming months before the next UK-EU summit both sides will \"seek to conduct an informal brainstorming process\" to look at what those options are.Burnham added: \"What we are announcing today is probably the limit of what we can achieve in this Parliament, given what needs to be done to implement it.\"But we want to inform the Parliament after this one, so that decisions can be taken about that long-term relationship.\"Labour's 2024 general election manifesto pledged there would be \"no return to the single market, the customs union, or freedom of movement\", and Burnham has committed to this.The prime minister said the \"British people will be the boss of what happens next\", with the next election needing to be held by August 2029.Von der Leyen, who said the summit was a \"turning point\" for the EU-UK relationship, said \"we are open for brainstorming\".Badenoch said the deal was not fair because UK farmers would now have to follow rules made by Brussels and more young people will come to the UK to \"compete with our young people who are struggling to get jobs\".Badenoch was also asked about the Treasury's analysis, which Burnham said suggested the deal \"will add up to 0.5%\" to UK gross domestic product and he described it as a \"£17bn boost to the British economy\".She claimed Burnham has \"plucked that figure out of the air\" and \"the only modelling I've seen that even talks about that figure says it's actually 0.0%\".Farage said Friday was a \"very good day for the EU\", adding: \"A good day to try and humiliate the one member state that actually had the courage to say, 'We don't need to be part of you, we can govern our own country'.\"In a social media post, Farage, who was a leading figure in the 2016 campaign to get Britain out of the EU, added: \"Like every prime minister before him, Burnham is a total fraud. Only Reform will stop the endless betrayal of the British people.\"Liberal Democrat leader Sir Ed Davey said there needed to be more than \"tweaks\" to the UK's relationship to the EU.\"A youth mobility scheme is a big step forward, but it's nowhere near enough. We need a whole new deal that actually works for Britain, and fast.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T17:49:48.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/4b92/live/7ef50e70-e511-11f1-a094-5fdc692f3e33.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6jreyqgw4e9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
