      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-calls-for-consent-defence-change-after-joanne-young-rape-cas",
      "title": "Calls for consent defence change after Joanne Young rape case",
      "summary": "Joanne Young's daughter joins her mother in calling for a change to the law on consent.",
      "body": "The daughter of Joanne Young, who was drugged, raped and advertised for sex online by her husband, is among those calling for a change in the way cases are defended in court.Philip Young drugged and abused his wife over a 13-year period, using websites and apps to invite men to their home in Swindon without her knowledge. On Thursday, four men were found guilty of sexual offences against her.The trial has sparked calls for the government to ensure defendants cannot use \"third-party consent\" as a defence in rape trials.Daughter Alex Young said: \"It has been 23 years since this was last updated. Society has progressed, crime has progressed. So why hasn't the law progressed with it?\"Warning: This story discusses sexual violence and abusePhilip Young advertised his wife for sex using websites such as Craigslist, Grindr and FabSwingers without her knowledge.During the trial, the prosecution stated that the four men, who were all strangers to each other, failed to seek consent from Joanne Young before they engaged in sexual activity with her.The defendants claimed they had been duped by Philip Young and believed she was consenting.The Sexual Offences Act 2003 states that \"a person consents if he agrees by choice, and has the freedom and capacity to make that choice\".There is no defined law which states third-party consent is a valid defence.In a post on LinkedIn, Alex Young said: \"We live in a world where there is debate over whether third-party consent is enough to have sex with someone... and that is enough to use it as an argument in a court of law.\"This is simply unacceptable.\"Her calls echo those made by her mother, who spoke on the steps of Winchester Crown Court after the trial.\"No-one should be able to give it [consent] on your behalf,\" said Joanne Young, who has waived her right to anonymity.\"I urge the government to look at this as a priority so that women are properly protected in future.\"Kate Parker, criminal barrister and founder of the Schools Consent Project, said legally, a third party or a partner cannot consent on a person's behalf. \"The law makes that very clear. A person consents if he or she agrees by choice and has the freedom and capacity to make that choice,\" she added.\"In a case like this where someone has been made unconscious, there is no possible way that that person is able to consent because of course they have no capacity.\"She added that the defendants' claims that they thought Joanne Young had consented through her husband \"doesn't make sense legally, and that's not a legal position that can be supported in court\".Parker said the defence used by the four men in the trial is not a correct application of the law but might be something defence lawyers would try and rely on as their client's genuine belief in their victim's consent, or in mitigation to try and get a lower sentence.Like Joanne Young, Laura Bishop from Bristol has also waived her right to anonymity after her ex-husband received a 14-year prison sentence for drugging and raping her while inviting other men to abuse her.Speaking to BBC Radio 4's Today Programme, Bishop said the crimes will affect her for the rest of her life. \"It's something that unfortunately you can never get over,\" she said.\"You have to try and remain positive and optimistic but the betrayal, particularly by men, is very difficult to move on from.\"Bishop has a PhD in cyber crime and uses her expertise and experience to work with MPs and charities to improve governance around digital safety and regulations.\"The sites my husband used to advertise me are still active and are known by the police to not provide evidence and forensics when asked. \"So why are they still accessible?\" she said.Bishop understands why many choose to maintain their anonymity but wants to encourage those who can speak out to do so.\"There's a million reasons why its not be appropriate to waive your anonymity and it might just be because it's something you can't actively do,\" she said.\"But for those of us that are able to, we have to stand strong for victims.\"Debbie Beadle, chief executive officer at Wiltshire-based domestic abuse charity Fear Free, also hopes that Joanne Young's decision to speak out will encourage others to do so.\"Six in seven rapes against women are done by someone they know and this adds a barrier to people coming forward,\" she said.\"The fact that Joanne has come forward will help others when this has happened to them.\"Zoe Watts is the founder of a global campaign called End Eye Check that works to raise awareness of drug-facilitated sexual abuse.Like Joanne Young and Bishop, Watts waived her right to anonymity after she was drugged and raped by her husband.Watts hopes that Joanne Young's case \"sets a precedent\", but told Woman's Hour that the crime is more widespread than many realise.\"I have had women contact me from 25 different countries,\" she said. \"It's a daily process of women either contacting me because they've woken up to find their husband having sex with them while they're asleep and have done on numerous occasions.\"Or perhaps they're not sure if they've been drugged or maybe they are genuinely asleep and wake up and find him that way, or women that have found videos or recordings on their phone of things they haven't consented to,\" Watts added.If you have been affected by any of the issues raised in this story, support and information is available at BBC Action Line.Professor Miranda Horvath, director of the University of Suffolk's Institute for Social Justice and Crime, told the BBC that more needs to be done to tackle and \"de-platform\" perpetrators.\"The research being conducted and the evidence base we have is emerging [because] these kinds of offences are under-reported and under-detected,\" she said.\"We need to ensure proper implementation of the legislation we have through suspect-focused investigation, prosecutions and resourcing and training of the professionals working in these environments.\"[We also need to be] holding the platforms and tech firms to account that are continuing to proliferate this material and saying 'this is enough, we will not tolerate this in our society'.\"Prosecutors said Thursday's trial was the first of its kind in England, but former minister for safeguarding and violence against women and girls, Jess Phillips, told Radio 4's Woman's Hour that current laws do not do enough to protect women.\"The law says that somebody can seek a defence to defend themselves against a rape claim if they reasonably believe that they had consent,\" said Phillips.\"These people went on to argue that they reasonably believed that Joanne's husband had sought her consent and got it. \"They were found guilty, but it's whether the law needs to explicitly say, 'you cannot reasonably believe someone's given consent unless they have given it'.\"If there was clarity in the law, Joanne wouldn't have been put through the court case,\" Phillips added.She also believes that more needs to be done to hold the websites hosting adverts and similar content to account.\"Can every pornographic site in our country categorically say that not one of these snuff videos or sedated videos is on their site? \"Unless they can guarantee it with a signed piece of paper from the person that they know is in that video [they should not be published],\" Phillips said.Sarah Hammond, the chief crown prosecutor at the CPS who oversees many complex sexual offence cases involving drugs, said Joanne's \"incredibly powerful\" statement after the convictions \"laid bare the devastating impact of the horrific abuse she had suffered over so many years\". \"A lot of people may not know exactly what consent means in law, but the principle is simple. Consent must be a free choice made by someone who has the freedom and capacity to make it, and nobody else can give consent on their behalf,\" she said.\"In this case, the evidence proved that not only did Joanne not consent to the sexual activity, but that the perpetrators could not reasonably believe that she was consenting.\"This meant asking what steps the perpetrators had taken to establish consent for themselves, rather than relying on assumptions or what somebody else had told them.\"The convictions of Philip Young, Dean Hamilton, Norman Macksoni, Connor Sanderson-Doyle, Richard Wilkins and Mohammed Hassan send a clear message: consent is something you establish with the person themselves, and where someone is drugged or otherwise incapacitated, they cannot make that choice.\"Wiltshire Police Supt Int Geoff Smith, the lead investigating officer in the case, said he hoped the guilty verdicts made women feel \"safer\". \"I hope that men take note about seeking consent and what's required from them, and I hope that women feel more able and confident to come forward and speak out,\" he said.\"I really hope it makes a difference.\"",
