      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-uk-braces-for-coldest-night-of-autumn-so-far-with-temperatur",
      "title": "UK braces for coldest night of autumn so far with temperature of -10C forecast",
      "summary": "Friday night is expected to be the coldest of the autumn so far with temperatures dropping as low as minus 10C, as Simon King explains.",
      "body": "The coldest night of the autumn so far was on Thursday night when the temperature got down to minus 8.4C (18F) at Tulloch Bridge, Highland.Temperatures for many of us have been up and down recently due to cold Arctic air temporarily sweeping southward across the UK.Southern areas of the UK have mostly held onto milder air from the Atlantic.During Friday though a northerly wind will once again bring some of the colder air from the Arctic, and bring a colder day for most of the UK.Any early cloud and rain in southern England will clear and for most of the UK it will be a bright day with some sunshine.  A few wintry showers are possible across Scotland.By night, temperatures will drop with frost expected widely, mostly across the Midlands, northern England, Northern Ireland and Scotland.Although it is likely to be the coldest night of the autumn so far, with minus 10C predicted in rural Scotland, we are a long way off the coldest November night on record, when a temperature of minus 16.1C was recorded in Cumbria in 1912.  We came closer to that record low temperature in 2025, when the UK experienced the coldest November night in 12 years with minus 12.6C in Moray.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T08:12:15.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_weather/1200/cpsprodpb/deb6/live/abdec670-e4c5-11f1-a094-5fdc692f3e33.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/weather/articles/c30rkzwvwy49o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-care-home-predator-sexually-assaulted-my-mum-despite-warning",
      "title": "Care home predator sexually assaulted my mum despite warnings",
      "summary": "John said the \"horrific\" sex attack took place despite concerns previously being raised by staff.",
      "body": "Documents seen by the BBC show that safeguarding concerns over McAuley's behaviour were raised by three staff members at the care home in the months before the attack.The documents show that McAuley was reported for making sexualised remarks and inappropriate conduct. He had slapped John's mother, who we are calling Mary, on the bottom as well as similar behaviour with two female staff members. McAulay was also reported for giving Mary food that she was not allowed to eat and touching her thighs.One staff member told management that McAuley was \"grooming\" John's mum.Then, on a Saturday night in late 2023, John got a call from the care home to tell him his mother, who was in her 80s, had been seriously sexually assaulted.\"When I got to the home it was full of police cars,\" he said.\"They called the rape team in and that's when the penny was starting to drop and I was told exactly what happened, and I was shocked.\"John said his mum, who had limited speech due to her condition, was very subdued and only able to say \"bad man, bad man\".John said that the phrase still haunts him years later.\"I was so angry,\" he said. \"I was angered that I put my mum in that home to be safe.\"I just cannot make sense of it. How somebody could even want to do things like that, especially to a vulnerable person.\"John described the situation as \"totally horrific\" but said it could have been worse if a carer had not walked into the bedroom and intervened. \"I wouldn't wish that on my worst enemy because that is horrible, knowing that your mother is vulnerable, can't defend herself or do anything,\" he said. \"And to do what that monster has done to her.\"McAuley remained at the home under staff supervision for two days following the attack, undergoing assessments on his mental capacity. During this time, Mary was confined to her room until McAuley could be removed by police.After being found to have full capacity, he was charged with four counts of sexual assault. Two were in relation to Mary and he was also alleged to have sexually assaulted two carers.The case took several years to call in court and was due to be heard at Greenock Sheriff Court earlier this year but by that point 86-year-old McAuley was deemed unfit to stand trial. He has since died.\"There was no justice,\" said John.\"Nobody's got justice. That man did what he did. In those three years nothing happened to him. Where's the justice? There's none.\"A spokesman for the Crown Office and Procurator Fiscal Service (COPFS) said the indictment was served within the relevant statutory timescale but he \"understood\" John's concern. He added that assessments on McAuley's fitness to stand trial affected the progress of the case.Seeking accountability for his mother's ordeal, John took civil action against HC-One.In March 2025 the care home provider admitted primary liability for its failure to safeguard Mary and paid damages to the family.Michael McKitrick, of PBW Law, who represented John in the case, said his client had expected his mother to be taken care of and not subjected to the risk of sexual assault.\"The second assault was certainly avoidable,\" he said.John says he has never received a formal apology from the home and, three years on, still \"just wants answers\".Merino Court Care Home has been rated good or very good in its most recent inspections by the Care Inspectorate.The BBC asked an HC-One representative to take part in an interview, but the company declined.HC-One was asked if it had made any changes to its protocols in the wake of the attack on Mary and why it had not apologised to the family.A spokesperson for the care home said: \"Following this incident [serious sexual assault] we took immediate and appropriate steps to manage the situation. We alerted all relevant authorities including social services, the Care Inspectorate and the Police.\"We also immediately informed the family of the victim and stayed in contact with them throughout the incident and the subsequent investigation. \"We apologised at the time for the distress caused to the victim and do so again now.\"The Care Inspectorate said it was satisfied that the care provider notified it appropriately. The Inverclyde Health and Social Care Partnership (Inverclyde HSCP), which oversees care for the local council and NHS board, told John three years ago that an inquiry would take place but he says has heard nothing since.The BBC asked Inverclyde HSCP what the outcome of its inquiry was.A spokesperson said: \"Any concerns regarding an individual's welfare are taken seriously and are dealt with through the appropriate safeguarding and professional processes.\"We recognise the importance of families being heard and remain willing to meet with, or speak directly to, family members to understand and discuss any concerns they wish to raise.\"Charity Hourglass runs a 24-hour helpline for those suffering or dealing with the abuse of elderly people.It said calls to its phone line over concerns about abuse in care homes had risen by 125% in Scotland in the past year.Chief Executive Richard Robinson said sexual abuse in care homes was a \"hidden issue\". \"It's more common than anyone would expect, sadly, and I believe we are just seeing the tip of the iceberg in terms of the amount of cases that we're dealing with in Scotland,\" he said.\"There were a number of missed opportunities here. It's not just the victim survivor here. It's not just the son. It's also the staff themselves that have been let down.\"John says his mother \"was never the same person\" after the attack. Her dementia has advanced rapidly in the years since and John says: \"I don't think it helped\". When he visits her now, his focus is on trying to make her smile.The feeling of guilt has taken a toll on him too, John said.\"I wouldn't wish this on anyone. I don't think there's a time gone by I don't think about it.\"I had to go and seek counselling and things like that because it was affecting me in many ways. It's always on your mind. I'll live with this for the rest of my life.\"He hopes speaking out may help others.\"It was never about the money,\" he said.\"I wanted justice. This happened to my family, and I wanted to stop this happening to anyone else's family. \"It's the only way we can bring it out into the light to maybe put safeguards in to stop this happening again.\"If you or anyone you know has been affected by the issues in this article, support and advice can be found at BBC Action Line.",
