    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-girls-as-young-as-five-and-women-as-old-as-87-targeted-for-u",
      "title": "Girls as young as five and women as old as 87 targeted for upskirting",
      "summary": "Police forces in Wales received 219 complaints over the past three years, with 18 people charged, an FOI request reveals.",
      "body": "Girls as young as five have been targeted for upskirting, new police figures show. Over the past three years, Wales' four forces received 219 complaints from people up to the age of 87, with 18 people charged, results of Freedom of Information (FOI) requests, external reveal. Upskirting involves taking a photo or video, external beneath someone's clothing - without their knowledge - with the intention of viewing their genitals, buttocks or underwear. It was made a criminal offence under the Voyeurism (Offences) Act 2019, external and carries a maximum sentence of two years in prison.The Crown Prosecution Service, external urged people to report incidents immediately, saying those that led to charges almost doubled between 2022 and 2026.\"As well as being an extremely damaging offence in its own right, our prosecutors often see upskirting forming part of a wider pattern of sexual offending,\" said national lead for sexual offence prosecutions Siobhan Blake.Between January 2023 and June 2026, Wales' four police forces received 219 complaints, with the highest being 159 in the Dyfed-Powys area and the lowest, six in Gwent, according to the FOI figures.Where ages were recorded, 11 involved under-10s, 67 came from girls aged 11 to 17, 35 from those 18 to 24, and 12 from over-55s.From these 219 complaints, nine people were charged in the north Wales force area, six in Dyfed-Powys, three in south Wales, while Gwent had no data.A previous FOI to Greater Manchester Police, external showed it received 146 reports between 2019 and 2025, leading to 41 arrests and 10 people being charged.West Midlands Police received 75 reports between 2022 and 2026, with 23 charged, according to another FOI, external. \"I think a lot of the time [boys] think it's a joke or banter, but it's not funny,\" said student Rowan Stanger, 21. \"Whilst it is illegal, I don't think a lot of people take it seriously.\"She was in secondary school when it was made a criminal offence in Wales and England in 2019, and as a teenager, heard about lots of cases of it happening to friends. \"I was wearing shorts under skirts in school, I think that's what was kind of expected even before I really knew why,\" she added.\"That is still ingrained in me. I'll rarely wear one without.\"Rowan is part of a student-led Time to Act, which fights against sexual violence in higher education.Emily Hasling, 23, is also part of the Cardiff-based campaign group, and said she remembers when she was in university and going to nightclubs, she would only wear jeans. \"Young boys don't see it as sexual assault or sexual violence, because it's not a physical kind of touching element, it's a photo,\" she said.\"So that's how they see it 'just a photo'.\" Another group member Freya Price, 22, described feeling wary on public transport. \"Obviously trains are quite crowded so if I can't stand in a corner I'll feel quite anxious, because I can't guarantee that someone's not going to violate my privacy,\" she said. Olivia (not her real name), a teenager from south Wales, said she loved wearing skirts but from an early age had been made to feel uncomfortable in them.She described how boys in primary school had an obsession with \"flipping up girls' skirts\", which led to them either wearing shorts underneath or switching to trousers. This was her first experience of feeling unsafe in them, while she was then upskirted on a night out while at university.\"There was a little stage area and I'm dancing, having a great time,\" Olivia said. \"I look over to my right and someone has got a camera with the flash on underneath my skirt.\"We made eye contact and he walked off. I was frozen and I didn't know if I was imagining it. I didn't know if I could tell someone or who to tell.\"She has not felt comfortable enough to wear the same dress again until recently, describing her head as being \"on a swivel\" every time she was in a club, and feeling like she could not get too drunk. In 2019, Libby Squire, 21, was raped and murdered by Pawel Relowicz, who was found to have previous convictions for voyeurism and public sex acts.Libby's mother Lisa Squire has spent the past five years trying to increase the reporting of non-contact sexual offences, launching the It Does Matter campaign in 2024.\"I know first hand how harmful behaviours that are too often dismissed as 'minor' can build into something catastrophic, and I am committed to ensuring no other family experiences what we have,\" she said. The NSPCC said its Childline service, external has heard from young people who have had indecent images of them taken and shared - comments that are based on real calls but are not direct quotes. \"They tell us how devastating the abuse can be on their mental and emotional wellbeing, leaving them afraid, ashamed and unsure where to turn,\" a spokesperson said. One 16-year-old described how the same boy tripped her up on the way to her seat every day in her tutor group, laughing and looking up her skirt.\"It just freaks me out and I don't know what to do about it,\" she said.A mum called to say her daughter, also 16, had heard a rumour a boy in her year had taken a photograph up her skirt, and shared it with his friends.She added: \"The thought alone is incredibly upsetting for her.\"The Children's Commissioner for England Dame Rachel de Souza said: \"The fact a number of teachers have admitted or been found guilty of upskirting offences, some in their own classrooms, is particularly shocking. \"Teachers and other school professionals have a clear responsibility to keep children safe, so staff must be alert to the signs of sexual harassment and abuse and ensure all children know where they can turn for help and support if an incident occurs.\"If you have been affected by any of the issues raised in this story, information and support can be found at the BBC Action Line",
