      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-does-our-ethnicity-change-how-we-experience-dying",
      "title": "Does our ethnicity change how we experience dying?",
      "summary": "Death is the one certainty we all share - but could our ethnicity change how we go through it?",
      "body": "Pravin Navekar's wife Gita started experiencing chronic pain in her back and legs in 2010, but despite multiple GP visits over a two-year period, was repeatedly sent home with pain relief and no further investigations.In the end the couple, from Leicester, paid to see a consultant privately, at which point Gita was referred for a CT scan. This picked up a rare cancer, a synovial sarcoma, in her pelvis.Though she was given a prognosis of under five years when she was first diagnosed, Gita lived for another 11, before passing away in April 2021.Pravin felt culturally appropriate care was \"lacking\" at times when Gita was in hospital.\"We're all individuals and we have individual needs and wants, and they're tied in with your faith, your culture, your beliefs,\" he said.\"If she was in hospital for Diwali, then it was another normal day. But when you have Christmas they do all sorts of things and make people really comfortable… [for Diwali] they don't even acknowledge it. There's nothing wrong with a nurse or a doctor coming up to you and saying, 'Good morning, and happy Diwali to you'.\"It's about recognising a person as a whole person, rather than somebody in a bed who needs care.\"Six years later, Marwa set up a support group for Muslim, Arabic-speaking women called Heya - meaning \"she\" in Arabic. It's grown from 50 women at the start to more than 700. If there's a death in the community, they all rally around to support each other.\"People send condolences and speak to each other, read the Quran together, we can send out timings if they'd like people to visit,\" she said.Marwa has since lost three other close family members, including her mother - but she says having Heya made the experience completely different.\"When the other deaths happened, I had hot food, people coming over, people calling… It's a beautiful thing,\" she said.But she worries that other communities won't have the same support, and that funding is scarce for the groups that do exist.\"I think there should be more research, and a clear support and referral path with information about where to go and who to speak to,\" she said.",
