      "id": "bbc-2026-11-20-tens-of-thousands-of-uk-fridges-secured-from-hackers-in-secr",
      "title": "Tens of thousands of UK fridges secured from hackers in secret nationwide operation",
      "summary": "Supermarkets, warehouses, restaurants and stadiums have been scrambling to make their devices safe.",
      "body": "Image source, AFP via Getty ImagesImage caption, Sweden was among countries to report a cyber incident in which some supermarket fridge units had been hiked up in temperatureByJoe TidyCyber correspondent, BBC World ServiceA behind the scenes cyber-security operation has taken place to secure tens of thousands of fridges and freezers at major UK supermarkets, warehouses, restaurants, petrol stations and stadiums from hackers, the BBC has learned.Recent waves of cyber-attacks in Europe have seen tonnes of food spoiled at hundreds of stores, causing millions of pounds of damage.France has blamed Iran for one of the waves of attacks that impacted its supermarkets, a claim Tehran denies.It was thought the UK had largely escaped such hacks as the British refrigeration industry relies on different devices but there was in fact a potential vulnerability the sector has been rushing to fix.In late October, security researchers discovered a major flaw that, if found by hackers, could have led to even worse damage to the UK.Since then, researchers, IT teams and the National Cyber Security Centre have been working around the clock to warn dozens of companies.The BBC has not reported on the large-scale, secretive operation until now to reduce the chances of hackers causing harm if they found the vulnerability.Tech Life: The everyday tech at risk of hacksAs Europe sees a wave of cyber-attacks on commercial fridges, we investigate how everyday devices are vulnerable to basic attacks using easily-available tools.Thousands of fridges taken offlineThe security threat was discovered by staff at refrigeration consultants Omega Solutions - it was then verified and expanded upon by experts at Pen Test Partners who helped raise the alarm.The National Cyber Security Centre (NCSC), the Department for Environment, Food & Rural Affairs (Defra) and Food Standards Agency (FSA) were then involved in the campaign to plug the security holes.They held industry conference calls and tasked teams to contact firms at risk.Thousands of devices controlling tens of thousands of refrigeration devices were taken off the open internet either remotely or, in some cases, physically - with staff ordered to remove internet cables and monitor temperatures manually.BBC news has learnt most major supermarkets have been forced to divert IT teams to address the risks, with a worker at one large chain saying they took hundreds of devices offline every day over the course of a week.\"It did feel like a race against time as we were expecting another wave of these fridge hacks to come,\" said the person, who did not want to be publicly named.\"From a business perspective it could have been potentially devastating with business interruption and supply chain problems. Our warehouses have millions of pounds worth of food that could have been spoiled if any hackers found out about this method of attack,\" they added.There have been three waves of hacks on refrigeration temperature controllers so far this year:In August, fridges at a small number of US military bases were impactedOn 11 and 12 October hundreds of supermarkets and food processing sites in France were hit when Danfoss temperature control systems were targetedOn 1 and 2 November dozens of supermarkets across seven European countries were hit, with Belgium and Luxembourg the worst affected. A small number of blood bank sites in France were also impactedThe waves of attacks prompted an expert at Omega Solutions to examine the security of the most popular refrigeration controllers in the UK, made by British firm Resource Data Management (RDM).RDM disputes the critical nature of the vulnerabilities exposed but has been praised for its professional response to address the concerns, the BBC understands.As well as supermarkets, multiple other areas of UK food and beverage industry were also warned to take action about the risks.These include convenience stores, petrol stations, restaurants and fast food chains, sports arenas, hotels, food processing plants and cold storage sites.Image caption, Each RDM controller can be used to remotely control the temperature of dozens of fridges and freezers\"These popular controllers had a similar weak password problem to the Danfoss and other controllers that were attacked so successfully in previous hacks so we had to get the message out as soon as possible and keep it secret,\" said Ken Munro of Pen Test Partners.\"There is so much extra attention on these ubiquitous bits of kit now that it was likely a matter of days until criminals or nation state hackers figured it out,\" he added.RDM, though, says comparisons with the units attacked in mainland Europe are not fair because hacking their fridges and freezers would have involved getting hold of a piece of hardware.The ethical hackers say this would have been achievable as the hardware in question can be purchased cheaply online on sites like eBay. \"This was a vulnerability identified in a test environment,\" said Ged Evans, CEO of RDM. He said the test \"went beyond\" what such exercises usually involved, as it \"included dismantling our hardware\".\"In any event we have acted at pace to address this issue and have already deployed first patch releases and have been in proactive contact with our customers to flag any issues and recommend mitigatory steps,\" he added.RDM confirmed that there was no sign of any malicious activity on the controllers and that it continued to work with the NCSC and other authorities to monitor the situation. Get in touchWhat are your thoughts on this topic? Let us know",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-20T00:02:07.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/d3db/live/57d6aa20-d769-11f1-a956-ff083523ef77.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6eq49zyx7p4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-arsenal-have-ingredients-but-europe-s-elite-on-another-level",
      "title": "Arsenal 'have ingredients' but Europe's elite on another level",
      "summary": "In a difficult defeat by French giants Lyon, Arsenal were shown the gap they need to bridge to challenge for the Champions League.",
      "body": "Arsenal came into Thursday's defeat by Lyon off the back of a fifth successive Women's Super League draw at Emirates Stadium against London City Lionesses.In contrast, Lyon have been flying domestically, winning all nine of their matches, scoring 49 goals, and then not conceding any in their unbeaten Champions League run. Such form sets the pace and it was clear at Meadow Park that they were superior to Arsenal in every department.Vibrant Germany international Jule Brand gave them the lead midway through the first half and Melchie Dumornay sealed the win with a powerful strike in the 57th minute. Arsenal defender Leah Williamson said the \"better team won the game\" and manager Renee Slegers highlighted the difference in physicality between the teams.\"Compared to last year when we played Lyon [in the semi-finals], they are even better now. They have standout individual quality,\" Slegers said.\"They dominated in the first half. We had solutions for the second half and had more presence. We wore them down better but it still didn't lead to open chances.\"Lyon had a 3.33 expected goals ratio on Thursday, serving up 23 shots to Arsenal's seven. They created six big chances and 14 of their shots were inside the box.Dumornay oozed quality, midfielder Lily Yohannes showed composure and Brand was very impressive both defensively and going forward for Lyon, deployed in a new position.Those individuals have benefitted from the expertise of former Barcelona manager Jonatan Giraldez, who joined in June 2025 - the month after Arsenal won the Champions League - and has now had the time he needed to impose his style.As a result, Lyon have gone up another level and the gap between these two teams has widened by Arsenal's drop-off this season.Slegers also said Arsenal had \"prepared all we could\" for the intensity that Lyon brought in London - but it still did not have an impact.Asked how far off Lyon they currently are, Slegers said: \"It's 2-0 today and they are better than us but it doesn't mean if we were to play them again soon, this season, that it would be the same game again.\"We saw moments where we did well and we know from the last couple of years we can hurt Lyon.\"But for today, their pace and physicality helped them get too much dominance in the game and that is one of their strengths. Then it became hard for us.\"Williamson said Arsenal \"know what they need to do to be better\" and that they are aware of \"where the gaps are\" to Europe's elite.They are already 12 points off the pace in the Women's Super League title race and are unlikely to qualify automatically for the Champions League quarter-finals.\"The level rises year on year. Everybody cops goals - we have to try not to and we have to create,\" said Williamson following the defeat by Lyon. \"We're all focused and we're all together. That's the most comforting thing for me - but this has been a tough period.\"It has not all been bad despite disappointing results - and context is important.Over a gruelling seven days, they have faced European holders Barcelona, WSL high-flyers London City Lionesses and record Champions League winners Lyon.They showed grit and resilience out in Barcelona last week - traits that have not been on show enough for Arsenal this season, but are clearly there.Players like Williamson, striker Alessia Russo and summer signings Georgia Stanway and Ona Batlle know what it takes to compete at the highest level.Arsenal invested in the summer transfer window and brought in proven, experienced players, but that has not translated into results - yet.Giraldez said time with his players has helped improve Lyon and perhaps that is what Arsenal need - patience.\"I think they are changing now. They made a good game against Barcelona and it's very difficult to go there and get a point,\" said Giraldez.\"The game against London City was much better. It's not just about them, but also about us. Now we have improved and play better football than last year.\"I think it is a matter of time [for Arsenal]. They have a good coach and good players. In general, they have all the ingredients to have a good season.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
