      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-19-launch-hn-onlook-yc-w25-an-open-source-developer-tool-for-de",
      "title": "Launch HN: Onlook (YC W25) – An open-source developer tool for designers",
      "summary": "Hey HN, I'm Daniel, founder of Onlook (https://github.com/onlook-dev/onlook and onlook.com).\nOnlook is a desktop app that puts a canvas on top of your real codebase, letting you design directly in code with the agent you already use. Over 25,000 designers and developers have supported the project, 100+ have contributed, and it's been the #1 trending repo in the world twice. We just rebuilt the ent",
      "body": "Craft websites, prototypes, and designs with AI on a canvas built for creative work. See\nyour real app running live, shape it visually, and let your own agent write the code. An\nopen-source alternative to Figma, V0, Replit Agent, Base 44, Webflow, etc.\n🎨 Built for design. Onlook is the open-source developer tool for designers. You see your real app running live on a canvas, explore ideas side by side, and shape the interface visually instead of from a terminal. Design apps, websites, posters, graphics, and more with your local fonts and assets.\n🤖 Bring your own agent. We don't ship our own model. Connect the Claude Code or ChatGPT/Codex account you already have, and that agent does the work inside your project. Plug in more tools through MCP connectors to bring all of the context you need to your creative workflow. Work on multiple design tasks at once across repos and pages.\n📁 Works on your real repo. Onlook opens any Git repository on your machine and runs your own dev server, so what you design is your actual product, not a mockup that someone has to rebuild later.\n🖥️ A desktop app, local-first. Onlook runs on your computer. Your code stays on your computer, and the source build needs no Onlook account, cloud keys, or .env file.\n📤 Share what you make. Export artifacts as self-contained HTML, PNG, JPEG, or PDF to hand off, review, or post.\n🧡 Open source. Apache 2.0. Read it, fork it, and customize it yourself (we'd love to hear how you customize it!)\nWhat you can do with Onlook\nDesign on your real codebase\nLive app on an infinite canvas\nMobile, tablet, and desktop frames side by side\nSafe worktree copy, so your folder is never touched\nDesign with your own agent\nBring your own Claude Code or ChatGPT/Codex plan\nAsk about any element on the canvas\nAgent arranges the canvas for you (variations, states, flows)\nReference images on the canvas\nArtifacts for quick HTML sketches outside your repo\nExport to HTML, PDF, PNG, and JPEG\nStart entirely new codebases from scratch\nOne-press pull requests, from checks to merge\n🖥️ Download the Desktop App (Recommended) – The fastest way to get started with Onlook.\nmacOS (Apple Silicon · Intel x64) → Download\nWindows – Coming soon. Join the Windows waitlist here and be emailed when we have updates.\nLinux – No Linux artifact is published and we're still gauging demand for Linux. Join the Linux waitlist here and be emailed when we have updates.\n🧑‍💻 Build from source – SOURCE_BUILD.md covers building and running Onlook on a Mac. No Onlook account or cloud keys needed.\nOpen a project. Point Onlook at any Git repository on your computer, or import one. Your code stays where it is.\nOnlook runs your app. It starts your own dev server locally, the same way you would from a terminal.\nSee it live on a canvas. Your running app appears in frames on an open canvas, so you can look at pages, sizes, and ideas side by side.\nDesign with your agent. Describe what you want in chat. Onlook hands the request to the Claude Code or ChatGPT/Codex account you connected, and that agent edits the real files in your repo.\nWatch it update. Changes show up on the canvas as your dev server reloads, and Git keeps track of everything that changed.\nShare the result. Export artifacts as self-contained HTML, PNG, JPEG, or PDF.\nBecause Onlook runs your project with its own tooling, it works with all web frameworks or languages your dev server already supports.\nStart a new empty project by clicking the \"New Draft\" button in the upper left corner of the projects page.\nUse the input box in the lower right corner of the screen to write what you'd like to be designed in Onlook.\nAnswer clarifying questions from the agent, and wait for it to build\nClick to select individual frames for context in follow-up prompts, or double-click on a specific element to use the inline chat and guide the AI to edit that specific element.\nPrompt more ideas on one or multiple designs until you're ready to share your work with a colleague.\nSelect the frame you'd like to share, and ask the agent to convert it into an Artifact for exporting.\nExport the artifact as HTML/CSS or as a PNG using the export button in the upper right corner of the frame.\nPaste the exported artifact in Slack, email, or any messaging tool.\nElectron · React 19 · Astryx (Design system)· React Flow · Zustand · CodeMirror\nGit for working directly on your repo · node-pty for running your dev server and shells\nBun · Turborepo · usage analytics (see Usage data)\nHTML · PNG & JPEG (Electron capturePage) · PDF (Electron printToPDF) & pdf-lib\nIf you have a suggestion that would make this better, please fork the repo and\ncreate a pull request. You can also browse open issues.\nSee the CONTRIBUTING.md for instructions and code of conduct.\nTo report a security issue, follow SECURITY.md instead of opening a public issue.\nContact us: Discord - Email\nUpdates: Twitter - LinkedIn - YouTube - Substack\nLooking for the previous version of Onlook?\nOnlook v1 (web): the browser-based version lives on the\nv1 branch of this repository.\nOnlook Desktop (Electron, original): the first desktop version is at\nOnlook's source code is distributed under the Apache 2.0 License. See\nThe license covers source code, not every asset in this repository. Onlook's\nicons come from Central Icons by Iconists. They\nare licensed to Onlook for its own product, and that license does not extend to\nforks. Onlook's brand marks, DMG art, and sounds aren't covered either. See\nASSETS.md for what's covered and what to replace if you fork, and",
