      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-surgeon-calls-for-corporate-homicide-probe-over-claims-patie",
      "title": "Surgeon calls for corporate homicide probe over claims patients unnecessarily bled to death",
      "summary": "Dr Mohammed Habib said he had been raising concerns about NHS Grampian for a decade, including with government ministers and police.",
      "body": "Habib, who has been a consultant with NHS Grampian since 2014, held a media conference on Thursday with his representative Aamer Anwar.The lawyer and human rights campaigner said Habib had taken the step of speaking out because he was taught when he started as a doctor 36 years ago that taking care of patients must be his first concern.\"Catastrophically, the operating theatre he worked in at times could be described as a slaughterhouse, which killed through incompetence and negligence whilst not giving a damn for accountability,\" Anwar said.Dr Habib said he was putting his career on the line to demand accountability from the health board over its failings.The consultant said he formally reported serious concerns about dozens of avoidable deaths in 2021.In 2024, he reported 20 cases from the previous decade to the police but no charges were brought.He has also leaked two reports by external experts which examined surgical care in NHS Grampian.A 2022 review looked at 45 patients in the care of liver and pancreatic services at Aberdeen Royal Infirmary (ARI) between 2015 and 2021. It said 31 had died, and 19 of them were considered to have died of avoidable consequences of surgery or intervention.Dr Habib, who has worked in General Surgery & HPB (liver, pancreas and bile duct) since 2014, said he thought several hundred patients had come to harm from an excessively high rate of complications.He said he had tried his best to use the whistleblowing procedures but the institutions he had contacted had failed to act as robustly as they should have.He said he first became aware of avoidable surgery-related deaths in 2016.  In July 2021, he formally raised serious concerns about what he believes were dozens of avoidable deaths.In February 2024, he spoke to Police Scotland. Habib said he believed NHS Grampian was guilty of \"wicked recklessness and an indifference to patient deaths\" and called for a corporate homicide investigation.The consultant said the 2022 external expert report, which was never made public, concluded that 19 deaths out of a case study of 45 were avoidable (42%).He said he had contacted the first minister and health secretary on numerous occasions since June 2023 but the Scottish government had said it had \"no jurisdiction\" to investigate whistleblowing claims.Habib also said that the Independent Whistleblowing Officer (INWO) refused to progress his complaints.He also wrote to the chief executive of NHS Grampian, Laura Skaife Knight, in December 2025.She replied: \"I would be grateful if you can let HIS (Healthcare Improvement Scotland) complete its enquiries rather than continuing to correspond with me about these matters, as I have nothing further to add to this response at this stage.\"Habib said: \"They are avoiding accountability by not being transparent about what damage has been done, while promising heaven and earth going forward.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T13:31:03.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/a0bf/live/715d23f0-e41d-11f1-9e08-55a8a3b48445.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crjeqxexyq05o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
