    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-19-grand-theft-auto-vi-understands-just-how-important-a-smartph",
      "title": "Grand Theft Auto VI understands just how important a smartphone is",
      "summary": "Grand Theft Auto VI, in many ways, tries to simulate real life. And life in 2026 would not be complete without a smartphone. GTA VI understands this and makes the phone not just a central part of the experience, but an object that exists within the world. You may try to escape into a fantasy of robbing banks and fighting drug lords, but you can't get away from texts from your mom or nagging fitnes",
      "body": "Grand Theft Auto VI, in many ways, tries to simulate real life. And life in 2026 would not be complete without a smartphone. GTA VI understands this and makes the phone not just a central part of the experience, but an object that exists within the world. You may try to escape into a fantasy of robbing banks and fighting drug lords, but you can’t get away from texts from your mom or nagging fitness app notifications.\nSeeing a smartphone in a game isn’t a new phenomenon. The device has become a natural stand-in for the main menu in many games, giving players an interface that feels intuitive, because a phone is something they use every day. But the phone in GTA VI is different for a few reasons. One is that it behaves much like any other object in the game, whether it’s a gun or a bottle of beer. The action doesn’t pause when you use your phone. Instead, the game switches to a first-person viewpoint, where you can still walk around — or drive, if you’re feeling ambitious — while reading texts or flipping through a fictional version of Instagram.\nRob Nelson, the co-studio head at developer Rockstar North and producer on GTA VI, says that it would’ve felt “archaic” to have a static phone that worked like those in other games, or the one in GTA V, which appeared as a pop-up at the bottom of the screen across from the minimap. “We felt that it was important to make it physical so you have to look at it and put your attention on it, including while driving,” he explains. “It’s about finding the line between realism and player convenience. But the act of being lost in your phone felt like something worth doing, even though it could mean slightly more inconvenience. In this game you are committing to using it.” (Of course, texting and driving, like much of what you do in GTA VI, is both very dangerous and very illegal.)\nYou have to interact with the phone in the game constantly. Often the story plays out through phone calls and text messages, and you have a surprisingly large array of apps to use that serve a real purpose in the game. You manage your money in a banking app, use a rideshare app when you need a lift, track your workouts in a fitness app, scroll through a social network complete with comments and the ability to like posts, and sideload an AI tool that scans nearby cars so you can see how easy they’ll be to steal. You can even change the wallpaper and ringtone. (GTA VI has two main characters that you swap between, and each has their own phone, though they’re functionally identical.)\nWhat’s most impressive is how all of these apps interact with each other. For instance, after pulling off a major heist, a friend might text you a news story about it, asking “was this you!?” You can then click the link, open up the news app, and read an actual article about what went down. These kinds of interactions can be functional, like when another character texts you a location, which you can then add to your map so the GPS can guide you there. Other times, they add texture to the world or depth to the characters. GTA VI is a love story about two criminals named Jason and Lucia, and you play as both. At one point, while controlling Lucia, I got a text asking for a selfie. I opened up the camera, shot a pic of Lucia drinking a latte, and sent it over. Jason replied immediately — and their relationship got a boost.\nPhones are so fully integrated into the game that they can even interrupt the characters’ sleep. At the end of each day, you can click on a bed to rest for the night. But neither Jason nor Lucia will instantly fall asleep when you do — instead, they’ll sit down and pull out their phone, and you have the option to doomscroll or catch up on texts instead of going to sleep right away. Nelson says that this was implemented because the developers wanted to include the ability for the characters to sit on the bed at night, much like a real couple in a shared living space might do. “We needed something to do aside from just sitting there staring at nothing,” he says.\nIf nothing else, GTA VI’s phone shows how alike we all are: Even bank robbers being hunted by the police can’t seem to stop scrolling. “Phones are a constant part of our lives,” Nelson says of the evolution, “even more than they were in 2013.”",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Analysis",
        "Entertainment",
        "Gaming",
        "Report"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew Webster",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T13:30:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/1a1ef06931b17-screenshotUrl.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,6.9226294357184,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/1037106/grand-theft-auto-6-smartphone",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-premier-league-clubs-to-cover-legal-costs-if-man-city-sponso",
      "title": "Premier League clubs to cover legal costs if Man City sponsors sue",
      "summary": "Premier League clubs vote to provide legal cover to the organisation's five board directors in the event Manchester City's sponsors sue them.",
      "body": "Premier League clubs have voted to cover legal expenses for the organisation's five board directors if any of Manchester City's sponsors sue them.The clubs met in London on Thursday for the first time since City were found guilty of a catalogue of charges relating to the breaching of financial rules by an independent commission.City have appealed, with a sanction yet to be decided.The club's sponsors Etihad and Etisalat have both said they are considering legal action against the Premier League.At present, a director is only financially covered should they win a legal case against them but the league's rules will now be amended to provide legal indemnity for CEO Richard Masters, chair Alison Brittan and three other non-executive directors.Sources have told BBC Sport that City and Nottingham Forest were the only two clubs that voted against the amendment, with the other 18 clubs voting in favour.An independent commission appointed by the Premier League found City guilty of artificially inflating the club's revenues by over £830m over a nine-year period in September. The commission determined that the club arranged \"sham\" contracts with sponsors, which disguised funding from club owners the Abu Dhabi United Group. Premier League CEO Masters said City \"systematically broke Premier League rules for nearly a decade\". In October, City sponsor Etihad Airways said it was considering legal action against the Premier League.In a statement, the United Arab Emirates-based airline said it \"categorically rejects any finding, conclusion or implication that suggests the airline has ever been involved in improper commercial arrangements\".Etisalat signalled its intention to follow suit earlier this month and described the Premier League's verdict as \"entirely false and defamatory\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T13:29:42.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/4515/live/c1425af0-e41a-11f1-b37c-b3a71d7200af.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cqy4jvdw7jdlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-19-making-8-page-foldable-zines-from-a-single-a4-sheet-in-the-b",
      "title": "Making 8-page foldable zines from a single A4 sheet in the browser",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://krthr.co/paper-folds-and-whats-new-in-fanzines-app/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50559719\nPoints: 231\n# Comments: 51",
      "body": "A few months ago, I wrote about how I started giving away zines to my friends, my family, and people at work.\nIf you've never made one, the fold is simple. Take a single A4 sheet of paper, fold it in half three times, open it back up, and cut along the center crease with scissors. Fold the sheet into a cross and close it, and you have an 8-page booklet with no staples or glue.\nI fill mine with whatever I feel like printing: photos, poems, or notes.\nWhy fanzines.app existsThe part I never liked was laying out the flat sheet on the computer before printing. Because the A4 paper folds back on itself, the pages sit in a strange order and the top row has to be upside down:\n│    6    │    5    │    4    │    3    │   ← upside down (180°)\n│  Back   │  Cover  │    1    │    2    │   ← right-side up\nI got tired of rotating pictures and double-checking columns in Photoshop every time I wanted to print a new zine, so I built fanzines.app.\nIn the browser, you lay out each page right-side up with photos and text. When you export, the app places all 8 panels onto the A4 grid with the top row rotated 180 degrees and generates a 300 DPI PDF. Everything stays local in the browser; no photos are uploaded to a server.\nOver the last few days, I rebuilt the editor around the things that kept getting in my way when I sat down to make a zine.\nFixing how pages are pairedIn the first version, the sidebar listed the 8 pages in a plain two-column grid. It paired the Front Cover with Page 1, Page 2 with Page 3, Page 4 with Page 5, and Page 6 with the Back Cover.\nIt looked tidy on screen, but it didn't match the physical booklet. When you hold a folded zine, the front cover sits alone on the right. Open it and Page 1 faces Page 2 across the fold, then 3 faces 4, 5 faces 6, and the back cover ends up alone on the left.\nThe sidebar now groups the page thumbnails into those real spreads, in reading order.\nI also added a Spread view on the canvas. While you're editing Page 1, Page 2 sits beside it at lower opacity so you can check how the two facing pages sit together before printing. Clicking the dimmed page switches straight to editing it.\nSmaller changes that make the editor less fussyWhen I make a photo zine, I usually start with a folder of 8 photos already picked out. Now you can drag the whole batch onto the editor window, and the sidebar highlights which pages will take each photo in reading order before you drop them.\nI changed the side panel too. Early on, clicking a photo opened four coordinate inputs (X, Y, Width, Height). I rarely needed to type coordinates; mostly I wanted a photo to fill the page, fit inside the margins, or sit centered. Fill, Fit, and Center now sit at the top of the inspector and in a small toolbar above the selection.\nThe exact fields are still in a collapsible section at the bottom, now in millimeters (for the 74.25 × 105 mm folded panel) and points for text.\nBefore downloading the PDF, a Review step shows the full unfolded A4 sheet with toggles for cut/fold lines and a 5 mm safe margin. Most home printers clip a few millimeters around the edge of an A4 sheet, so the safe margin scales the layout to about 95% to keep the covers intact.\nA couple of other additions: the editor now works on phone screens (one page at a time with a thumbnail strip along the bottom), and the site is available in English as well as Spanish. Changing the language keeps your current pages.\nYou can create your own zine in English or Spanish at fanzines.app",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "krthr",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T13:18:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://fanzines.app/images/editor-shot.webp",
      "originalUrl": "https://krthr.co/paper-folds-and-whats-new-in-fanzines-app/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-google-rejects-uk-request-for-russell-brand-youtube-video-to",
      "title": "Google rejects UK request for Russell Brand YouTube video to be removed, sources say",
      "summary": "Move comes after Brand's wife shared a message from him in which he claimed his innocence.",
      "body": "Google has refused a government request to remove a YouTube video posted by jailed rapist Russell Brand's wife in which he claims his innocence, Whitehall sources have told the BBC.The disgraced comedian is in prison after being sentenced to 10-and-a-half years following his conviction for rape and three counts of sexual assault.Andy Burnham's official spokesperson said the prime minister believed \"the public have heard enough from Russell Brand\", and his victims \"now deserve peace from him\".Google told BBC News in a statement it was \"aware of the new content and our teams are investigating\".It added: \"We suspended Russell Brand's channel from the YouTube Partner Program in September 2023. The channel has not been able to monetise on YouTube since then.\"On Sunday, Brand's wife Laura shared a video on their YouTube channel and read out a message from Brand in which he claimed: \"I am not guilty of the offences I have been jailed for.\"The Prison Service of England and Wales asked for the video to be taken down, on the basis it was offensive to Brand's victims and had reached a substantial audience.A Ministry of Justice (MoJ) spokesperson said \"it was only right\" to ask for content that was \"distressing for victims\" to be removed, adding that while \"removal ultimately depends on the platform's own terms\", the department strongly believes \"YouTube should remove this content\".Asked if the YouTube channel should be taken down, the prime minister's spokesperson said: \"We hope and trust they will do the right thing.\"The BBC was initially told a minister had met Google to request the deactivation of Brand's account. The MoJ later clarified the request had focused on the controversial video, and it is now understood a minister did not meet Google and the request was made via an established mechanism the prison service has with tech companies to request deletion of material. The BBC has been told that Google declined the request, saying that its rules had not been broken, but housing minister Matthew Pennycook - who described the video as \"abhorrent\" - told BBC Breakfast that the government remained in \"close dialogue\" with Google.Jess Phillips, the former minister for safeguarding and violence against women and girls, told BBC Radio 4's Today programme the government \"doesn't have the power to enforce that this video is taken down\".\"It relies on YouTube, in this instance... to do the right thing, which is a much, much broader argument about whether Ofcom codes that are in the Online Safety Act are there actually to protect women,\" she said.An Ofcom spokesperson said in a statement that the current guidance was ultimately voluntary, and \"any changes to the legislation to make it mandatory would be a matter for government and parliament\".Phillips, describing Brand as an \"absolute narcissist\", suggested it was \"very likely\" that the government would look at introducing a second online safety act to address gaps in the current regulatory framework.\"Before, we haven't had prisoners who had massive YouTube followings,\" she said. \"This is an entirely new era.\"However, the BBC understands there are no new plans to update or bring fresh legislation governing online platforms as a result of Brand's case.Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch said she would not immediately demand Google remove the video, if she was in power - although agreed it was concerning \"if people in prison are able to use other conduits to send messages\".Badenoch told Today, YouTube was a private platform, and demanding content be taken down \"without looking at exactly whether a law has been broken is not where I would start from\".\"If we want to make legislation for that, then we can look at that. But I'm quite worried about government intervening,\" she added.Brand's message has prompted an investigation into whether he had broken prison rules.Downing Street previously called the message \"clearly appalling\", adding: \"Prisoners are strictly banned from making statements that cause distress to victims, and speaking to the media is only permitted in exceptional circumstances.\"Justice Secretary Alex Norris said the case was being \"urgently investigated, and I expect action to be taken if he is found to have broken the rules\".Prisoners' phone conversations are routinely recorded, but not necessarily listened to by prison authorities. If Brand is found to have breached the rules, possible repercussions could include a temporary phone ban or live monitoring of his calls.In 2022 notorious inmate Charles Bronson reportedly had restrictions placed on his phone use after he sent Sky News an unauthorised audio message about his bid for parole.In recent years, Brand has positioned himself as a Christian influencer and has a large following.Mia Latter, a woman who has made allegations against Brand, said she found the message shared by Laura Brand on the couple's Sunday Service show \"upsetting\" and was appalled to hear \"this Christian message alongside absolute lies\".Latter, 37, has alleged she was raped by the comedian when she was 16. She was not a complainant in his recent criminal trial and he denies her claims.\"I just felt for those women that stood trial and everything they went through,\" she told the Today programme this week.She is one of the women who made allegations against Brand in 2023 as part of a Times, Sunday Times and Channel 4's Dispatches investigation.The video and the Brands' YouTube account are still available on the site, and also still active is Brand's X account, where a message last appeared on 11 November.\"There was never a Russell Brand, according to @meta,\" it read, referring to Meta's decision to disable Brand's social media accounts on Instagram, Facebook and WhatsApp.\"Thank God for @elonmusk,\" the message added, referring to X's owner, before concluding: \"Stay tuned.\"The BBC has approached X for comment; Musk himself has criticised Brand's conviction for historical sex offences.Children's minister Josh MacAlister told the Today programme the Meta decision provided a clear template for taking action.\"Meta's community standards prohibit accounts owned by or run on behalf of convicted sex offenders, and it's as simple as that,\" he said. \"Google should take this down.\"Additional reporting by Steven McIntosh and political correspondent Harry Farley",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
