      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-uk-joins-in-gta-6-frenzy-as-game-becomes-available-worldwide",
      "title": "UK joins in GTA 6 frenzy as game becomes available worldwide",
      "summary": "The game, which was in development for 13 years, is set become one of the world's most popular entertainment products ever.",
      "body": "The game, set in the fictional US state of Leonida, follows two protagonists, Lucia and Jason, through the seedy underbelly of America after an \"easy score goes wrong\".It was released at midnight in all the countries it is available in, which meant New Zealanders were the first to access it.\"The graphics are great. Even from the set-up and credits, it's feeling pretty good,\" Aleana Fernando, who lives in Auckland, New Zealand, told BBC Newsbeat soon after she started playing.Once the game was released at midnight UK time, gamers were equally enthralled by the game's graphics and attention to detail.Charlie Wood from London said he had \"already laughed at the dialogue before even stepping into the gameplay\".About 30 minutes into playing in Leonida, he said it was \"most definitely worth the wait\".Meanwhile in Germany, Timur told the BBC he was impressed by how lifelike the game was.\"It really feels like a life simulation with the amount of in-game encounters,\" he said.The Guardian's Keza MacDonald, external was impressed by the relationship between the main characters.\"GTA has never been particularly kind to women... there is still a lot of sexism in GTA VI's world, but now we have Lucia to react to it,\" she said. \"This is the first GTA in which I have felt the women are treated as people, not only Lucia but also the many other female characters in the story\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T12:26:39.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/01c9/live/8b711120-e422-11f1-99b6-a35a62fa4264.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6z74ep2n17zo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-19-florence-pugh-hits-out-at-weight-loss-and-cosmetic-surgery-s",
      "title": "Florence Pugh hits out at weight loss and cosmetic surgery speculation",
      "summary": "The actress said how stressful\" it was with people \"talking about you... in ways you can't comprehend\".",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesByHelen BushbyCulture reporterFlorence Pugh has said she was \"disturbed\" to see \"thousands of people\" discussing her recent appearance at the Governors Awards, and denied speculation about weight-loss drugs and facial surgery. The actress wrote on Instagram, external how \"stressful\" it was waking up to people \"talking about you and your body in ways that you can't even comprehend\". \"I have never been on Ozempic. I have never done any face surgery,\" she wrote. \"And don't ever say that I didn't make my own nose, I've always had that beautiful ski slope. Even as a baby!\"She added: \"For the first time in my entire career, I have had the opportunity to be rested, to sleep, to wake up and work out and to eat regularly and healthily.\"In her eight-page statement, the Oppenheimer, Dune and East of Eden star told followers: \"I'm looking after myself, and that's literally it.\"Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Pugh has spoken out before about judgement about how women look Pugh highlighted that losing weight had not been her goal, and she had lost \"two pounds\".\"The thing that's most important to me and my trainer is muscle,\" she said. \"I think it's the most important thing a woman can do. Build muscle and be strong. I can now do six pull-ups and lift 110kg.\"Which I'm sure to many of you isn't a lot... but to me?! It's everything.\"The actress said that from the moment she entered the acting industry she was \"subjected to fat shaming\". \"I have loved every time I get to tell someone they are being inappropriate about my weight, and I'm not changing that now just because you've all said so.\"She also said she was \"disturbed\" that \"women who now think that I'm someone I'm not, for having my body dissected and pointed at again\".\"I have spent my whole career defending and protecting bodies that just grow the way they grow.\"Professor Heather Widdows from Everyday Lookism, external, which campaigns to end body shaming, told BBC News: \"Pugh is right that we are in a place where 'beauty standards are changing, getting harder and harder to keep up with'. \"A global beauty ideal makes impossible demands and normalises what were once extreme,\" added Widdows, who is Professor of Philosophy at the University of Warwick. \"We know that body-shaming individuals is harmful. Given this, we should not say negative things about other people's bodies... in a world where our bodies are increasingly ourselves, when you shame bodies you shame people.\"Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Pugh is no stranger to a striking red carpet outfit Pugh, who was Oscar-nominated for Little Women in 2020 and played Black Widow assassin Yelena Belova in this year's Spider-Man: Brand New Day, has previously spoken out against judgement about women's looks.In 2024, she told the Times, external she aimed to \"challenge how women were perceived\".\"Look, not everybody has legs that go on for days. I remember watching this industry and feeling that I wasn't represented,\" she said. \"I've loved challenging ideas I don't like.\"She also spoke about why she thinks she receives criticism about her appearance. \"I've always been aware that seeing a confident woman be happy in her skin is triggering to a lot of people and for a long time just accepted that I will receive commentary like that,\" she said. \"But now this...? I'm asking us all to just be mindful. We are real people, living real lives.\"Pugh did not have comments turned on for her post, but it was liked by celebrities including Ashley James, who is known for speaking out about body positivity, plus Carol Vorderman, Zoe Ball, writer and actress Katy Brand and doctor and broadcaster Amir Khan.  'Please be gentle'Pugh also said she had sued several publications earlier this year, claiming they published AI-generated images appearing to show her receiving cosmetic injections that were presented as real photographs.\"I was... horrified,\" she wrote, claiming the articles had damaged her reputation.The actress concluded by urging followers to think about their attitudes towards physical appearance.\"I understand that we are in a warped part of image history right now, with beauty standards changing, getting harder and harder to keep up with,\" she said. \"But please be gentle to yourselves and others when you go in on people's physical appearance.\"I'm not angry at you, I'm angry at the situation that's being projected onto us.\"It feels inescapable, but part of changing the tide is on you as much as us in the public eye.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T12:17:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ed67/live/ffc65c00-e437-11f1-a094-5fdc692f3e33.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmge37e27r40o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
