      "id": "bbc-2026-11-18-israeli-military-launches-rare-ground-operation-in-gaza-beyo",
      "title": "Israeli military launches rare ground operation in Gaza beyond Yellow Line",
      "summary": "Hamas condemns the incursion, which the Israeli military says is dismantling a \"terror tunnel\", as a \"flagrant\" ceasefire violation.",
      "body": "Israeli ground forces have launched a rare operation in part of the central Gaza Strip outside their control, after issuing evacuation orders for residents.Witnesses said tanks advanced east of Nuseirat refugee camp and rolled onto the territory's main north to south road under the cover of artillery strikes.The Israeli military said the \"targeted\" operation aimed to dismantle a \"terror tunnel\" west of the so-called Yellow Line, which marks the more than 60% of Gaza's territory occupied by Israel.The Palestinian armed group Hamas, which controls the rest of Gaza, accused Israel of a \"flagrant violation\" of the October 2025 ceasefire agreement.Violence has continued in Gaza over the past 13 months, with near-daily Israeli strikes, and living conditions for the 2.1 million people there remain dire.Early on Wednesday afternoon, Israeli military body Cogat's Arabic spokesperson announced the start of the incursion and warned Palestinians living in an area north of Nuseirat and Bureij refugee camp to stay away for their own safety.It also said a section of the north to south Salah al-Din road that passed through the area would be closed to traffic.Later, the Israel Defense Forces (IDF) said it had begun a joint operation with the Shin Bet security service \"to dismantle a terror tunnel west of the Yellow Line that posed an immediate and direct threat to IDF troops\".\"IDF troops under the Southern Command are deployed in the area in accordance with the ceasefire agreement and will continue to operate to remove any immediate threat and thwart attempts by the Hamas terrorist organisation to harm Israeli civilians or IDF troops, or to violate the agreement,\" it added.Hamas warned in a statement that the advance of Israeli military vehicles beyond the Yellow Line and the evacuation of residents constituted \"a dangerous development and a flagrant violation of the ceasefire agreement\".Footage published by Palestinian media outlets and on social media showed families fleeing areas near Nuseirat camp following the announcement.In one video verified by BBC Verify, a man can be seen ducking after hearing artillery fire and then running along Salah al-Din road, about 1.5km (0.9 miles) west of the Yellow Line. \"The Israeli occupation now is firing randomly near the houses and tents of people,\" he says.Automatic gunfire can be heard in another video filmed just to the south-east.A resident of Nuseirat who was forced to flee told BBC Arabic's Middle East Daily programme: \"The situation is now extremely dire, with Israeli forces firing indiscriminately at areas where displaced people have sought shelter.\"\"We couldn't take any of our belongings with us - not even our blankets, pillows, or clothes. We had no choice but to leave everything behind.\"A second resident also reported \"indiscriminate gunfire and shelling\" and said there had been \"numerous casualties, including fatalities\".Asked about reported fatalities, the IDF sent a general statement about the operation.Gaza's Hamas-run Civil Defence agency said three people were killed and others were wounded in three separate Israeli drone strikes on Wednesday.One man was killed in Nuseirat, the second to the north in Gaza City, and the third in the central town of Deir al-Balah, it added.The IDF told the BBC that it had \"struck several military operatives\".At least 1,658 people have been killed in Israeli attacks in Gaza since the ceasefire began last year, according to the territory's Hamas-run health ministry, whose figures are considered reliable by the UN.The Israeli military says six of its soldiers have been killed over the same period.Meanwhile, more than 1.9 million people - about 90% of Gaza's population - remain displaced, with 1.4 million of them living in tents or makeshift shelters, according to the UN.Reconstruction has yet to begin in Gaza, where an estimated 82% of all structures have been destroyed or damaged during the war, as US President Donald Trump's peace plan has largely stalled.The disarmament of Hamas has been a key sticking point.The group has agreed to give up its weapons in exchange for a phased withdrawal of Israeli troops from the 60% of Gaza they currently occupy. But Israel has insisted that Hamas must fully disarm before any pullout.The war was triggered by the Hamas-led attack on southern Israel on 7 October 2023, when about 1,200 people were killed and 251 others were taken hostage.Since then, more than 74,700 people have been killed in Gaza, the health ministry says.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-18T22:34:56.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ee79/live/56061990-e382-11f1-a094-5fdc692f3e33.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cw8r7510degno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-18-llm-assisted-reverse-engineering",
      "title": "LLM assisted reverse engineering",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.slowchronicle.com/posts/llm-assisted-reverse-engineering\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50553225\nPoints: 275\n# Comments: 69",
      "body": "I have been hacking both hardware and software for 2 decades. Previously manually. But in 2026 with the help of AIs the proccess is greatly sped up. Below I will describe my proccess briefly or how I do it.\nTools which I mostly use are these:\nWhat I basically do nowadays is just point the agent at the binary to be analysed and use my set of dedicated agent skills. Rest usually takes care of itself. But putting the skills besides, the flow is basically this.\nFind binary type, platform, build etc - based on this using appropriate decompiler (note - current AIs do fairly well with just disassembly . ie pure  x86 or ARM instructions).\nCommand agent to document all findings along the way to MD files. This serves several purposes. They become notes for your own understanding. They help the agent not lose the track after compaction / handoff to different agent. And last but not least, it helps the agent work more effectively, not get lost in circles etc. More about this later.\nAsk the agent to analyse basic structure. Main functions, main branching, imported functions etc of given binary. By binary I mean whatever. Desktop executable, website or firmware image.\nOnce I have this basic overview I can usually proceed any way I need to do modifications to the original binary or build mods or just help me understand it better.\nOn some occasions some things are not easily resolvable by simple disassembly or decompilation. In those cases you can do:\nLet the agent debug given binary and set breakpoints on specific parts to help it figure out given inner workings.\nUse logging. Either by somehow enabling it in the original binary or by making a probe (and this can mean many things) that injects to the original binary and records you via logs (to file) whatever is needed to resolve given feature.\nGive agent outside access - ie computer use for desktop apps, or api controllable emulator for running and controlling given firmware.\nAsk agent to try different approach. But you must be specific. Ie when it gets stuck in disasembly by following xrefs, ask it to try to follow strings in the binary instead.\nRead the entire LLM conversation and thinking. Current LLMs are smart but they still sometimes get to a narrow approach. By reading the traces you can often instinctively figure the agent it going into a blind path and give it suggestion as to how it might be approached better. That however skill of your own to have.\nHere are some of my major skills I use frequently.\nMatt Pottock - Grill me\nIt gives you several questions when your input might be valuable. I usually use it with this prompt:\nUse skill /grill-me to ask me up to 5 questions\nwhose answers are crucial for your further progress.\nAsk only one question at a time.\nmattpocockskillsShellSkills for Real Engineers. Straight from my .agents directory.Open repo Opens in a new tab.\nGhidra / other RE tooling skill\nIt helps a ton to have a skill where your specific reverse engineering tooling is described. Ie to use radare2 or kuna. Or description of how to use the MCP Ghidra bridge and for what. Without such a skill, agents often give up too quickly getting lost, not knowing how to do the actual disassembly / decompilation.\nioma8ghidra-decompile-skillJavaAgent skill + 22 Ghidra scripts for fast targeted decompilation, and the fix for GhidraMCP extension/Ghidra version mismatches.Open repo Opens in a new tab.\nThat is also my own skill. Its related to how I wrote earlier how important it is to make the agent document everything into MD files. This skill forces agent who is getting stuck to follow this loop:\nMake a hypothesis of why / how something works. Write it down into CSV table of hypotheses, saved on disc.\nDesign experiment (probe, logging, ...) which will definitively prove or disprove this hypothesis.\nThis is variation of scientific method and it significantly helps agents when they are getting stuck on some problem. Stops it going in circles. By forcing it to do definitive experiments. No endless guessing and trying random things. This one is crucial in my opinion. I made it public too, so other can benefit from it.\nioma8hypothesis-debuggingAgent skill for hypothesis-driven debugging — one testable hypothesis at a time, logged and validated in a CSV loop.Open repo Opens in a new tab.\nImplementation of hacks and probes\nFor implementing anything I basically go for 2 toolings.\nPython but with UV tooling. UV tooling allows to make custom envs super quickly and portably. Also allows to make single-file, inline dependencies scripts. Python is great for prototyping or quickly making probes or even decompilation helpers.\nRust for actual implementation of given hack. Whenever it is possible I am using rust for this. It has many reasons but he two main are:\nit is very elegant language for me personally to work with and\nit provides brutally good feedback loops to the agent when it iterates - the compiler is very strict and points the agent immediately to any possible memory or syntax issues he might be heading to.\nOf course rust has also its downsides, like slower compile times, but overal its mature language with plethora of build targets (I have implemented in it webs, desktop apps, hacks / trainers but even firmwares for various embedded languages).\nWith the AI assisted reverse engineering I was able to achieve such crazy reverse engineering succeses, in the last 6 months, which would take me years each on its own, if done by hands.\nReverse engineered and hacked Alphasmart NEO typewriting device\nCompletely mapped communication protocols of many devices like EmWave biofeedback device or both my Sony Alpha mirrorless cameras. Allowing me to make custom desktop and mobile apps for file transfer, wireless control and more. I even managed to make this way a wireless webcam from my A7III mirrorless camera.\nHacked into and developed custom firmwares for musical synthesizers:\nMade custom firmware for Xteink X4 ebook reader\nFixed driver for old HP printer for MacOs\nCracked tons of  license-locked abandonware softwares from my youth\nDeveloped custom lightweight Evernote client\nSometimes its a thin boundary between reverse engineering and software development. Sometimes you just decipher the proprietary save file format of the ugly paid app and then move into the clean software development phase of building your new, totally unique editor, to work with these files. (yes I made custom Photoshop too and its not the ArtCraft suite - I did it about a year before them, just for me).\nReverse engineering and hacking into old softwares or devices has always been a hobby for me. I understand assembly, I understand hardware. But it was long and tedious process.\nWith the cheap accesibility to smart LLM tools in these days, this hobby took dramatic turn for me. I do not miss manually analyzing the assembly instructions. But I love quickly getting the results. Yes my long experience helps me steer the agents the correct way. But that is also fun. And I am learning a ton of things along the way.\nI wish everyone would try to do some hacks of his own. For me the hobby is driven by curiosity. And LLMs didn't take that away. They gave me turbo boost to my wonderings and explorations. Let's free our minds!\nEdit: We are getting popular on HN! Didnt expect this one day after deploying my blog! Super happy!",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "ioma8",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-18T22:29:13.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.slowchronicle.com/og-default.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.slowchronicle.com/posts/llm-assisted-reverse-engineering",
      "readTimeMinutes": 6,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-18-gta6-like-as-a-942kb-uefi-bootable-image",
      "title": "GTA6-like as a 942Kb UEFI bootable image",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://github.com/eklavya-raj/gta6-uefi\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50553192\nPoints: 11\n# Comments: 7",
      "body": "A GTA style game set in the real Miami that boots straight from UEFI, no Windows under it. The whole game is game.efi, 964,608 bytes, and the CPU draws every pixel.\nMade it for a video on my channel: https://www.youtube.com/@CodingWalaXD\nmkdir -p esp/EFI/BOOT && cp game.efi esp/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI\nqemu-system-x86_64 -machine q35 -m 512 -bios OVMF.fd -drive format=raw,file=fat:rw:esp\nbuild needs clang and lld-link, or skip it and use the game.efi that's already here. I've only run it in qemu so far.\narrows or wasd to drive, enter to get in and out, mouse for the camera, m for the map, j in a taxi for fares, 1 2 3 for weapons, ctrl to shoot.\nThe map is baked with osm/bake.py from OpenStreetMap, Miami-Dade County data, a NOAA lidar scan and NAIP photos. lidar/steps.txt has how, credits are in MAP_DATA.txt and PEOPLE_DATA.txt.",
