      "id": "bbc-2026-11-18-regulator-mistakenly-sent-complaints-about-doctors-to-newspa",
      "title": "Regulator mistakenly sent complaints about doctors to newspaper",
      "summary": "Information sent to the Times newspaper by the General Medical Council included summaries of complaints alleging antisemitism and Islamophobia against doctors.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, More than 200 doctors have had their details leaked to the Times newspaperThe doctors' regulator the General Medical Council (GMC) has apologised after it mistakenly sent information about more than 220 doctors to a national newspaper.The information included summaries of complaints alleging antisemitism or Islamophobia against the doctors.For some of the medics involved, it was only when the GMC alerted them to the data breach that they discovered a complaint had been made about them.The GMC says it is \"very sorry\" for the incident and has reported itself to the Information Commissioner's Office.The GMC - an independent body that regulates doctors, physician associates and anaesthesia associates to protect patient safety - sent the information about 223 doctors to a journalist at the Times newspaper. While no names were revealed, GMC identification numbers were included, which would easily allow doctors to be found on the regulator's register. Some doctors have also raised concerns that their ethnicity was included in the data breach. All the medics involved have been the subject of a complaint involving either antisemitism or Islamophobia, dating from the Hamas attack on Israel in October 2023 to September 2026.The GMC says it has a legal and statutory responsibility to investigate such claims. Meanwhile, the Times is understood to have promised not to make any of the leaked details public, but has said it will not delete the leaked information.Calls to investigate the leakA spokesman for the doctor's trade union, the British Medical Association (BMA), condemned the leak.\"The BMA is aware that a number of doctors have received communication from the GMC to say that it erroneously sent personal identifiable data about them to the Times newspaper when responding to a media request,\" it said.\"The GMC must take action to ensure such a breach never occurs again.\"The BMA said it would look at ways to support members affected and urged them to make contact via their website.A doctors' campaign group, The Doctors' Association UK (DAUK), called for an independent investigation into the leak.\"If a doctor sent identifiable patient information to a newspaper, they could expect to be referred to the GMC,\" said Dr Matt Kneale, DAUK co-chairman.\"The GMC should hold itself to the same standard of confidentiality it expects from doctors. It owes every affected doctor a clear account of what it sent, how that happened and what it is doing to get the information deleted.\"'Very sorry'The doctors' regulator says around a fifth (20%) of the complaints made have already resulted in some sanction related to antisemitism, including two doctors who have been struck off the medical register.Just over a quarter of complaints over Islamophobia - 26% - have led to disciplinary action. But other complaints were closed, having failed to reach the threshold for investigation. The GMC said it had contacted the doctors affected to explain what had happened, to apologise and provide information and support.A spokesperson added: \"We recognise the concern this will cause to both those impacted personally and other registrants and we are very sorry this happened.\"Our priority continues to be supporting those affected and taking steps to reduce the impact of this incident.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
