      "id": "bbc-2026-11-18-human-is-cambridge-dictionary-word-of-the-year",
      "title": "'Human' is Cambridge Dictionary Word of the Year",
      "summary": "The growth of AI means we need to say when something is made by humans, says the book's editor.",
      "body": "\"Human\" is the Cambridge Dictionary's Word of the Year — defined as \"having the qualities, faults, and feelings that people have, as opposed to gods, animals, or machines\".\"The growth of AI means it is now necessary to explicitly say 'human' for things that once could only be done by humans,\" said chief editor Colin McIntosh.Examples of current usage included actors Scarlett Johansson challenging \"an assault on human creativity\" and Matthew McConaughey protecting himself against unauthorised digital reproduction of his voice or image. \"The measure of AI's success should be how much it improves human lives,\" said Microsoft co-founder Bill Gates on learning about the dictionary's choice.McIntosh said dictionary editors had to be on top of how words could change over time \"to reflect what people say and what they write\".His research revealed a four-fold use of the word human since 1900.\"'Man' used to be used in this sense a lot more, but now it is not acceptable to use 'man' to mean men and women,\" he added. \"Human didn't come into use in Middle English until the 14th Century, from a French word. Shakespeare used it, but after that it was very restricted in use.\"There had also been an increased use of compound words such as human-made and human-driven, to distinguish the works, qualities and skills of people from AI.\"Fears about what humans may lose when we outsource our thinking have made us reconsider the extent to which we rely on AI, from top mathematicians to teachers of young children,\" said McIntosh.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
