    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-as-trump-and-xi-meet-china-s-ai-dreams-face-an-unexpected-hu",
      "title": "As Trump and Xi meet, China's AI dreams face an unexpected hurdle: the Nvidia spy scandal",
      "summary": "China worries the scandal could ramp up pressure on the US  to further restrict the sale of advanced chips.",
      "body": "Jensen Huang is out but his shadow looms over the upcoming meeting between US President Donald Trump and his Chinese counterpart Xi Jinping this week. For Beijing, the former Nvidia CEO's dramatic fall is a major challenge - and not just because of claims that a Chinese spy may have honey-trapped Huang, one of the artificial intelligence industry's most influential figures.Rather, the allegations surrounding Huang and his affair with Chinese businesswoman Alice Huang are turning into a new flashpoint in an already fragile US-China relationship.US intelligence reports claim she had ties to Beijing's intelligence apparatus - in particular the Ministry of State Security, China's main civilian spy agency. The claim remains unproven, but it has already reignited debate in Washington over exports of advanced chips to China and whether the Trump administration has been too willing to ease restrictions.For years now, advanced computer chips have been a key battleground in the US-China rivalry because they are essential for AI, supercomputing and military technology.Chinese officials are worried this scandal could strengthen voices in Washington who argue in favour of further limiting Chinese access to cutting-edge American tech.Just this week, the Trump administration introduced new export restrictions on Chinese firms that access advanced US chips in third countries such as Malaysia, Thailand or Brazil. They typically do this by renting computing power in data centres abroad to train their AI models. But some of the firms, like the one Alice Huang worked for, are also accused of smuggling chips to China, although they have denied this.Until now, this controversy appeared to be a sideshow in US-China relations. But with Beijing calling the new rules \"economic coercion\" on the eve of Trump arriving in Shenzhen, there is a growing risk it could become something much bigger.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T23:02:12.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ec0d/live/1061d030-dea2-11f1-b441-2b33b063138f.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5vg23g92rj4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-american-soldier-arrested-by-uk-police-on-suspicion-of-spyin",
      "title": "American soldier arrested by UK police on suspicion of spying taken into US military custody",
      "summary": "The Metropolitan Police say he has been taken into custody by the US Military over suspected national security and espionage allegations.",
      "body": "Article: published on 17 November 2026Image source, FacebookImage caption, Lt Col Brandon Carroll Tyner was arrested on 10 November American soldier arrested by UK police on suspicion of spying taken into US military custodyByHafsa Khalil and Daniel SandfordUK correspondentA US Army officer arrested in the UK on suspicion of spying for Russia earlier this month has been taken into US military custody, British counter-terror police have said.The serviceman has been identified as Lt Col Brandon Carroll Tyner, BBC News understands. The 41-year-old - who served in the US Army since 2008 - was arrested near Gloucester last week by British counter-terror police. Tyner was based at Imjin Barracks in Innsworth, Gloucestershire, the headquarters of Nato's Allied Rapid Reaction Corps.Counter Terrorism Policing said he is now in US custody over suspected national security and espionage allegations. At the time of his arrest, Russia said it had \"nothing to do with it\".British police previously said he was being held in London after he was arrested under section one and section three of the National Security Act 2023, which relate to obtaining or disclosing protected information, and assisting a foreign intelligence service.The day after Tyner's arrest on 10 November, Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said he was \"unaware\" of what it was connected to. \"We have nothing to do with it, and it has nothing to do with us,\" he told reporters in Moscow.Image source, AFP via Getty ImagesImage caption, The US serviceman is understood to have been based at Imjin Barracks in GloucestershireOn Tuesday, Assistant Commissioner Laurence Taylor, head of Counter Terrorism Policing, said: \"Subsequently, a decision was made to pass the investigation to them [their American partners] to progress.\"\"These are extremely serious allegations, and our collective response should send a strong message that we will act swiftly and robustly where we suspect this kind of espionage activity taking place,\" he added.Tyner's arrest was the result of a long-running operation by MI5 - the UK's domestic intelligence and security agency - in collaboration with the FBI, the BBC understands.He had previously served in Afghanistan and Iraq, but at the time of his arrest was working with NATO's Allied Rapid Reaction Corps near Gloucester - where he was said to be entrusted with sensitive information which would have been extremely damaging if leaked to Russia.Detectives sent the Crown Prosecution Service a file on the senior US soldier's alleged spying for consideration - allegations described by the head of Counter Terrorism Policing as \"extremely serious.\"But at that stage the Attorney General's Office in London got involved. As did the US authorities.And now the decision has been taken that Lieutenant Colonel Tyner should be taken into custody by the US Military.It means he will not stand trial in the UK, but under US Uniform Code of Military Justice.FBI director Kash Patel said an army officer was in custody \"as the result of an espionage investigation and the close cooperation\" between the bureau, US Army and various UK partners.\"Together we worked to identify a potential threat to national security and take swift action,\" he said. \"This FBI has zero tolerance for anyone who betrays their oath to the United States or puts our national security at risk.\"US Brig Gen Sean Stinchon said the case should serve as a \"stark warning to any service member suspected of violating the oath\" they all take.\"Whenever we suspect somebody of betraying the trust of their fellow soldiers and jeopardising our collective defence, we will identify you, and we will ensure justice is served,\" he added.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T22:46:30.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/42be/live/81981920-e2db-11f1-950d-ffe0c8f51f90.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6x284ez38vdo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-thomas-markle-charged-over-child-sex-abuse-material-in-phili",
      "title": "Thomas Markle charged over child sex abuse material in Philippines",
      "summary": "Markle is being held in custody in the US after one charge and has previously denied all of the allegations.",
      "body": "Police in the Philippines have charged Thomas Markle Sr, the father of Meghan, Duchess of Sussex, with possession of and intent to distribute child sexual abuse material.The charges were filed on Monday, Cebu province prosecutor Graeme Elmido told the BBC.Earlier this month, Markle, 82, was separately arrested and charged in the US with possession of child sexual abuse material. The FBI said agents, after searching storage units Markle rented, seized about 78 items, including film negatives, slides and books, that they allege contained child sex abuse material.Markle, who is in custody, has previously denied the allegations. The BBC has contacted Markle's attorney in the US for comment.Elmido told the BBC that Philippine National Police had charged Markle Sr with \"two counts of intent to distribute CSAEM [child sexual abuse and exploitation material], and one count of possession of CSAEM material\".Last month, police in the Philippines obtained a warrant to examine devices allegedly removed from Markle's former home in the country.Four boxes of material, including computers, phones, hard drives and old video tapes, were handed to officers by Meghan's half brother Thomas Markle Jr.Earlier this month, Markle Sr was arrested and charged in the US. He had initially been placed under house detention at his assisted living facility in Florida, but was taken back into custody last week after prosecutors alleged he removed his ankle monitor. Meghan Markle has been estranged from her father since 2018. The BBC understands she broke contact with him last year, telling him she and the authorities knew he had abused young girls.In a previously shared statement after the allegations against her father emerged, Meghan wrote: \"I am deeply distressed... my thoughts are with any children who may have been harmed.\"She said she hoped it could be understood why she had largely cut off contact from her father and did \"not allow him to have contact with our children\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T22:44:15.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5b0e/live/040fc640-dd79-11f1-93e1-3b657b665bcf.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckg5ry8m49ggo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-17-jeff-bezos-says-he-was-heavily-involved-in-picking-the-new-j",
      "title": "Jeff Bezos says he was ‘heavily involved’ in picking the new James Bond",
      "summary": "Robert Pattinson's casting as James Bond made enough sense given his recent work with director Denis Villeneuve, who's making the franchise's next film over at Amazon. But it turns out that Amazon founder Jeff Bezos played a significant role in making the final decision, too.\nDuring Semafor's Silicon Valley and the World conference today, Bezos said that he was \"heavily involved\" in the process of",
      "body": "Robert Pattinson’s casting as James Bond made enough sense given his recent work with director Denis Villeneuve, who’s making the franchise’s next film over at Amazon. But it turns out that Amazon founder Jeff Bezos played a significant role in making the final decision, too.\nDuring Semafor’s Silicon Valley and the World conference today, Bezos said that he was “heavily involved” in the process of casting the newest 007:\n> We ran a really tight process and we made the final decision on Friday. There was a big group initially, and we down-selected to five finalists. Then we did real old-fashioned screen tests of those five finalists, and it was really interesting — full costume, real sets, other actors. We did three different scenes with each of the five finalists, and then we got together on Friday to spend a couple of hours looking at all those screening tests, and reviewing all of that.  And all of the actors were extraordinarily talented.\n> By the time we got to this list of five, they were all amazing, but there was something special about Rob Pattinson and his performances. It was kind of immediately undebated. Denis — the director — we all sort of gravitated to Rob. By the way, initially, I would say we were slightly biased a little against him, just because he’s already so famous. We sort of thought, ‘maybe we should have somebody a little more unknown.’  But in the screen test, it was so obvious. He is James Bond.\nIt’s not exactly surprising that a studio leader would want to have a say in casting the new face for one of their most lucrative franchises. But with Bezos being so hands-on, one can only image what this new era of Bond films will look and feel like once Amazon starts releasing them in theaters.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Amazon",
        "Entertainment",
        "Film",
        "News",
        "Streaming",
        "Tech"
