      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-south-africa-head-coach-erasmus-banned-for-six-matches",
      "title": "South Africa head coach Erasmus banned for six matches",
      "summary": "South Africa head coach Rassie Erasmus has been banned for six matches by World Rugby over comments he made about match officials.",
      "body": "South Africa head coach Rassie Erasmus has been banned for six matches by World Rugby over comments he made about match officials in his side's Nations Championship defeat by France last week.Speaking at a news conference conference in Dublin on Monday before the Springboks' match with Ireland, Erasmus questioned what he perceived as double standards in officiating to the point where he asked: \"is that not match-fixing?\".The two-time Rugby World Cup-winning coach was referencing the implementation of new rules surrounding hip-drop and knee-drop tackles, and felt the laws were not being applied correctly.It comes after Ireland fly-half Sam Prendergast became the first player to be cited for a hip-drop tackle in Ireland's win over Fiji on Saturday, which was not penalised in-play by referee Angus Gardner and television match official [TMO] Mike Adamson.World Rugby said an independent panel determined that Erasmus' comments \"suggest that match officials acted with bias/prejudice or deliberately influenced the outcome of a match\". In response, South Africa Rugby said it has requested a full misconduct hearing and an opportunity for Erasmus to \"contextualise his comments and assure the panel that it was not his intention to allege that any match official had acted with ulterior motives\". It is the third time Erasmus has been sanctioned by World Rugby over his comments about match officials. When he was South Africa's director of rugby, he was suspended for two months by World Rugby after he posted an hour-long video criticising the refereeing in the Springboks' opening Test defeat by the British and Irish Lions in 2021.World Rugby found the video contained \"multiple abusive and insulting comments and attacks on the officials\".In 2022, he received a two-match ban for a series of tweets criticising officials in a defeat by France.Erasmus' latest ban has been imposed under World Rugby's match official abuse sanction process, which was introduced by the governing body in July. If the suspension is upheld, Erasmus will miss Saturday's game with Ireland in Dublin (16:40 GMT), next week's finals weekend fixture at Twickenham's Allianz Stadium and the start of his side's Rugby Championship campaign next summer.In a statement on Tuesday, World Rugby said an independent panel determined that Erasmus' comments had met  \"the threshold for serious misconduct\".World Rugby confirmed the ban will stop Erasmus having any match day involvement for six matches, which includes coaching, direct or indirect contact with the team management or players on match days, entering the stadium, coaches' box, playing enclosure or technical area.\"The panel determined that the comments suggest that match officials acted with bias/prejudice or deliberately influenced the outcome of a match, and when the conduct is considered as a whole a reasonable person would conclude that the conduct constituted an unacceptable attack on the credibility and competence of the match officials,\" its statement said.\"In respect to the sanction, the panel determined that the conduct clearly amounts to disrespecting the authority of the match officials by impugning their integrity. \"Therefore, the panel reviewed a number of elements of the commentary including the timing and reach of the statements, combined with the allegations of bias or bad faith, and found that the comments attracted a six-match automatic suspension.\"World Rugby added that \"respect is fundamental to rugby, and match officials are central to the sport\". \"Protecting their wellbeing and integrity, and their right to perform without abuse or intimidation, is a responsibility World Rugby and unions take seriously,\" their statement added. \"Coaches, players, unions and everyone in the game must uphold these standards.\"Robust, respectful debate is natural, but teams have access to well-established and supported channels and protocols for raising officiating matters before and after matches. Challenging a decision is not the same as questioning an official's integrity or making comments that expose them to personal abuse.\"In the same news conference, which lasted more than half an hour at the Springboks' team hotel, Erasmus also questioned what he felt were double standards in terms of sanctions.The outcome of the citing has yet to be released, but he predicted that Prendergast will be available to play against South Africa due to a \"technicality\", and also questioned the decision not to ban Ireland prop Tadhg Furlong for an alleged groin grab while playing for Leinster ahead of the November Tests.Last year, South Africa prop Jan-Hendrik Wessels was handed a nine-match ban for a similar offence and missed November's matches for the Springboks.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
