      "id": "bbc-2026-11-17-where-are-the-most-jobs-for-18-24-year-olds",
      "title": "Where are the most jobs for 18-24 year-olds?",
      "summary": "How many jobs are there where you live and in which sectors are you most likely to find an opening?",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesByTommy LumbyBusiness data journalist and Nick EdserBusiness reporterIf you're one of the nearly one million people aged 16-24 not in education, employment or training - otherwise known as \"Neet\" - then you might be wondering where to start.Under-25s should be guaranteed job placements or training to help them find permanent work, a major review aimed at cutting the number of Neets will say.But how many jobs are there where you live? Use our look-up tool to find out.Where are the available jobs?Where you live will often have a big impact on your ability to find a job.The Institute for Public Policy Research (IPPR) think tank used online job adverts to help identify the number of vacancies suitable for young people in the area they live, relative to the size of the local population.It used AI to help identify the jobs, which were broadly defined as roles requiring relatively low experience or education.According to its analysis, South Oxfordshire had 387 suitable vacancies per 1,000 18 to 24-year-olds in the area - the most of any local authority. Rotherham had the least, at 53 per 1,000.Use this tool to check out the situation in your area. The data for London is only available for the region as a whole and is not broken down by individual councils.Some people live in one area and work in another, but the analysis did not account for this.Which sectors have the most entry-level jobs?Even if there are jobs available in your area, you might not find one in the sector you want to work in.The sector with the highest average number of entry-level vacancies in the year to October was teaching, with most of those jobs being for teaching assistants, learning support and cover supervisor roles, according to jobs website Adzuna.The next largest was hospitality and catering, traditionally an area where younger people get their first experience of work.Adzuna, a major online jobs website, includes adverts for full-time roles marked as entry-level, those asking for up to two years' experience, graduate roles, internships and apprenticeships.Accounting and finance had the third-highest number of job openings.What are the typical rates of pay?Figures on salaries for vacancies can be patchy because many employers do not include them in job adverts.Prime Minister Andy Burnham recently urged businesses to start publishing salaries ahead of plans to make pay details compulsory in adverts.Only about 40% of Adzuna's jobs data for the year to October stated a salary, although that figure varies widely by sector.Here are the average salaries for jobs in the sectors with the largest number of entry-level jobs, according to Adzuna. The highest average salary is £30,530 in engineering, while social care has the lowest at £25,880.How many job vacancies are there overall?While it can be hard for anyone looking for their first job, the situation is also tough for older jobseekers.Across the whole economy there were 690,000 jobs available in the August to October period this year, which is the lowest number since early 2021.The Covid pandemic led to huge swings in the number of job vacancies available as companies reacted to shutdowns and then reopened as restrictions were lifted.Stripping out the fluctuations seen during the pandemic, the number of vacancies is now at its lowest level for 12 years.Apprenticeships have often been a way for people to get their first taste of employment.In England the number of people starting apprenticeships has risen in recent years, although this has been driven by an increase in those aged 25 and over after schemes for older workers were fully rolled out in 2008.The number of people aged 24 and under starting apprenticeships in England in 2025 was 172,230, down from a peak of 291,300 in 2012.Get in touchShare your experiences and ask your questions. More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T12:35:53.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/0f7b/live/5b9d4c80-e345-11f1-99a1-253766e7ad8b.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cw3djdjk5d7ko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
