      "title": "Business Daily",
      "summary": "Jensen Huang quit Nvidia, but the AI chip boom rolls on. What's changed is who's paying?",
      "body": "Homepage    Accessibility links   Skip to content   Accessibility Help     BBC Account      Notifications        Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live               More menu             Search Sounds              Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food        Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live         Close menu   BBC SoundsSounds home pageHomeMusicPodcastsMy SoundsUse BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsWorld Service,·17 Nov 2026,·17 minsFollow the money: Who pays for the AI boom now?Business DailyAvailable for over a yearJensen Huang has left Nvidia, but the money behind AI chips is still flowing to a handful of firms. What's changed is who's paying. Five months after our first look at the chips powering AI, Sam Fenwick follows the money again, examining soaring memory prices, growing competition with China and why the costs of the AI boom are increasingly being felt beyond the technology industry.\n(Photo: The Nvidia logo sign displayed outside the corporation's headquarters in Taipei, Taiwan, 9 November 2026. Credit: Ritchie B Tongo/EPA/Shutterstock)\nYou can email the team: businessdaily@bbc.co.uk",
