      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-16-sam-altman-on-nvidia-without-jensen-huang-the-industry-will-",
      "title": "Sam Altman on Nvidia without Jensen Huang: The industry will be ‘fine’",
      "summary": "OpenAI CEO Sam Altman acknowledged the special role Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has played in the AI boom, but ultimately thinks the industry will be “fine” without him at the helm.",
      "body": "OpenAI CEO Sam Altman says the AI industry will be “fine” without Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang at the helm, even as he acknowledged the special role Huang has played in the AI boom.\nHuang resigned last week from Nvidia after admitting to having an affair with an alleged Chinese spy.\nAltman made his first public comments about Huang’s departure on Monday at Semafor’s Silicon Valley & The World event, where he also discussed topics ranging from data center pushback to recursive self-improvement.\nNumerous executives and members of the AI industry have argued for Nvidia to reinstate Huang, including Elon Musk and Epic Games chief executive Tim Sweeney, who were among the signatories in a public letter. Many fear that Huang’s departure may cause Nvidia to rethink the enormous financial risks it has taken that have fueled the AI boom.\nHuang is also reportedly setting up a “war room” of Silicon Valley executives to lobby for his return.\nWhen asked whether he thought Huang should return to the company, Altman said: “I don’t think I know enough to answer that question.”\nHe expressed empathy for Huang’s situation, citing his own ousting from OpenAI in 2023 after board members accused him of withholding information.\n“It’s hard to imagine the AI revolution without Jensen and the company he has built,” Altman said, adding that he sees Huang as a hero, and that “heroes have flaws.”\nOpenAI and Nvidia have had a close relationship for the last decade, one that has evolved from a simple chip supplier-customer relationship into a potentially circular financing arrangement, in which Nvidia’s money helps fund purchases of its own chips. Most recently, Nvidia agreed to provide a guarantee of up to $105 billion backing OpenAI’s Ohio data center project. That’s just one of many such circular deals Nvidia has made to bankroll the AI boom while fueling demand for its own chips.\nAltman said he’s not worried that the Bank of Nvidia will dry up.\n“Nvidia is, like, a behemoth of a company,” he said. “I think people still trust their guarantees very much.”\nAltman acknowledged the “ambition” Huang brought to the industry, the scale of which “has been quite important.” But he didn’t go so far as to agree with the many who worry about ripple effects from Huang’s departure.\n“I think there’s so much demand, and it’s so clear that AI is real, and people want to use it, and the revenues and demand are growing so strongly that I think the industry will be fine,” Altman said. “But again, I think Jensen has played this extremely special and valuable role as someone to really drive things forward with great ambition.”\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nRebecca Bellan is a senior reporter at TechCrunch where she covers the business, policy, and emerging trends shaping artificial intelligence. Her work has also appeared in Forbes, Bloomberg, The Atlantic, The Daily Beast, and other publications.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Rebecca by emailing [email protected] or via encrypted message at rebeccabellan.491 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
