      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-barristers-must-stop-using-rape-myths-in-trials-justice-secr",
      "title": "Barristers must stop using rape myths in trials, justice secretary says",
      "summary": "Russell Brand's barrister has been accused of peddling rape myths while defending the comedian.",
      "body": "Brand was sentenced to 10-and-a-half years in prison for one count of rape and three counts of sexual assault.Winter said to the court \"not one of them [Brand's accusers] resisted or screamed or even tried to leave\".Winter also asked the jury if they could be sure that out of the \"many, many women\" Brand had had sex with over a decade, \"he raped just three of them\".Norris wrote in his letter to the BSB: \"[The board] must send a strong signal to the profession on the harm arising from rape myths, and that the perpetuation of such myths within a court room is unacceptable, just as they are unacceptable in our society at large.\"The charity Rape Crisis says it is \"really common for people who experience rape, sexual assault, sexual abuse or other types of sexual violence to find they can't move or speak\".The judge presiding over Brand's case was praised by the Criminal Bar Association and campaign groups for comments he made during his sentencing remarks to dispel rape myths.Mr Justice Bennathan told Southwark Crown Court: \"Whatever women wear, wherever they go, indeed whatever they drink, they retain the right to say no and to demand justice from the criminal courts when sexual predators like you, Russell Brand, act without their consent.\"A spokesperson for the BSB said such cases \"must be decided on the basis of the evidence and not prejudice\", adding: \"Any other approach may discourage others from coming forward and adversely affect public confidence in the criminal justice system more widely.\"Kirsty Brimelow KC, chair of the Bar Council of England and Wales which is part of the group examining the need for further training, said it was important for the BSB to remain \"independent of the government\".She told BBC Radio 4's Woman's Hour that a \"regulatory gap\" had not been made clear to her and there were already powers to discipline barristers if breaches of the code of conduct happen. She said training for \"vulnerable witnesses\" was already undertaken but it could be \"problematic\" if it was made mandatory.Brimelow said there were currently a lack of barristers defending cases of rape and sexual assault and the possibility of further compulsory training could see more pushed out.\"What I wouldn't like to see is publicity around one case to take a life of its own rather than looking at what we already do,\" she told the BBC.A spokesperson for the Ministry of Justice said the justice secretary was \"determined to make protections for victims as strong as possible\". \"Rape myths have absolutely no place in our courts, giving evidence in cases of this nature is a brave act and can be traumatic for victims: they must never feel as though they are the ones on trial,\" the spokesperson said.\"Independent judges are already able to, and do, give directions to counter rape myths but we always want to keep looking at ways we can improve how the entire justice system treats victims of sexual violence.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T13:28:44.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/1dd3/live/4146e090-e1aa-11f1-8ee3-23d0d09d2796.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cj04w7n74gqno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-create-more-jobs-for-under-25s-by-cutting-national-insurance",
      "title": "Create more jobs for under-25s by cutting National Insurance, key report urges",
      "summary": "Some parents are so desperate for their children to find a job that they've offered to pay their wages, one hairdresser has told the BBC.",
      "body": "Her workforce has more than halved to 21 staff. Osborne told the BBC it costs \"around £60,000 to train an apprentice before we can actually start allowing them onto customers' heads\".She said she has had parents so desperate for their children to find employment at her salons that some have even offered to pay their wages.\"I'm inundated with applications - and I mean inundated - which is heartbreaking because I can't take them on,\" she said.Osborne does not feel she is getting help to offer jobs to youngsters. \"I feel like the government's foot is firmly on my neck,\" she said. \"They promise breathing space. I feel suffocated, suffocated by employment costs.\"The independent report by former Labour minister Alan Milburn has been in the works for a year and will be published on Tuesday in full.He has already unveiled some of his proposals including:Reducing benefits - under-25s should no longer be able to claim the health element of UK-wide Universal Credit or personal independence payment (Pip), which is the main disability benefit in England and WalesA legal guarantee of a job, training or employment support with those who refuse to take up potentially losing their benefits.Doubling the threshold at which businesses start paying NI for 21 to 24-year-olds from £5,000 to £10,000.At the moment, businesses pay no employer NI contributions for workers younger than 21 or for apprentices under 25 – unless they earn above a threshold of  £50,270.However, for non-apprentices aged 21-24, employers pay 15% on annual earnings above £5,000.This has been the situation since April 2025, after the then Chancellor Rachel Reeves increased the rate from 13.8% and lowered the threshold from £9,100.Under Sir Keir Starmer, the government raised NI for firms but Milburn will say the Neets issue was not started by the increase.Immediately axing NI for 21 to 24-year-olds would cost £5.1bn, according to research by the Resolution Foundation, a left-leaning think tank.Milburn is set to say that an immediate across-the-board cut in employer NI is not the best use of several billions of pounds of public money.But shadow work and pensions secretary, Helen Whately, said the government should \"just stop doing the taxing\".Milburn told the BBC's Laura Kuenssberg programme he supported the government subsidising wages to encourage businesses to take on young workers. \"Because young people today are walking into the workplace often with a mental health problem, ADHD or anxiety, if you're a small business that's a cost that you've got to absorb,\" he said.\"The state has a responsibility to help them with that.\"On Sunday, Work and Pensions Secretary Pat McFadden said the government would extend its subsidised jobs guarantee scheme for 18 to 24-year-olds who are claiming Universal Credit. People who have been on benefits for 12 months will be eligible, down from 18 months and the scheme would be available for people in England, Scotland and Wales.McFadden said the scheme, which guarantees workers a six-month job, \"will help more than 90,000 young people into work by March 2029\".Additional reporting by Ollie Smith and Anuk Weerawardana",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T13:19:33.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/283e/live/4753f640-e1ae-11f1-b0a4-450a350911f4.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6p9dzmj514ko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-16-microsoft-blames-monster-email-outage-on-software-defect-int",
      "title": "Microsoft blames monster email outage on software defect introduced in July fix",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.theregister.com/saas/2026/11/16/microsoft-blames-monster-email-outage-on-software-defect-introduced-in-july-fix/5310026\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50517321\nPoints: 391\n# Comments: 296",
      "body": "Summer code change caused a Fall fail – frantic engineers had to patch the patch instead of rolling back\nMicrosoft has blamed last week's epic outage on a change it made in July that lurked in the network gateway servers until a surge in load and a drop in capacity triggered the defect, giving the Microsoft 365 world a day off from email.According to a preliminary post incident report published by Microsoft on November 15, the defect was introduced in July 2026. The update looked fine at the time, Microsoft says, having passed load testing and been deployed without any impact.However, the testing wasn't good enough. Microsoft's report states: \"Our load testing tended to focus on weaker hardware with fewer cores.\" The defect caused \"lock contention inside an internal Windows process which caused the machine's resources (CPU & memory) to be exhausted.\"\n\"The contention issue is worsened on machines with more cores and newer hardware.\"\nThe fix containing the defect was prepared specifically for Microsoft 365 network gateway servers. Then two things happened at once. Teams failed over to the \"primary\" gateway that supports Exchange Online, creating a surge in load that was not, in itself, particularly unusual. At the same time, almost 14 percent of server capacity for the networking gateway was out of service, which wasn't a problem by itself either: it was on its way to being restored and, according to Microsoft, \"the service had sufficient capacity headroom.\" Together, they pushed the gateway into the defect and chaos ensued. It was a classic cascading failure scenario. When a server loses connectivity, traffic gets routed to the next healthy endpoint. Which encountered the same problem. So traffic was rerouted again and… you can see where this was going. Within two hours, the defect was encountered across Microsoft's fleet. \"At its lowest,\" the report states, \"more than 80 percent of servers were unresponsive.\"So, what to do? Bringing servers back to service didn't help – they were immediately overwhelmed and fell over again. \"Our normally automated recovery processes were not designed to restore service from this broad unresponsive state.\" A straightforward rollback was also not possible because the defect was months old.Instead, engineers developed patch for the patch or, in Microsoft's words, \"an update that returned the code path to the previously known good state in Windows.\" The patch was applied to groups of servers and service was gradually restored.The timings show the pain customers experienced and the floundering in Redmond as Microsoft tried to work out why Exchange Online and other cloud services were so poorly. The Teams failover, which turned out to be a factor in the outage, happened at 0107 UTC on November 12. During the day, the load began to ramp up until, at 1603 UTC, Microsoft's connectivity anomaly detection monitor identified an increase in connectivity issues.At 1633 UTC, the first support case came in.It is not difficult to imagine the stress engineers felt as, over the next 15 hours, nothing they tried resolved the issue. All ongoing and planned deployments and configuration changes were paused while the team attempted to resurrect the service. Extra capacity was added. Traffic was rerouted. Yet servers kept falling over.\n\"Those services that were able – like Teams, OneDrive, SharePoint, and Office apps – failed out of the faulty network gateway infrastructure,\" the report says. \"But not all services, like Exchange Online, can fail out of this network gateway for scale reasons.\"Hence the length of time for which Exchange Online (or, from the perspective of many users, Outlook) was out of action.By 0030 UTC on November 13, Microsoft had identified connection negotiation as the main culprit for the access failures and concluded that competing processes were preventing connections from being handled.However, it took until 0720 UTC before \"We identified a mitigation and applied the mitigation –  an update that reverted the defective change.\"At 1610 UTC, Microsoft confirmed that things were once again looking healthy and, at 0415 on November 14, issued its final customer update.A final post incident report is due on November 20. However, the preliminary report will do little to calm jangling nerves. An update applied three months ago managed to cause Exchange Online (and other services) disruption on a global scale? With load testing that didn't reflect scenarios under Microsoft's control?The engineers who worked feverishly to restore services deserve commendation and will hopefully never have to buy their own beverages in Redmond again (or at least escape the next round of firings). However, the processes that led to an update with a latent defect reaching production deserve a long, hard look. ®",
      "source": "Hacker News",
