      "id": "theverge-2026-11-16-the-nvidia-scandal-isn-t-slowing-down",
      "title": "The Nvidia scandal isn’t slowing down",
      "summary": "For years, Jensen Huang has had an almost unprecedented power to shape AI's advancement as Nvidia's CEO, cofounder, and president. As The Verge wrote last week, Nvidia's chips are \"the bedrock upon which advanced AI technology in the US is being built.\" Ever-increasing demand has consistently outpaced supply, leading AI CEOs to essentially bow at Huang's feet as they vie for more compute ahead of ",
      "body": "For years, Jensen Huang has had an almost unprecedented power to shape AI’s advancement as Nvidia’s CEO, cofounder, and president. As The Verge wrote last week, Nvidia’s chips are “the bedrock upon which advanced AI technology in the US is being built.” Ever-increasing demand has consistently outpaced supply, leading AI CEOs to essentially bow at Huang’s feet as they vie for more compute ahead of their much-hyped IPOs. Nvidia also has a varied investment portfolio of up-and-coming AI companies from neoclouds to infrastructure firms, worth nearly $100 billion, and Huang has President Donald Trump’s ear. He’s even been called Trump’s “most influential adviser” when it comes to technology. As of last Wednesday, however, Huang — a pivotal figure in the industry — is no longer CEO of Nvidia.\nSince Huang’s resignation last week, the scandal over his multiple affairs has shown no sign of waning. After the announcement, Nvidia shares dropped 14 percent, making it one of the worst weeks on the market for the company in the past year and a half. And the revelations kept coming. Hours after the resignation, Reuters reported that Huang had had a third affair with a second employee (though the employee was unnamed). We learned that Huang had reportedly misled Nvidia leaders about his infidelity, that Nvidia was allegedly linked to the supply of nearly $12 billion of its chips to China, and that alleged Chinese intelligence asset Alice Huang’s companies reportedly promised advanced chips to Chinese AI company DeepSeek.\nThere’s reportedly been a petition to bring back Huang by some Nvidia employees, as well as reports of Huang assembling a “war room” of allies to float a comeback as Nvidia CEO. Lawmakers have begun investigations into the alleged chip-smuggling and other offenses. Every day, there’s increasing evidence of fallout inside the Trump administration. And as Nvidia’s quarterly earnings on Tuesday loom, the future of the company and the AI industry as a whole remain in flux.\nWhat does an Nvidia without Huang look like? It’s hard to say, since he’s been at the helm for 33 years. CFO Colette Kress has taken on the role of interim CEO, and the broader AI world is waiting to see where one of the world’s most valuable companies — and one that helped shape the industry’s every milestone — goes from here.\nNvidia is such a large company that the implications of Huang’s departure “will take years to play out,” said Stephen Straus, CEO of KUNGFU.AI, an AI consulting firm. “[Nvidia has] their market share … they have the world’s best chips, they have the most manufacturing capacity … and they have a whole ecosystem built around what they’ve done. None of that changes with one person.” While there’s been discussion of Huang’s departure speeding along an AI bubble burst, Straus compares the situation to the dot-com bust — which didn’t stop the growth of the internet itself.\nHuang’s resignation may not cause a domino effect in AI, but longer-term AI funding initiatives championed by him may eventually be affected, says Lior Prosor, managing partner at Deep33, a tech VC firm. Huang championed fundraising for the broader AI buildout: In August, Nvidia signed deals with six large financial institutions for about $500 billion to fund AI infrastructure expansion, and those agreements or future ones could look different if “the most prominent CEO in the AI landscape is no longer orchestrating such deals,” wrote Morningstar senior equity analyst Brian Colello in a note last week on Monday.\nFar downstream of the biggest companies, smaller AI startups are wondering how the effects will trickle down. Michal Miernowski, co-founder and CEO of Amperos Health, which builds AI agents for healthcare billing, sits in the applied AI space about two layers removed from Nvidia. He doesn’t anticipate Amperos being directly affected much by the moves, since it relies on a mix of frontier AI models and open-source ones, but a cooling of the larger AI market — whether due to the Nvidia scandal or broader concerns — could stall business as well. There’s also more to come on Nvidia’s side, from the company’s investigation into Huang’s actions to Congressional inquiries. Evan Gotlob, partner at DarrowEverett LLP and a former state and federal prosecutor, said he thinks “there’s definitely more” to the story about potential leaks to China and suspicious chip dealing. He added the board’s investigation would “obviously” continue despite his departure: “The investigation is really not going to turn out anything good for him.”\nSome new lawmakers already planned to haul CEOs to Capitol Hill to testify under oath. The AI populism movement is growing, as well as the blowback against data centers, which was a key talking point in Democrats’ campaigns during the midterms. The revelations around Huang, a major Trump ally, provide an early opportunity to take action.\n“We want to see a Democratic Congress investigate Nvidia for anti-competitive practices that pass off costs to consumers, what the company knew about chip smuggling operations with China, and what Nvidia received in exchange for donations to the Trump campaign and ballroom,” Sacha Haworth, founder and CEO of The Tech Oversight Project, told The Verge.\nAI safety researchers also worry that in the worst-case scenario, the instability around last week’s events could speed up potentially dangerous AI milestones like recursive self-improvement, i.e., powerful AI that can autonomously improve and build future versions of itself. Marius Hobbhahn, CEO and founder of Apollo Research, an AI safety firm and third-party auditor, said he worries that the volatility stemming from Huang’s departure “actually increases loss of control risk because companies feel even more revenue pressure and therefore are more willing to cut corners on safety.”\nEarly last week, before Huang’s resignation, the general consensus among Silicon Valley heavyweights, analysts, and politicians was that Huang’s “irreplaceable” impact would mean a return to the status quo for Nvidia. Morningstar senior equity analyst Brian Colello wrote in a note that day that the firm “can’t rule out … Nvidia returning to business as usual” since Huang is the “only CEO the company has ever known.” Now, as Huang reportedly assembles allies to push for a comeback the likes of which haven’t been seen since the 2023 Sam Altman coup, the entire AI industry is waiting to see which way it will go.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Analysis",
        "Chips",
        "Gadgets",
        "Nvidia",
        "Policy",
        "Report",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Hayden Field",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T13:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/STKP210_JENSEN_HUANG_D.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1031740/jensen-huang-nvidia-scandal-isnt-slowing-down",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-wales-legend-davies-doubts-wru-can-build-on-beating-all-blac",
      "title": "Wales legend Davies doubts WRU can build on beating All Blacks",
      "summary": "Welsh legend Sir Gerald Davies questions the Welsh Rugby Union's ability to capitalise on the nation's historic victory over New Zealand.",
      "body": "Welsh rugby legend Sir Gerald Davies has questioned the Welsh Rugby Union's ability to fully capitalise on the nation's historic victory over New Zealand.Wales' first win against the All Blacks in 73 years has provided a timely boost for everyone connected to rugby in the country during a period of significant uncertainty.Steve Tandy's side delivered the memorable performance in front of a fervent but lowest Principality Stadium crowd of 55,152 for a Wales-New Zealand match.Not too long ago Wales were mired in a world record 18-Test losing streak, while a shadow hangs over the future of regional rugby with the Welsh Rugby Union (WRU) intent on cutting a team in 2028.Davies, WRU president from 2019 to 2023, believes the gutsy showing against New Zealand has lifted spirits but wants clear direction for Welsh rugby's future.\"New Zealand are the barometer around the world, but there's a big question about the future and where Wales goes from here,\" Sir Gerald told BBC Radio Cymru.\"Are the administrators within the union able to show leadership and the vision to improve the state of the game in Wales?\"The legendary British and Irish Lions wing said Wales needs a team to inspire the next generation to believe again after some tough years.\"It is great to celebrate and correct to feel proud of what we saw at the weekend, but the big question is what about tomorrow and what is the plan?\" he added.\"So far, I'm afraid I don't think we've seen the leadership required [from the WRU].\"We have to give a big thanks to the players for Saturday, they have raised the spirit of the nation. It was a game full of successes for Wales. \"I hope that the leaders within the union, who until now have only created misery, show the vision to move the nation forward.\"The WRU has been approached for comment.Davies - who sat on the Sir Tasker Watkins committee to reform Welsh rugby in the early 2000s - was not at the Principality Stadium, choosing instead to watch with his family at home.Like many of his fellow former team-mates, the iconic winger watched on as the current generation achieved a feat they themselves could not manage in the red shirt of Wales. Davies did, however, beat the All Blacks with the Barbarians and as a member of the Lions squad in 1971, and even crossed to score in the second Test victory in Wellington.The 81-year-old, who won 46 caps for his country between 1966-78, was full of pride as he took in a display of attacking intent and defensive endeavour, even if there was a hint of jealousy. \"First and foremost, you have to congratulate the coaches and players for what they delivered on Saturday,\" said Davies.\"You have to admit, some of us are jealous of what they delivered on Saturday, but totally in support of what happened.\"Wales have broken the hoodoo at last after 73 years and what we saw on Saturday certainly shows the way forward for Wales. \"It showed that we can play quickly, that we can stand up physically to the All Blacks and that we had the confidence to deliver.\"It has lifted the nation's hearts. Not just us as individuals, but as a nation. We all feel pride towards the victory.\"Wales have the chance to back up their memorable win when they host Australia on Saturday.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T12:38:29.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/2ae3/live/e3b42440-e1b5-11f1-9a3f-99aca912798e.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/rugby-union/articles/c65y1vlpjdeno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-16-freshworks-alums-raise-53m-to-take-on-salesforce-hubspot-wit",
      "title": "Freshworks alums raise $53M to take on Salesforce, HubSpot with AI-native CRM",
      "summary": "Dreamteam has signed up more than 100 customers across 13 countries, with some businesses switching from established CRMs.",
      "body": "Dreamteam, a startup founded by former Freshworks executives, has raised $53 million to challenge Salesforce and HubSpot with its AI-native CRM, betting that AI can finally shake up a market where established players have so far proved difficult to dislodge.\nThe funding comprises seed and Series A rounds, led by Together Fund and Accel respectively, with participation from Premji Invest. The San Francisco-based startup declined to disclose the size of each round or its valuation.\nTraditional CRMs depend on salespeople to manually record customer interactions and keep deal information up to date. That often leaves businesses with incomplete or outdated information. Dreamteam aims to eliminate much of that work by automatically capturing context from sources including emails and customer conversations. It also uses AI agents to handle tasks such as follow-ups, meeting preparation, and pipeline monitoring.\nDreamteam co-founder and CEO Anand Venkatraman (pictured above, right) told TechCrunch that he had repeatedly encountered problems with traditional CRM platforms over more than 25 years in enterprise software, including at Salesforce and Freshworks. Salespeople spent considerable time updating records, yet managers still struggled to get a reliable picture of their business. “In all these years, I have never come across an instance where I could click a button and get a forecast from the CRM,” he said.\nThat experience led Venkatraman to co-found Dreamteam in 2025 with Senthil Kanthaswamy (pictured above, center), who spent more than nine years at Freshworks, most recently as a product leader at Freshservice, and Ramesh Parthasarathy (pictured above, left), who previously worked on AI and platform engineering at Freshworks.\nAt the heart of Dreamteam’s CRM is what it calls the Company Brain. This acts as virtual memory, pulling together information from emails, meetings, and other customer interactions to build a shared record of customer relationships. Four built-in AI agents use that information to research prospects, draft follow-up emails, flag potential risks to deals, and configure CRM workflows, with sales teams retaining control over what gets sent to customers.\nDreamteam has signed up more than 100 customers across 13 countries in the three months since it began actively selling its product, with most paying for the service, Venkatraman said. The startup has also replaced established CRM platforms at several businesses, including Salesforce at healthcare company DMS Health and HubSpot at other customers.\nThe advertising sales team at media streaming company Plex also chose Dreamteam over HubSpot and other AI-native CRM alternatives, Venkatraman told TechCrunch. Plex’s co-founder and chief product officer Scott Olechowski said Dreamteam’s ability to integrate with AI tools already used across the company was a “game changer.”\nReplacing established CRM platforms at scale may prove more difficult. In a blog post earlier this year, venture capital firm Andreessen Horowitz (a16z) argued that while AI could transform how businesses manage customer relationships, existing CRMs are likely to remain the systems where customer records are stored, with a new generation of AI-powered tools capturing much of the value by working on top of them.\nVenkatraman acknowledged that Salesforce and HubSpot are unlikely to disappear anytime soon, but noted that their entrenched positions do not guarantee they will dominate the next generation of CRM software.\n“We see this as an electric-car moment for systems of record,” he said, adding that AI requires a fundamental rethink of how CRMs are built — not simply adding new features to existing platforms.\nKanthaswamy believes startups have a window of two to five years to gain ground before established CRM providers can fully adapt to AI. Rebuilding software around a fundamentally different architecture takes time, he stated, giving newer companies an opportunity to attract customers while incumbents work to overhaul their existing products.\nDreamteam also plans to expand beyond sales into other parts of the customer lifecycle, including onboarding, customer support, and upselling. The startup already has a feature called Magic Apps that allows users to create custom applications and dashboards within the CRM using natural-language instructions, without writing code.\nCurrently, Dreamteam employs more than 35 people, with its engineering team based in Chennai and its go-to-market operations in the United States. Kanthaswamy said the startup aims to reach eight figures in annual revenue by the end of 2027, though he declined to disclose the startup’s current revenue.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nJagmeet covers startups, tech policy-related updates, and all other major tech-centric developments from India for TechCrunch. He previously worked as a principal correspondent at NDTV.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Jagmeet by emailing [email protected].",
