      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-man-sentenced-for-attempted-murder-of-three-children-that-sp",
      "title": "Man sentenced for attempted murder of three children that sparked Dublin riots",
      "summary": "Riad Bouchaker was sentenced after the attempted murder of three children in November 2023.",
      "body": "The attack took place outside Gaelscoil Choláiste Mhuire in Parnell Square, shortly after 13:40 local time on 23 November.A group of 13 children, aged five and six, were lined up on Parnell Square waiting to go to their afterschool provision. Childcare worker Leanne Flynn was helping a little boy zip up his jacket when Bouchaker pulled his knife on the children.A five-year-old girl was left with a lifelong brain injury after being stabbed in the heart.Now eight, she is non-verbal and can only communicate by blinking, uses a wheelchair and has to be fed through a tube in her stomach as she cannot swallow safely by herself.The judge said the child will never live independently and that her life, and the lives of her family, had been permanently altered.He also noted that Flynn suffered life-threatening injuries, including a collapsed lung and the loss of a spleen, as well as profound psychological consequences and her services not being available to children.The judge said she had acted fearlessly to protect the children and hoped she could return to work in the future, but said the possibility of such remains an unanswered question.Speaking outside court Garda (Irish police) Det Supt Colm Murphy acknowledged the actions of those \"who immediately reacted the to the situation as it unfolded, in particular to Leanne Flynn – her actions on the day saved lives\".Murphy, who was the senior investigating officer in charge added: \"To those who intervened and stopped the attack again, their actions saved lives\".He also gave praise to medical personnel on the ground and hospital staff, saying that they too \"saved lives\".Murphy also acknowledged the principal of Gaelscoil Choláiste Mhuire, \"for the way he and the staff at the school reacted on the day\", and also to the parents who had to endure such a traumatic incident\".Judge Hunt said this was an unprecedented, highly unusual case involving a sudden, ferocious, sustained attack on young children and their childcare worker.He said he had to mark the exceptional gravity of the offending against very young children and their carer, with a lethal weapon leading to consequences of the utmost gravity.He said the circumstances were uniquely horrific and marked a profound assault on the most fundamental values protected by the criminal law.The judge said the children happened to be selected because they were there and he was not yet sure what inference could be drawn from how Bouchaker found himself at their collection point.At an earlier hearing, the court heard he received brain surgery in 2021 and a portion of his skull was removed, leaving a part of his brain vulnerable.He suffered a head injury when members of the public intervened during the Parnell Square attack, after which he received hospital treatment for a month.His defence team, which the judge ordered cannot be named due to safety concerns, said Bouchaker's cognitive function, memory, concentration, communication and decision-making were affected as a result of the impairment.",
