      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-i-survived-a-fire-that-killed-my-boyfriend-it-was-started-by",
      "title": "I survived a fire that killed my boyfriend - it was started by his brother",
      "summary": "Cameron Logan was killed and Rebecca Williams suffered serious injuries after Blair Logan set the bed they were sleeping on alight.",
      "body": "Figure caption, Bex Williams has told her story nearly 10 years after she was almost killedByMegan BonarBBC Scotland After spending the night at a Hogmanay party, Cameron Logan and his girlfriend Bex Williams returned to his family home, in a quiet suburb of Glasgow, in the early hours of New Year's Day 2017.The young couple fell asleep on an inflatable mattress in the living room.Within hours, Cameron would be dead and Bex critically injured.The couple were attacked by Cameron's older brother, who poured petrol over them and set fire to their bed.Blair Logan was jailed after admitting murder and attempted murder.Nearly a decade later, Bex is telling her story for the first time as part of BBC Scotland's Murder Case series.Warning this article contains graphic images.Bex was 24 and had recently finished university and started what she described as her \"dream career\" in radio.\"I loved my job. I loved radio reporting and news reading,\" she added.\"I had dreams of becoming a foreign correspondent at a big organisation like the BBC.\"But then came the fire.\"My life completely changed in those 10 seconds,\" she told BBC Scotland.\"I was just a normal young woman in a new relationship.\"My main worries were what I was going to wear and if I could afford that extra glass of wine.\"Instead, Bex woke up in intensive care days after the attack. She was on a ventilator and doctors were unsure if she would survive.Image source, Phill WilliamsImage caption, Bex suffered severe burns as well as internal injuriesShe was left with severe burns to her face and hands, and internal damage to her throat and lungs. Bex had a breathing tube put into her windpipe and underwent 10 surgeries.But it was damage to her vocal cords that ultimately forced her to give up her radio job. The damage to her voice is still apparent today.Bex says she has decided to speak about the attack now to highlight the trauma she and her family went through - and make sure Cameron is remembered.At the time of the attack the young couple were in the process of \"building a life\"  and had just moved in together. Cameron, 23, was at university and had ambitions of becoming a police detective.Bex said: \"There isn't a day that goes by that I don't think about Cameron, sometimes it is happy memories... sometimes it is not.\"I still can't really process what happened to him. \"It is horrific. When you actually think about what he would have experienced in his final moments.\"Image source, Bex WilliamsImage caption, Bex and Cameron were in the process of moving in togetherThe attack followed years of tension between the brothers.The High Court in Glasgow heard that Blair Logan, then 26, had barely spoken to Cameron since their grandmother's death in 2013.In the weeks before the fire, he had stored petrol in his bedroom and carried out internet searches relating to burn injuries.On the morning of the fire, Cameron's mother, Catherine, was woken shortly after 07:00 by the family dog. When she went downstairs, she saw a figure dressed in dark clothing in the living room.Moments later, it was engulfed in flames. Bex managed to crawl into the kitchen, where she put her head inside the freezer to protect herself from the heat.Cameron's father and a neighbour helped rescue her, but Cameron could not be saved. The family dog, Gomez, also died in the fire.Murder Case: Fire in the SuburbsBex Williams tells her story fully for the first time.Episode one will be shown on BBC Scotland at 21:00 on Tuesday 17 November, then available on BBC iPlayer.Blair told police he was out running when the fire started, but was arrested two weeks later after he could not be found on CCTV on his supposed route.Officers also found traces of petrol fumes on his clothes and a blue medical glove in his pocket, which he claimed he wore while running. Initially, he admitted pouring petrol over the couple but claimed he had not intended to kill his brother - only \"maim him\".In July 2017, he pleaded guilty to murder and attempted murder.He was sentenced to life imprisonment, with a minimum term of 20 years.Image source, PAImage caption, Blair Logan started the fire at his own family home in Milngavie The consequences of the attack extended far beyond the injuries Bex suffered that morning. She has worked hard to get over the \"pain, trauma and anger\" she was left with. She abandoned her ambitions of building a career in broadcasting and is now employed by Police Scotland, in a role that sees her working with family liaison officers and some of the officers involved in her case.She says her job change is a fitting tribute to Cameron, who himself wanted to join the force.\"I know I can never bring Cameron back, but what happened to us has to mean something. So if I can help other people, that is something,\" she added.Bex continues to rebuild her life. Her career has taken a different direction and she has found happiness with a new partner. What has changed most, nearly 10 years on, is her perspective on what matters.\"I am still very career driven; my job is very important to me - but some things just feel less important now, like what am I going to wear. \"Can I afford that glass of wine? Well, tell you what, I'm going to have that glass of wine,\" she said.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T10:48:53.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/e13a/live/38d22770-e1ab-11f1-8ee3-23d0d09d2796.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cw78pgrpnkm1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-yellow-weather-warnings-issued-for-snow-as-temperatures-plum",
      "title": "Yellow weather warnings issued for snow as temperatures plummet",
      "summary": "A spell of colder, wintry weather will grip some northern parts of the UK, bringing a threat of ice and hill snow at times.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesBySarah Keith-LucasLead Weather PresenterA spell of cold and wintry weather is set to grip northern areas of the UK early this week, bringing the threat of ice and hill snow.A yellow weather warning for snow comes into effect in the very early hours of Tuesday following a blast of Arctic air pushing across the UK causing temperatures to plummet.Cold health alerts for parts of northern England also remain in force from the UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) until 09:00 GMT on Thursday.Some snow is forecast over the hills and mountains in the north over the coming days but the wintry chill will be fairly short-lived in the south, where temperatures will rebound to above average by midweek.Coldest night of season so far recordedMonday morning started on a chilly note for all parts. The coldest conditions were seen across the northern half of the UK with northern England, Scotland and Northern Ireland waking up to a sharp frost with some wintry flurries over the hills.Tulloch Bridge in Scotland recorded an overnight low of -7.7C making it the coldest night of the season so far.Temperatures in the south generally remained a few degrees above freezing.Cold health alerts, issued by the UKHSA, are currently in force across north-west England, north-east England, Yorkshire and the Humber until 09:00 GMT on Thursday.These alerts are only issued in England and are aimed at health and social care professionals. A Met Office weather warning for ice from Sunday evening into Monday morning across northern and eastern Scotland as well as parts of Cumbria, Northumberland and Durham has now expired.Image source, BBC WeatherThough Monday will be dry and bright for many places, wintry showers will affect some eastern parts of England and Scotland, with these falling as sleet or snow over high ground.Afternoon maximum temperatures will be a chilly 4-8C in most places - a few degrees below the seasonal average.Later in the day, the next front will approach from the south-west bringing milder air and some heavy and persistent rain.As this band of rain then moves northwards overnight into Tuesday, a further spell of hill snow may develop.Met Office warnings for snow on TuesdayImage source, BBC WeatherImage caption, Areas covered by yellow wetaher warning for snow in place from 00:01 to 11:00 on TuesdayA yellow weather warning for snow is set to come into effect on Tuesday from midnight until 11:00 GMT, covering parts of the Midlands, north-east and north-west England, North Yorkshire and south-west Scotland.Where snow does accumulate, depths of 1-5cm are likely, although some locations above 300-400m could see 5-15cm. Some roads and railways are likely to be affected, resulting in longer travel times by road, bus and train services. Power cuts are also possible.Further south, Tuesday will be a very different day with blustery conditions and some heavy showers. This battleground of colder, drier conditions in the north and milder, wetter weather in the south will continue into the middle of the week.You can keep up to date with your local forecast by checking BBC Weather online and on the app.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T10:02:04.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_weather/1200/cpsprodpb/6f1b/live/2225a470-e133-11f1-a204-d95dd4dbb76b.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/weather/articles/c6y97j2xq531o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-avoid-this-black-friday-scam-how-to-check-it-s-a-real-deal",
      "title": "Avoid this Black Friday scam - how to check it's a real deal",
      "summary": "Sales and the looming tax deadline mean more scams - here's how to make sure you don't get caught out.",
      "body": "If you are self-employed or have more than one source of income you will need to file a tax return by the end of January.Fraudsters seize on this deadline to blitz people with messages that look like they are official and from the UK's tax authority, HMRC.Any such calls or messages should be ignored, but you can also check whether an email, external, text message, external or phone call, external claiming to be from HMRC is genuine.\"Reporting scams helps us shut them down and protect others,\" adds Lucy Pike, HMRC's chief security officer.New figures from HMRC, shared with the BBC, show that it received 95,340 reports of suspected scams in the year to the end of October.There were 900 reports of one specific scam which used emails containing a malicious attachment which directed customers to a fake gov.uk webpage designed to steal personal and financial information.To avoid being caught out, HMRC says:it never contacts people by email, text or phone to tell them they are due a refund or ask them to claim oneit never asks for personal or financial information by email or text messageit never leaves threatening voicemails about legal action or arrest",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T09:58:52.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/4f34/live/a04a1d00-e1c3-11f1-99b6-a35a62fa4264.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm4gnlz2p61ko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-you-re-going-to-win-the-war-harry-praises-war-veterans-on-su",
      "title": "'You're going to win the war': Harry praises war veterans on surprise Ukraine visit",
      "summary": "He has been showing support for injured veterans in Ukraine and praising the country's resilience.",
      "body": "'You're going to win the war': Harry praises war veterans on surprise Ukraine visitFigure caption, Watch: Prince Harry meets veterans on surprise visit to LvivByRoyal correspondentReporting fromLviv, UkraineThe Duke of Sussex has received an enthusiastic welcome in a surprise visit to Ukraine to support injured war veterans, meeting Ukrainian competitors taking part in his Invictus Games.There were emotional scenes as he was applauded by injured service men and women in Lviv, where he told his audience: \"You're going to win the war.\"Prince Harry spent two days at events for veterans, with a press blackout for security reasons until he had left Ukraine, as the country continues to face missile attacks from Russia.Ukraine has more than 150,000 injured war veterans and the prince visited projects supporting their treatment and rehabilitation.Image source, Press AssociationImage caption, Prince Harry watched a wheelchair rugby match taking place inside a noise sports centre as part of his trip\"Ukraine now is experiencing elements of war that no other country has experienced before,\" he said - with technology advancing in areas such as drone warfare, but also in protecting cities and providing treatment.A mobile phone app is where Ukrainians look for air raid warnings, telling them the direction, timing and type of any incoming missiles.At the Superhumans Center near Lviv, Harry was shown how growing numbers of injured veterans receive groundbreaking treatment.International experts used to come to help Ukraine, he said, but \"now that has flipped,\" with Ukraine having the greater expertise with trauma injuries to share with others.Image caption, Mykhailo Damaskin, pictured with his dog Stitch, has received experimental treatment to walkThat included an innovation for Mykhailo Damaskin, a Ukrainian air assault forces veteran, who was so seriously injured in fighting in Kherson he was never expected to walk again.In an experimental treatment he had a spinal cord stimulator implanted in his back, which allows him to walk, but has to be re-charged every 12 hours. He cannot feel his feet and controls his steps using his eyes.He needs to be plugged in and charged up to be able to move - and jokes that he is \"like a demo model of a Tesla\".\"My goal is to go to the Invictus Games. I promised my father before he was killed that I'd be part of the team to represent Ukraine internationally.\"I'll prove to every guy with spinal cord injuries that life goes on after injury,\" said Mykhailo.Image source, Press AssociationImage caption, Prince Harry met Kostiantyn Vlasenko, who was airlifted by a drone from the frontAt the Superhumans Center Prince Harry sat at the bedside of Konstiantyn Vlasenko, who was airlifted from the front line by a drone last month in a rescue that was a first of its kind.He had been alone for more than 200 days and had stayed at his position, despite losing both his feet.In a moving encounter, Prince Harry told him he represented the spirit of resilience of Ukraine.And he described the injured veterans as \"role models\", promising his audience: \"The rest of the world will continue to support you.\"Along with all the determination, it was an inescapably poignant sight too, with so many families and children at the medical facility visiting the growing numbers of badly injured war casualties.Image caption, Injured veterans took part in a game of wheelchair rugbyOlga Rudeva, chief executive of the SuperHumans Center, said it is seeing more injured civilians than ever because of \"changes to the frontlines\".\"The Russians are doing double taps, they are targeting civilian infrastructure,\" she told BBC Radio 4's Today programme.As a result, \"it's not only about [an] increased number of patients, it's about increased complexity of new cases.\"Russia has repeatedly denied it aims for civilian targets.Image caption, Blast damage from missiles on an historic building in LvivAt a noisy sports centre in Lviv, Prince Harry arrived to watch a wheelchair rugby match taking place, with some thunderous collisions between the players.He was welcomed by the competitors in what he has called his Invictus \"family\" and soon the visit turned into something approaching a group hug, with Harry disappearing into a sea of selfies and handshakes.He was presented with a T-shirt remembering prisoners of war held by Russian forces.And he later had a chance to play one of the events at the Invictus Games, a tennis-style competition called pickleball.Image caption, Ivan Voinov, who lost a leg, has learned to dance again with his wifeWatching was Ivan Voinov, who has lost a leg and suffered head injuries, but who is using sport to help his rehabilitation - including learning to dance again.He said that dancing was his way to be together with his wife again, which meant so much to his recovery and recovering his identity.At an Invictus awards ceremony during the visit, Prince Harry was greeted with choruses of \"Harry, Harry, Harry\", getting a rock star welcome.Image caption, A Banksy-style piece of street art in Lviv shows the missiles falling\"Every single time I come back to Ukraine, I feel welcome, I feel warmth, I feel encouragement,\" he said, having returned for a fourth trip here during wartime.He promised that \"when the war is over and when Ukraine wins, we will be ready to support you in holding an Invictus Games\".There have been 400 wounded service men and women in Ukraine taking part in trials for next year's games, being hosted in Birmingham.The sports events are an attempt to encourage rehabilitation and to recognise the resilience of people overcoming their challenges.Prince Harry told a gathering of new Invictus members of a \"cultural shift\" from people being socially awkward about seeing injured veterans to wanting to show respect.\"These people have so much to offer,\" he said.\"Recovery cannot wait for the war to end, The scale of need in Ukraine is huge, and wounded, injured and sick service personnel and veterans need the chance to rebuild their lives now,\" said Rob Owen, chief executive officer of the Invictus Games Foundation.",
