      "id": "bbc-2026-11-16-break-in-and-rising-costs-could-kill-my-business",
      "title": "'Break-in and rising costs could kill my business'",
      "summary": "Gordon Kidd says if things do not improve financially, he fears his firm in Shifnal will go under.",
      "body": "The owner of a cycle shop says he is considering closing the business after a break-in and soaring costs.Gordon Kidd, who runs Shifnal Cycles, estimates that a burglary last month cost him about £20,000 after three customer bikes and a frame were stolen and the subsequent repairs to his shop.Along with rising bills and a \"lack of government support\", the former armed forces veteran said the business in Shifnal may not be viable if financial pressures continue.A government spokesperson said that, from next year, businesses such as cycle shops would see their business rates either frozen, reduced or cut to zero, with half of the high-street retailers paying no rates.West Mercia Police said it had received a report of a burglary on 31 October, and an investigation was ongoing.\"They stole customers' bikes, which, for me, as a shop owner, is my biggest fear,\" said Kidd, who was concerned the burglary could affect his reputation.\"That might put them [customers] off from using my shop, and that kills a shop.\"He added: \"I've got to do what's financially viable for the business.\"I've got to look at another avenue now, and I've got to look at a more cost-effective way of doing it.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-16T05:57:19.000Z",
