      "id": "bbc-2026-11-15-who-swaggered-around-like-he-owned-the-place-spain-v-england",
      "title": "Who swaggered around like he owned the place? Spain v England ratings",
      "summary": "Spanish football reporter Elizabeth Conway and senior football correspondent Sami Mokbel give their player ratings for Spain v England.",
      "body": "Starting XI:Unai Simon: Made some brilliant saves early in the first half but still conceded three. 7Marc Pubill: Had a bright 20 minutes against Anthony Gordon until he came off injured. 6Pau Cubarsi: Gave away the penalty that Harry Kane scored for England's opener. 5Aymeric Laporte: Always under pressure against Kane but came into the game more as the England striker dropped deeper after the sending off. 7Marc Cucarella: Tried to get under Morgan Rogers' skin and kept him fairly quiet. 6 Martin Zubimendi: Made some decent challenges. 6Fabian Ruiz: Made little impact in midfield before he came off at half-time. 5 Mikel Merino: The Arsenal midfielder was ineffective and quiet for the first half - and then taken off at half-time. 5Lamine Yamal: Lewis Hall and Jarell Quansah has to double up to contain him. Moments of brilliance throughout. 8 Alex Baena: The Atletico Madrid forward made some decent runs into the box. 7Mikel Oyarzabal: Scored Spain's penalty to make it 3-1. Relatively quiet other than that. 7Substitutes:Pedro Porro (on 20, Pubill): Worked hard against Gordon. Had a great chance against James Trafford. 7Dean Huijsen (on 46, Cubarsi): Struggled initially against Kane but coped better after Elliot Anderson was sent off. 6Pedri (on 46, Fabien Ruiz): Constructive and rarely wasted a pass. 8Fermin Lopez (on 46, Merino) Very good and linked up with Yamal well. 8Nico Williams (on 71, Zubimendi) Made an immediate impact with a goal and constantly a threat. 8Subs not used: Barrios, Garcia, Munoz, Pino, Raya, Roca, TorresStarting XI:James Trafford: Had little to do until Quansah's error led to Oyarzabal's successful penalty but made crucial saves in closing stages to see England home. 8Ben White: That is one way to silence the boo-boys! Marked his first international start since March 2022 with a brilliant all-round display and the eventual winner. 8Ezri Konsa: Returned to the side after missing Croatia game through injury and proved just how important he has become. Colossal. 8Jarell Quansah: Was having a good night until making the mistake that led to Spain's opener from the spot and Anderson's red card. 5Lewis Hall: Growing in stature at international level. Excelled in the attacking third and battled well against Yamal. 8Elliot Anderson: Utterly brilliant until being shown a red card in trying to clear up Quansah's mess. Desperately unlucky. 8Declan Rice: Organised and drove the team forward - all while on an early yellow card. Delivered the corner for England's second. 8Jude Bellingham: In his adopted city, the Real Madrid midfielder swaggered around like he owned the place. Oozed quality. 9Morgan Rogers: A disciplined display from the Chelsea forward and still created Spain problems in forward areas. 8Anthony Gordon: A nuisance for Spain in every area of the field but dalliance in possession led to host's second to set up anxious finish. 6Harry Kane: Won and dispatched England's penalty and Spain could not live with his intelligence at times. Sacrificed following Anderson's red card. 7Substitutes:Myles Lewis-Skelly (on 78, Kane). 6Dominic Calvert-Lewin (on 84, Gordon)Djed Spence (on 84, Bellingham)Dan Burn (on 90, White)Subs not used: Pickford, Steele, Hill, Alexander-Arnold, Gibbs-White, Jobe Bellingham, Eze, Ngumoha.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-15T22:12:55.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/8e3c/live/1664df80-e132-11f1-a204-d95dd4dbb76b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cjg46lrpllrpo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-15-old-bitly-links-are-being-hijacked",
      "title": "Old Bitly links are being hijacked",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://ransomnews.com/burger-king-tweet-bitly-link-hijack-2026/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50511395\nPoints: 330\n# Comments: 165",
      "body": "Update, 15 November, 23:40 UTC: CNN reports redirected Bitly links in old posts from Bill Gates, Justin Trudeau, Mike Pence, the FBI and the US Department of Defense as well. Our third check of the Trump links, at 23:35 UTC, found nothing new: 49 links re-created years after their tweets still work.\nUpdate, 15 November, 22:30 UTC: Bitly has confirmed the problem. In a post on X at 19:13 UTC it said it had identified an issue that “allowed bad actors to repurpose certain old, inactive links” and send people to unrelated content. Bitly said it has suspended the accounts involved and is disabling the affected links. It added that its Trust and Safety team is working on protections against similar misuse.\nEarlier today, we identified an issue that allowed bad actors to repurpose certain old, inactive links to redirect people to content unrelated to the original posts. We have suspended the accounts involved and are disabling the affected links. Our Trust and Safety team is working…— Bitly (@Bitly) November 15, 2026\nUpdate, 15 November, 19:30 UTC: The Burger King and McDonald’s links no longer redirect. Both were still working at 15:05 UTC and returned Bitly’s “Link Deactivated” page by 19:22 UTC. The same has happened to the two extra Trump links we verified and to six of the seven links in our sample that pointed somewhere new. The seventh now shows a Bitly warning that the account that created it has been suspended. The 49 re-created links that look like the originals still work.\nBurger King’s X account was not hacked. A Bitly short link in a tweet the chain posted on 24 July 2011 was registered again by someone else on 15 November 2026 and pointed at Wikipedia’s list of Nazi concentration camps. The tweet was advertising a restaurant locator. A post quoting it passed 2 million views within hours, and Burger King’s original has since become unavailable.\nIt was not the only one. Eighteen minutes earlier a Bitly link in a McDonald’s tweet from 7 June 2011 was registered the same way and pointed at Wikipedia’s article on the September 11 attacks. Ransomnews also checked 97 Bitly links in Donald Trump’s tweets from 2009 to 2013, taken from a public archive. Bitly’s own link pages show that 56 were created after the tweets they sit in, and a 57th is blocked by Bitly as malicious or spam. Four were registered on 15 November. Two more, created in January 2024 and January 2025, point at domains that copy the names of crypto services.\nBurger King posted the tweet on 24 July 2011 with the short link bit[.]ly/19BFiM. Bitly’s information page for that link gives a creation time of 05:32 UTC on 15 November 2026 and a destination on Wikipedia.A link in a McDonald’s tweet from 7 June 2011, bit[.]ly/mLWJO0, was created again at 05:14 UTC the same morning, 18 minutes before Burger King’s. It opens Wikipedia’s article on the September 11 attacks. Both tweets have since become unavailable.Nobody posted from either company’s account. The links changed hands on Bitly’s side, so the old tweets send readers wherever the new owners choose.Of 97 Bitly links in Donald Trump’s tweets from 2009 to 2013, 40 are still the original. Another 49 were created again later but lead where the tweets suggest, 48 of them in a 17-minute run on 26 May 2023. Seven lead somewhere else and one is blocked by Bitly.Four of those seven were registered on 15 November 2026 between 07:49 and 09:57 UTC. They open a Roblox profile, a YouTube video (twice) and a Wikipedia article about Trump and Jeffrey Epstein.One Trump tweet from April 2011 carries three links to videos of his children. All three have been replaced.Bitly’s help pages say the ending of a bit.ly link cannot be reused, even after the account behind it is deleted. In February 2025 it was reported to have fixed a bug that allowed this.We found no public statement from Bitly, Burger King or McDonald’s by 14:30 UTC on 15 November.By 19:22 UTC both links returned Bitly’s “Link Deactivated” page. None of the seven Trump links that pointed somewhere new redirects any more.Bitly confirmed the issue on X at 19:13 UTC. It said it has suspended the accounts involved and is disabling the affected links.CNN reported the same evening that old posts from Bill Gates, Justin Trudeau, Mike Pence, the FBI and the US Department of Defense were affected too. We have not checked those links ourselves.\nNo. Nothing was posted from Burger King’s account and nothing suggests anyone got into it. The tweet is 15 years old and reads as it always did, pointing followers to “our handy restaurant locator” through a Bitly link. What changed is where that link goes. As with the Microsoft 365 outage three days earlier, the first question people asked was whether it was an attack on the company, and the answer is the same.\nBitly keeps a public information page for every short link. Add a plus sign to the end of the address and it shows where the link points and when it was created. For bit[.]ly/19BFiM that page gives the destination as Wikipedia’s “List of Nazi concentration camps” and the creation time as 15 November 2026, 05:32 UTC. A link printed in a tweet from 2011 can only carry a 2026 creation date if the same ending was issued a second time.\nWe tested the method on links nobody disputes. A link whose creator says he made it in 2009 shows a 2009 creation date. A custom link tweeted by a YouTube channel on 21 June 2017 shows 20 June 2017. Among the Trump links that are still original, 32 of 40 were created on the day of the tweet or the day before.\nBurger King’s 2011 tweet as it appeared on the morning of 15 November 2026, quoted by another user. Screenshot: X.\nThe post that spread it came from an X user at 06:51 UTC, 79 minutes after the link was created, and had 2.2 million views by early afternoon.\nBy 13:45 UTC the Burger King post inside it was showing as unavailable. We do not know whether Burger King removed it or X did. The short link was still redirecting to Wikipedia at 13:47 UTC.\nNo. Its link was changed 18 minutes before Burger King’s. On 7 June 2011 McDonald’s tweeted a Bitly link to something its team in Sweden had done. Bitly’s information page for bit[.]ly/mLWJO0 shows the link was created at 05:14 UTC on 15 November 2026, and it redirects to Wikipedia’s article on the September 11 attacks. The tweet was returning a not-found error when we checked at 14:20 UTC.\nThe two changes are close in time and alike in style. The X user Alex Greenland, who spotted both timestamps, thinks one person or group is probably behind them. Nothing we have seen proves that.\nThis Burger King Bitly link was changed at 05:32 UK time today (15 Nov 2026), which is very close to the change of McDonald's tweet Bitly link at 05:14 today.This means it is likely the same actor or group of actors is making these changes. https://t.co/2gqoms5veu pic.twitter.com/u4OxXaPkn2— Alex Greenland (@ajrgd) November 15, 2026\nHow does an old Bitly link get hijacked?\nSomeone registers its ending again. The part of a Bitly link after the slash is called the back-half. Bitly generates one at random, or the user types a custom one. If an old link stops existing and its back-half can be claimed, whoever claims it decides where every tweet, email, PDF and printed QR code carrying that link now leads. No password is stolen and no account is touched, so the brand that posted the link sees nothing wrong.\nBitly says this cannot happen. Its help page on deleted accounts states that bit.ly links keep working and that their back-halves “cannot be re-used”, whether they were generated or custom. A separate page says Bitly deletes accounts that have been inactive for three years. The creation dates we collected do not fit the first statement. Back-halves that appear in tweets from 2009 to 2012 belong to links created between 2023 and today.\nIt has happened before. In February 2025 Disruptionist reported that memecoin promoters had taken over Bitly links in 2013 posts by Donald Trump and Melania Trump and pointed them at token listings. According to that report, a bug let people claim a back-half once the original link or its account had been deleted or deactivated, and Bitly fixed it and switched off the hijacked links. Links created on 15 November 2026 show the same thing is possible now.\nThe X user Damin Toell identified it as link hijacking at 07:25 UTC and noted that old Trump posts were affected too.\nAt 09:18 UTC another user described the method in a post that has been viewed more than 46,000 times. Three more Trump links were registered in the 40 minutes that followed.\nHow does your own site score?\nRun the same forty passive checks against your own domain: TLS and certificates, security headers, SPF and DMARC, cookies before consent, and what your stack quietly reveals. A grade out of 100 in about fifteen seconds.\nNo signup. Nothing installed. We only request what your site already serves publicly.\nHow many old links have been replaced?\nMore than half of the ones we tested. We took every tweet Donald Trump’s account posted from 2009 to 2016 from the Trump Twitter Archive data set on GitHub, left out retweets and extracted the bit.ly links. That gave 97 different links, first used between 2009 and 2013, with 61 of them from 2011. We chose this account because its old posts were named in the reports of 15 November and in the 2025 case, and because a full public archive exists. For each link we recorded where it redirects and the creation date on Bitly’s information page, at about 13:45 UTC on 15 November 2026.\n57 of 97 Bitly links in old Trump tweets are no longer the original\nBitly links in @realDonaldTrump tweets from 2009 to 2013, by what the link is today. Checked on 15 November 2026.\nCreated again, leads where the tweet suggests49\nCreated again, leads somewhere else7\nSource: Ransomnews check of Bitly link information pages. Tweets from the Trump Twitter Archive data set.\nForty links are untouched. Forty-nine were created again later and lead to addresses that fit the tweets: mostly videos on Trump’s YouTube channel, plus pages at CNBC, CNN and the Wall Street Journal. Forty-eight of those were created between 14:55 and 15:12 UTC on 26 May 2023, roughly one every 20 seconds. We do not know who did that. It looks like a repair job by someone who knew where each link used to go, and it means those back-halves were free to claim by May 2023.\nThat leaves eight links that now lead somewhere their tweets never pointed.\nTweet dateWhat the tweet promotedLink created againWhere it goes now3 Jun 2009An iGoogle page15 Nov 2026, 07:49 UTCA Roblox user profile1 Jul 2010A golf course in Scotland15 Nov 2026, 09:36 UTCA YouTube video17 Sep 2010A QVC jewellery show15 Nov 2026, 09:47 UTCThe same YouTube video7 Oct 2010A golf course in Scotland15 Nov 2026, 09:57 UTCWikipedia’s article on Trump and Jeffrey Epstein14 Mar 2011A video blog29 Jan 2025, 21:09 UTCtest[.]com25 Apr 2011A video of Donald Trump Jr.18 Jan 2025, 21:43 UTCworldlibertyfinancial-ai[.]com25 Apr 2011A video of Eric Trump17 Jan 2024, 11:08 UTCdappradars[.]website25 Apr 2011A video of Ivanka TrumpNot shownBlocked by Bitly as malicious or spamCreation times are from Bitly’s information pages, read on 15 November 2026. Domains are written with brackets so they cannot be clicked.\nOne tweet, three replaced links\nOn 25 April 2011 the account posted three Bitly links to “The Next Generation” videos of Donald Trump Jr., Ivanka Trump and Eric Trump. The first now leads to worldlibertyfinancial-ai[.]com, a domain that borrows the name of World Liberty Financial, the Trump family’s crypto venture. The third leads to dappradars[.]website, one letter away from the crypto analytics site DappRadar, at a path named “eligibility/check”. Bitly blocks the second with a notice that it was “flagged as redirecting to malicious or spam content”.\nWHOIS records from WhoisXML API show both domains were registered afresh after the links were created. The current registration of dappradars[.]website began at 02:56 UTC on 15 November 2026, through Hostinger, although the Bitly link that points there was created in January 2024. The domain now forwards to a YouTube video. The current registration of worldlibertyfinancial-ai[.]com dates from 11 April 2026, through Namecheap, almost 15 months after the link was created, and its HTTPS certificate failed verification when we checked. We have not seen what either domain served when the links were first made.\nA replaced link can be replaced again. When the domain behind it lapses, the next buyer inherits whatever traffic the old tweet still sends.\nMore Trump links outside our 97\nX users found replaced Bitly links in Trump tweets from 2012 and 2013 that the archive stores only as t.co addresses. We checked two. The link in a tweet of 10 January 2013 was created at 23:52 UTC on 14 November 2026 and opens a page on Steam. The link in a tweet of 10 September 2012 was created at 01:04 UTC on 15 November and opens a YouTube video. Late on 14 November is the earliest change in this wave that we have seen.\nWhich re-created links still work?\nWe ran the same check on the 97 Trump links twice more, at 19:25 and 23:35 UTC on 15 November. The seven links that pointed somewhere new no longer redirect. The other 49 links created years after their tweets still do: 48 were made within 17 minutes of each other on 26 May 2023 and one in February 2025. They point to what look like the original pages, mostly YouTube videos and news articles. We do not know who re-created them or why. The 40 original links work as before.\nWe used the archive’s condensed yearly files for 2009 to 2016 and found bit.ly links only in tweets up to 2013. From late 2011 Twitter wrapped links in its own t.co shortener, and the archive stores most later links in that form (1,218 of them in 2012), so Bitly links behind those are not among our 97. A link counts as created again when Bitly’s information page shows a creation date more than 30 days after the first tweet that used it. In practice the gap was at least 11 years in every case. Destinations were read from the redirect header without opening the pages. The count covers one account and one link shortener. It shows that these links were claimed. How many of Bitly’s older links can be claimed is something only Bitly can answer. Domain registration data is from WhoisXML API.\nCNN reported on the evening of 15 November that old posts from Bill Gates, Justin Trudeau, Mike Pence, the FBI and the US Department of Defense carried redirected Bitly links too, along with a 2013 post from McDonald’s Indonesia account. It counted at least a dozen in Trump’s posts, in a wave that appears limited to posts from 2009 to 2014. Burger King told CNN it had nothing to do with the redirects.\nWe have not checked those links ourselves. Bitly was already disabling affected links when they were reported, and a disabled link no longer shows when it was created. The accounts we verified directly are Burger King, McDonald’s and Donald Trump.\nWhat happened on 15 November 2026?\nThe first change we found came late on 14 November. The McDonald’s and Burger King links followed at 05:14 and 05:32 UTC the next morning. All times below are UTC and on 15 November unless stated.\nHow the Bitly link hijacking unfolded\nSaturday 14 to Sunday 15 November 2026. All times are UTC.\nSaturday 14 November: the earliest change we found\n23:52 Link takenA link in a 2013 Trump tweet is registered and pointed at a page on Steam, the earliest change we found.\nSunday 15 November, morning: the brand links are taken\n01:04 Link takenA link in a 2012 Trump tweet is registered and pointed at a YouTube video.\n02:56 Domaindappradars[.]website, the target of a Trump link replaced in 2024, is registered afresh.\n05:14 Link takenThe back-half in McDonald’s 2011 tweet is registered and pointed at Wikipedia.\n05:32 Link takenThe back-half in Burger King’s 2011 tweet is registered and pointed at Wikipedia.\n06:51 XAn X user quotes the Burger King tweet. The post goes on to pass 2 million views.\n07:25 XAnother user identifies it as link hijacking and says old Trump posts are affected.\n07:49 Link takenA link in a 2009 Trump tweet is registered and pointed at a Roblox profile.\n09:18 XA user describes the method in public.\n09:36 to 09:57 Links takenThree links in 2010 Trump tweets are registered.\nSunday 15 November, afternoon and evening: Bitly responds\n13:45 Burger KingBurger King’s post shows as unavailable. Its link still redirects to Wikipedia.\n14:20 McDonald’sMcDonald’s post returns a not-found error. Its link still redirects to Wikipedia too.\n15:05 Our checkBoth links still redirect to Wikipedia.\n19:13 BitlyBitly says on X that an issue let old, inactive links be repurposed, that it has suspended the accounts involved and that it is disabling the affected links.Why it matters: this is the first confirmation from Bitly that old links could be taken over.\n19:22 Our checkBoth links return Bitly’s “Link Deactivated” page. So do six of the seven Trump links in our sample that pointed somewhere new and the two we verified outside it. The seventh shows a warning that the account that created it has been suspended.\n21:10 CNNCNN reports redirected links in old posts from Bill Gates, Justin Trudeau, Mike Pence, the FBI and the US Department of Defense.\n23:35 Our checkOur third check finds no further change. Forty-nine re-created links in the Trump sample still redirect.\nStatus NowThe Burger King and McDonald’s links no longer redirect, and neither do the Trump links that pointed somewhere new. Forty-nine re-created links in the Trump sample still work. Bitly has not said how many links were taken. Checked at 23:35 UTC on 15 November.\nLink creation times are from Bitly’s information pages and post times are from X. Checks are our own. CNN’s report is timed by its publication. Last checked 23:35 UTC, 15 November 2026.\nWhy does a joke link matter?\nBecause the same move works for fraud. The examples from 15 November are pranks: a Wikipedia page, a game profile, a video. The 2025 wave pushed memecoins, and two of the Trump links in our table lead to domains named after crypto services. A link inside an old post from a verified brand, a bank, a ministry or a news outlet carries that account’s credibility, and X shows the post exactly as it was written. Readers have no reason to doubt it.\nThe exposure is wider than X. The same short links sit in old emails, PDFs, slide decks, printed QR codes and help pages. Security researchers file this under broken link hijacking: a link outlives the thing it pointed to, and someone else moves in.\nWhat should brands and readers do?\nAny organisation that posted short links years ago should assume some of them are no longer its own.\nList the short links in your old posts. Export your post archive from each platform and pull out every bit.ly address.Check each one. Add a plus sign to the link and compare Bitly’s creation date with the date of your post. A later date means the link is no longer yours.Remove what you cannot fix. Delete posts whose links were replaced or no longer resolve, and report replaced links to Bitly.Use a short domain you own for new links, so nobody else can issue the same address.\nFor everyone else, the checks take a few seconds.\nDo not trust a link because the post is old or the account is verified. The account owner may have no control over where it goes.Preview before you click. Adding a plus sign to a bit.ly link shows the destination without visiting it.Be most careful with pages that ask you to sign in or connect a crypto wallet.\nBitly has to say whether old back-halves can still be claimed and what it will do about the ones already taken. We found no public statement from Bitly, Burger King or McDonald’s by 14:30 UTC on 15 November. Later that day the links that pointed somewhere new were deactivated, and 49 others created years after their tweets were left working. Bitly confirmed the issue on X at 19:13 UTC and said it was still disabling affected links. Its statement does not say how many links were taken or how. Checks like this one are what our breach verification desk is for, and we will update this article when either company responds or the numbers move.\nWas Burger King hacked in November 2026?\nNo. Nothing was posted from Burger King’s X account. A Bitly short link in a tweet from 24 July 2011 was registered again by someone else on 15 November 2026, so the old tweet began sending readers to a different page.\nNo. A Bitly link in a McDonald’s tweet from 7 June 2011 was registered again at 05:14 UTC on 15 November 2026 and pointed at Wikipedia’s article on the September 11 attacks. Nothing was posted from the McDonald’s account.\nWhy did a Burger King tweet link to a list of Nazi concentration camps?\nThe tweet’s Bitly link, bit[.]ly/19BFiM, originally advertised a restaurant locator. Bitly’s information page shows the link was created again at 05:32 UTC on 15 November 2026 with a Wikipedia article as its destination. Burger King did not choose that destination.\nWhat is Bitly link hijacking?\nIt is when someone registers the ending of an old Bitly link that no longer exists and points it at a page of their choice. Every old post, email or document that carries the link then leads to the new destination. No account is broken into.\nHow can I tell whether a Bitly link has been replaced?\nAdd a plus sign to the end of the link. Bitly shows the destination and the date the link was created. If the creation date is later than the post or document the link appears in, the link is not the original.\nWere Donald Trump’s old tweets hacked?\nNo. Ransomnews checked 97 Bitly links in tweets from 2009 to 2013. Fifty-six were created again after the tweets were posted and one is blocked by Bitly. Seven of them lead to destinations unrelated to the tweets, including two domains that copy the names of crypto services.\nHas Bitly fixed the problem?\nA fix was reported in February 2025 after links in Trump posts were pointed at memecoin pages. Links created on 15 November 2026 show that old endings can be claimed now. Bitly had made no public statement that we could find by 14:30 UTC that day. It confirmed the issue on X at 19:13 UTC and said it was disabling the affected links. It added that its Trust and Safety team is working on protections against similar misuse.\nWho else was affected by the Bitly link hijacking?\nCNN reported redirected Bitly links in old posts from Bill Gates, Justin Trudeau, Mike Pence, the FBI and the US Department of Defense, as well as Burger King, McDonald’s and Donald Trump. Ransomnews verified the Burger King, McDonald’s and Trump links directly.\nAre the hijacked Bitly links still active?\nThe ones we tracked are not. By 19:22 UTC on 15 November 2026 the Burger King and McDonald’s links, and the Trump links that pointed somewhere new, returned Bitly’s “Link Deactivated” page. Bitly said it was still disabling affected links, so check any old short link before you trust it.\nBitly Help Center: what happens to links after an account is deleted\nBitly Help Center: why an account would be deleted\nDisruptionist: Crypto scammers hacked Trump’s tweets via a bug (3 February 2025)\nTrump Twitter Archive data set on GitHub\nX post quoting the Burger King tweet (15 November 2026)\nX post by Damin Toell on the hijacked links (15 November 2026)\nX post by Alex Greenland on the McDonald’s and Burger King timestamps (15 November 2026)\nAcunetix: What is broken link hijacking\nBitly statement on X (15 November 2026)\nCNN: Trump, McDonald’s and Burger King among targets of past X posts that contain redirected website links (15 November 2026)\nDomain registration records: WhoisXML API.\nRelated reading on Ransomnews: Microsoft 365 outage: was it a cyberattack?\nOne email a month. Original leak-site data, victim census updates, and the findings that did not make the articles. No spam, unsubscribe any time.\nThe Ransomnews Research Team is the collective byline used for collaborative pieces, editorial briefings, and articles drawing on contributions from multiple researchers. Coverage spans ransomware operations, breach economics, threat actor profiling, OSINT methodology, and emerging risks across security, privacy, and AI.",
