      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-14-monkey-island-all-13-versions-side-by-side",
      "title": "Monkey Island: all 13 versions side by side",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://monkeyisland.pages.dev/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50493904\nPoints: 206\n# Comments: 62",
      "body": "[■]Versions, in release order · drag one onto the screen to compare\nEvery screen was captured in ScummVM, except the Atari ST monochrome screens, which come from the Hatari emulator. Each capture was scaled to 1600x1200 with nearest-neighbour pixels and 4:3 aspect correction, so every original pixel keeps the shape it had on a period monitor.\nThe twelve moments are the same in every version, with the same framing, so switching versions changes the machine, not the moment. The PC 16-color screens come from a Spanish copy and the Atari ST mono ones from a French copy; all the others are in English.\nDrag a title bar to move a window. Press Esc to go back to the viewer.\nScummVM source, SCUMM palette codeSega CD shadow mode\nThe Secret of Monkey Island CD VIDEO FIXLinkuei, 2022\nMacTech, Fast Blit StrategiesEPX by Eric Johnston\nAaron GilesLucasArts Mac and Windows ports\nMobyGames, Amiga creditsChris Hülsbeck, Rudolf Stember\nThe Ultimate Oldschool PC Font PackVileR, int10h.org\nDOSBox Staging, CRT shaderscrt-hyllian and the VGA 1080p shaders\nlibretro slang-shaders, crt-guest-advanced-hdguest(r), Dr. Venom: curvature, corners, vignette\nASCII chart · IBM VGA 9x16\nChar:    Decimal:   0  Hex: 00\nPxPlus IBM VGA 9x16 by VileR (int10h.org), CC BY-SA 4.0. Stretched x1.35 here for 4:3, shared under the same licence.\nEmulatorsScummVM, and Hatari for the Atari ST mono screens\nOutput1600 x 1200 pixels per screen\n[X] The same 12 moments in every version\nThe Mac draws a 640x400 picture inside a 640x480 screen, so it shows black bars. The Hercules picture uses the middle 640 of its 720 columns.\nCRT shader · DOSBox Staging\nThe CRT monitors of DOSBox Staging, ported to WebGL2: its presets for Hyllian's CRT shader, and its own VGA shaders where a screen has few pixels. Each version is drawn from its native pixels on the monitor closest to its own, with a whole number of screen pixels for every scanline where the size allows.\nPC, FM Towns31 kHz VGA monitor. Each of the 200 lines is drawn twice: 400 fine scanlines.\nMacThe same kind of monitor, at 480 lines.\nEGA, CGAThe 31 / 15 kHz button swaps the VGA monitor for the card's own 15.7 kHz one: 200 lines.\nAmiga, ST, Sega CD15 kHz monitor or TV, with DOSBox's arcade presets: dark gaps between the 200 lines.\nHercules, ST monoNo mask. Hercules in white, green or amber phosphor.\n[X] On in the viewer: the CRT button under the picture, or R\n[X] Scanlines need pixels: zoom in where the window is too small\n[X] Every setting in the CRT lab, on the menu bar\ncrt-hyllian and its presets: Copyright (C) 2011-2020 Hyllian, Copyright (C) 2020-2024 The DOSBox Staging Team, MIT. VGA 1080p shaders: Copyright (C) 2020-2024 The DOSBox Staging Team, GNU GPL v2 or later. Curvature and corners from crt-guest-advanced-hd: Copyright (C) 2018-2025 guest(r); its vignette: Copyright (C) 2019-2025 guest(r) and Dr. Venom; GNU GPL v2 or later. The port, crt.js, is shared under the GNU GPL v2 or later; here are all the notices.\nWe are The Paninos off-line: two brothers, and that's the whole team. We made this site, with the help of AI.\nWe are also remaking Sierra's The Black Cauldron (1986) in Adventure Game Studio, free and non-commercial. Its website opens soon.\nA fan-made reference. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Lucasfilm Games, LucasArts or Disney.",
