    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-13-lying-to-zed-to-get-its-remote-server-running-on-freebsd",
      "title": "Lying to Zed to get its remote server running on FreeBSD",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://hayzam.com/blog/03-lying-to-zeds-remote-editor/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50491295\nPoints: 21\n# Comments: 4",
      "body": "I’ve used VS Code since I was about 16, so for almost ten years and honestly it was mostly fine. But as of late it has become unbearable: slow, and increasingly weighed down by AI slop. grahamperrin@ pointed out to me that VS Code now has chat.disableAIFeatures, and they’re right. I totally missed it! It really does turn the built-in AI features off atleast from what I can see in limited testing, and it works. But even with it off, VS Code still feels clunky as hell. Zed, on the other hand, is fast, and it had a native switch that turns all of its AI stuff off slightly earlier than VS Code did, which I absolutely adore. (It’s a toggle in Settings, or \"disable_ai\": true in your settings file.)\nThat’s not because I avoid LLMs. I just don’t want them trying to edit every line I write, or sitting in the corner judging me ominously. I still write most of my FOSS code myself: it keeps me sane, and I feel FOSS developers have a responsibility to keep code clean for LLMs to steal from lol. What I do hand off is the tiring part: regression testing, edge cases, and exploration beyond what my tiny 20-watt brain can hold. For that I use harnesses that work well with models, like OpenCode. Beyond auto-complete.. I’ve always found entire harnesses like claude code etc. very clunky in editors.\nZed has no FreeBSD remote server offering (yet), but FreeBSD can run Linux binaries in spades, so we use Zed’s Linux one. All you need is a FreeBSD server you can SSH into.\n1. Turn on Linux compatibility\nkldstat -m linux64    # should print a module\n2. Make Zed pick the Linux server\nZed asks the host what platform it runs. FreeBSD says FreeBSD amd64, there’s no server for that, and Zed gives up. This wrapper says Linux x86_64 to that one question and passes everything else to the real uname. Save it as ~/.local/bin/uname and make it executable:\n1:-sm|1:-ms) printf 'Linux x86_64\\n' ;;\n*) exec /usr/bin/uname \"$@\" ;;\nPut ~/.local/bin on PATH for non-interactive SSH sessions. For zsh, add this to ~/.zshenv (use your shell’s equivalent):\nIt’s a small lie, but a contained one, and the part I’d love to retire.\nConnect over SSH from Zed and open a folder. The first connection uploads the Linux server to ~/.zed_server/. To watch, run tail -f ~/.local/share/zed/logs/server-workspace-*.log.\n4. Run a kernel with the (FreeBSD) fix\nWithout it you’ll connect fine and get an empty project tree, with this in the server log:\nerror processing entry failed to read directory entry\nInvalid argument (os error 22)\nThe fix is ec6629628cb, in main, stable/15 and releng/15.2, so 15.2-RELEASE will include it. If the error persists, check you booted the new kernel with uname -v.\nThe bug in layman terms\nTo show your project, Zed asks the server for the names of the files in each folder, a few at a time. Zed sets aside a small amount of space for each batch.\nPicture a teacher reading a class roll to a student who writes the names on a sticky note (I couldn’t come up with a better analogy, bare with me). The teacher crosses off each name as she reads it, and she never reads a crossed-off name again. The student’s note has room for 6 names. The teacher reads in groups of 4, a sensible habit that works fine almost every time. But 6 isn’t a whole number of groups, so she reads 8 names and crosses all 8 off. Only 6 fit on the note.\nNow she’s in a bind. Her slip was grabbing more than fit before checking. She can’t hand over the 6 that fit and keep the other 2 for later, because they’re already crossed off and she would never read them again. So she reports an error instead, which at least is loud. When I tried the quiet way, 112 of my 1,002 files vanished (from the listing) with no warning. A loud error beats a quiet loss I’m pretty sure. Zed saw the error and decided it couldn’t read the folder, and showed it up empty.\nThe fix is small: read no more names than fit on the note. Read 6, cross off 6, come back for the next 6, until the roll is done. In the real bug, the teacher is FreeBSD’s Linux translator (Linuxulator), the note is a 768-byte buffer, and a group of 4 names is a 512-byte block.\nThis is a regression. A February 2026 fix for NFS directories returning bogus entries (110f2567cb51, PR 292282) changed how sys/compat/linux/linux_file.c sizes its native read, in both linux_getdents() and linux_getdents64():\n/* before February 2026 */\n/* after: every request rounded up to a 512-byte block */\nbuflen = min(roundup2(args->count, DEV_BSIZE), MAXBSIZE);\nIf the caller’s buffer isn’t a multiple of 512 bytes, the native batch can outgrow it. The emulation layer converts records until one doesn’t fit and returns EINVAL, but kern_getdirentries() has already advanced the directory offset past the whole batch. That’s why returning a partial batch isn’t enough on its own.\nThe fix (ec6629628cb, reviewed in D60760):\nIt’s safe because Linux directory records are never larger than FreeBSD’s: for dirent64 the header is 19 bytes against 24, and both round each record up to a multiple of 8. A native batch that fits in the caller’s buffer translates into one that fits too.\nLegacy getdents had the same bug for 32-bit and 64-bit callers. My first patch only fixed getdents64; fixing both took the test matrix from 86 failures to zero.\nI’ve had plenty of patches merged before this one, but this was among the quickest to land in releng/15.2: it didn’t sit in stable for long (thankfully!). The release engineering team (re@) runs a really cool process that I am yet to fully understand in depth. Thank you also to Kyle Evans (kevans@) and Mark Johnston (markj@) for reviewing the patch and for their notes along the way.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "arch1e",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T22:54:47.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://hayzam.com/og/blog/03-lying-to-zeds-remote-editor.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://hayzam.com/blog/03-lying-to-zeds-remote-editor/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-fitness-obsessed-control-freak-what-can-man-utd-expect-from-",
      "title": "Fitness obsessed & 'control freak' - what can Man Utd expect from Pochettino?",
      "summary": "What can Manchester United fans expect from their new manager Mauricio Pochettino?  BBC Sport gets an insight from Toby Alderweireld, who played under him at Spurs.",
      "body": "Alderweireld, 37, says Pochettino has a no-nonsense approach to management and that he can get the best out of a squad, as long as you do it his way.\"He is very approachable but you need to follow his plan. If you don't follow his plan, you go on the side and you're not coming back any more.\"You need to think about team, team, team. If you're thinking about yourself, you're gone.\"As a player you have your own life, your own situation but he says 'look, I understand and you can talk to me about it but the team is number one' - that's the way it is and if you're not following it, he'll put you on the sideline.\"He's straightforward, has his own ideas and maybe you agree with them or don't, that's up to you. But you need to follow him and I think that's something Manchester United need - a big character, everyone in the same direction, following the plan and if not, no problem, you can go. He's the main man.\"He's a very smart man, knows how to talk to the press and what to say. He will create an environment like 'it's us against the world' - we are getting fit, we are together as a team. It doesn't matter what people say, let's work together and get results together. \"At Spurs there was coaching staff but he will be there. He knows everything. If he's not there, he's looking at video and data. He knows everything about the players on and off the pitch - the players will be watched.\"He is, in a positive way, a control freak because he says your body is your weapon and it needs to be ready to perform.\"Manchester United have been criticised this season for an apparent lack of fitness and for not running enough in games - they rank in the bottom five in the league for distance covered and most high-intensity runs.Alderweireld says Pochettino's number one demand is fitness and running.\"First of all he made us very fit. Pre-season was brutal. \"The way he wants to play - on the front foot, putting people under pressure, a high line, very dynamic play - you need to run. You need to be capable of running and be capable of high intensity runs - the basic is to be fit.\"Every manager wants a good pre-season and time to give the players their ideas and the fitness. But the players are going to feel straight away that it's a different level. \"This is every day at the training ground, it's 100% - that's what he asks, what he demands. It will be a big difference for the players and they're going to see results there straight away.\"In the Champions League semi-final, we were 3-0 down [against Ajax] and he didn't have a tactical half-time talk. He just said 'you're going to be physical, you're going to fight, you only need one goal to be back in the game, believe that everything is possible'. \"That was a big moment because we were nowhere near Ajax at that time, especially in that game. But we changed everything by intensity, by duels. I remember the long ball to [Fernando] Llorente and just fighting and asking 'how badly do you want it?' It changed from that moment.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T22:53:17.000Z",
