    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-us-air-force-says-it-responded-to-suspected-drones-over-raf-",
      "title": "US Air Force says it responded to 'suspected drones' over RAF Lakenheath",
      "summary": "The American military says there were no injuries or damage after \"suspected unauthorised drone activity\" this evening.",
      "body": "The US Air Force said it responded to \"suspected unauthorised drone activity\" over RAF Lakenheath in Suffolk on Friday evening.No personnel, aircraft or infrastructure were affected, a spokesperson for the US Air Force said.It comes after unconfirmed reports that sirens were sounded at the base, which residents reported hearing from a nearby village. A Ministry of Defence spokesperson told the BBC it was \"coordinating with all relevant parties\", adding that US Air Force security personnel responded to the reports and the situation was resolved.\"We are working closely with them to establish the facts,\" they added.West Suffolk MP Nick Timothy said he had been in touch with authorities at the base, and was told \"there is no threat to the public, and the incident is now resolved\".\"Given the raised threat level, I continue to encourage everyone to report anything suspicious or out of the ordinary to the authorities,\" he added in a social media post.Earlier this week, at least 12 US Air Force aircraft departed the nearby RAF Mildenhall base.They included two Boeing Stratotanker KC-135 refuelling aircraft, with one apparently headed towards Germany and another seen landing at Prestwick Airport near Glasgow.There has been heightened security activity at US bases in the UK following an incident at RAF Fairford in September that Prime Minister Andy Burnham said Iran played a part in.Police said petrol but no explosives were found during searches of vans near the base. Two men have been charged by the Crown Prosecution Service over their alleged involvement in the incident. While Iran denied any responsibility, the US removed all of its bombers from the base in October.The Wall Street Journal reported the departure of the US aircraft from RAF Mildenhall this week was in response to a further threat from Iran but the BBC has not independently verified the claim.Earlier this week, Downing Street insisted British airbases were safe and that their security is a \"top priority\".RAF Lakenheath hosts the US Air Force's 48th Fighter Wing, or the Liberty Wing as it is also known, which has nearly 7,000 active-duty personnel, some of whom are stationed at the base. The Liberty Wing is the largest US fighter operation in Europe, its website says, employing four combat ready fighter squadrons.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T22:12:33.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/d291/live/9c151020-dfa5-11f1-ab35-8541e0a03fdd.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6jd75geegzyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-13-us-government-grounds-all-flights-across-the-united-states-d",
      "title": "US government grounds all flights across the United States ‘due to extraordinary circumstances’",
      "summary": "A spokesperson for the FAA told TechCrunch that it issued the ground stop \"following communication issues impacting multiple Air Route Traffic Control Centers,\" which manage the airspace across the United States.",
      "body": "Image Credits:Tayfun Coskun/Anadolu / Getty Images\n1:44 PM PST · November 13, 2026\nThe Federal Aviation Administration said Friday afternoon that it has grounded all flights across the United States due to “extraordinary circumstances” and issues related to safety.\nThe FAA’s website said the cause is an “equipment outage.” The site itself was down for a brief period at the time of publication, with a note that the website was “experiencing technical difficulties.”\nPer an alert sent by the Air Traffic Control System Command Center, or ATCSCC, the FAA said the ground stop means that aircraft cannot take off from departure gates. The message said that the disruption was “due to extraordinary circumstances and for reasons of safety,” but did not specify further. The alert was not visible on the ATCSCC website at press time.\nThe alert stated that all departing flights must remain on the ground “until further notice.” Inbound international flights will be “managed on a case-by-case basis,” it said.\nA spokesperson for the FAA told TechCrunch that it issued the ground stop “following communication issues impacting multiple Air Route Traffic Control Centers,” which manage the airspace across the United States.\nTransportation Secretary Sean Duffy said in a post on X: “FAA technicians are investigating the issue.”\nIt’s not clear what caused the ongoing FAA equipment outage. The outage comes in the same week that the administration rolled out an AI-powered tool for air traffic controllers to save aircraft flight time and fuel costs — though it’s unclear if the ongoing outage is related.\nSpeaking to reporters outside the White House, Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin said that there is “no indication yet that this was a nefarious act,” but warned that the recovery “is going to take some time.”\nUpdated with comment from the FAA, and remarks from Homeland Security.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nHe can be reached via encrypted message at zackwhittaker.1337 on Signal. You can also contact him by email, or to verify outreach, at [email protected].",
