      "body": "Aged mice engineered to lack granzyme K have improved heart and liver function and less kidney damage.\nAge-related stiffening and scarring of liver tissue (pictured) declined in mice that were bioengineered to lack a certain enzyme.  Credit: Ziad M. El-Zaatari/Science Photo Library\nResearchers have found an enzyme that drives ageing in the body’s tissues1. Drugs that reduce the levels of this protein might one day combat the effects of ageing without impairing the body’s immune defences.\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\nReceive 51 print issues and online access\nRent or buy this article\nPrices vary by article type\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\nBistable regulation of adipocyte heterogeneity governs thermogenesis\nCan science make your dog live longer? Longevity craze comes to pets\nWorms live longer in cool conditions thanks to a muscle-derived molecule\nNews & Views 21 OCT 26",
