      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-piddington-to-bring-legal-challenge-over-asylum-base-plans",
      "title": "Piddington to bring legal challenge over asylum base plans",
      "summary": "Up to 1,256 men could be housed at the site near Piddington in Oxfordshire under the plans.",
      "body": "McNally told BBC Radio 4's Today programme that the village could also apply the new Hillsborough Law.Once passed, the law would create a legally enforceable duty of candour which compels public officials and authorities to act transparently when investigations and inquiries take place.McNally told the programme: \"Last night, [Prime Minister] Andy Burnham was on a podium talking about the violence, sexual violence towards women, and bringing it upon the media industry as their responsibility to manage.\"But the reality is, is that this is exactly what the government is doing to us here.\"Asked how the village would respond, he said: \"There's going to be many actions, you will see. We will disclose that as we move forward.\"Today is a day of reflection, and we'll work out where we plan to go from this point. But I will say, the Hillsborough Law is there for a reason, and we're going to apply it.\"Asked if ministers should be braced for a legal challenge, he said: \"Absolutely, absolutely.\"A government report said the decision received 3,177 representations against and one in support.It also said Rayner had ruled permission via an Urgent Crown Development application should be granted because \"national demand for asylum accommodation remains high\".The permission allows for demolition of existing buildings, the building of new modular accommodation, and provisions for health and welfare facilities.Jo Haw-Cook, who has lived in the village for 56 years, said she is \"saddened\" but \"not surprised\".Speaking to the BBC earlier, she said: \"There's still a long way to go, and we're still going to fight on.\"I think the mood is reasonably positive, obviously we are disappointed, but we're resilient, and we are a community, and we will come together to fight this as a community.\"Defence minister Luke Pollard told LBC: \"It's pretty obvious that there's a strong sense of feeling from the community around Bicester, especially in Piddington.\"The Home Office continues to engage with those.\"There's still a few more stages to go here, so it's not yet fully locked in and delivered, so that we can understand where the concerns are, so they can be mitigated.\"I recognise that any change for the people around MoD Bicester will be a concern, and that's why it's so important that the Home Office engages with the community.\"In a statement posted on social media, Bicester Town Council said it is \"deeply disappointed\" by the government's decision to grant planning permission.\"This decision was taken through the Urgent Crown Development process, bypassing normal local planning scrutiny, despite strong and well-founded objections... regarding the suitability of the site, infrastructure capacity, community safety, and the lack of meaningful consultation,\" it said.The council said it will now prepare a formal response to the government \"setting out its continuing concerns and seeking clear assurances on the issues raised\".Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch said the plans were a \"betrayal of the women of Piddington\" and \"emblematic of Labour and Angela Rayner's disregard for the law and women and girls' safety\".She said on social media: \"When I visited Piddington I met a village terrified of what was about to land on their doorstep.\"The Government said it was listening, instead it's completely ignored this community's concerns.\"The 1,000+ male migrants that Labour are about to deposit in Oxfordshire are in Britain illegally. They should be deported as soon as possible, not housed in areas completely unsuitable for the task.\"Reform UK home affairs spokesman Zia Yusuf said Piddington has become \"ground zero for the invasion of Britain\".Speaking in Piddington, he told broadcasters: \"Despite an enormous campaign from local residents demanding that the government doesn't dump 1,200 illegal migrant men into their village, Angela Rayner has approved it. It's absolutely shocking.\"On whether using the former military site to house asylum seekers is a better use of taxpayers' money than using hotels, Yusuf said: \"The only thing that should be happening with these people is them being detained and deported back to the country from which they came.\"Additional reporting by Press Association.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
