    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-13-jensen-huang-admitted-he-was-facing-an-avalanche-of-excess-w",
      "title": "Jensen Huang Admitted He Was Facing an \"Avalanche\" of Excess Wafers and Memory",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://respawnfirst.com/before-resigning-jensen-huang-admitted-he-was-facing-an-avalanche-of-excess-wafers-and-memory-new-report-sheds-more-light-on-the-gpu-ram-crisis/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50482992\nPoints: 13\n# Comments: 6",
      "body": "For the past two years, the narrative around inflated GPU and RAM prices has been the insatiable AI demand eating up wafer at TSMC and every module on Earth must go to AI data centers. However, the narrative is cracking ever since reports of channel stuffing and over commitments came out.    Now with Nvidia's former CEO Jensen Huang gone, more details are out that corroborate our earlier reports on inflated demand on AI. According to Wall Street Journal, Huang admitted in August that major cloud providers are moving away from Nvidia in favor of in-house chips, leaving Nvidia staring down a massive oversupply of prepaid TSMC silicon and memory that was \"coming at him like an 'avalanche'\".    The WSJ report details a heated clash in August 2026 between Huang and Nvidia’s longtime Chief Financial Officer, Colette Kress, the executive who has now taken over as Nvidia’s interim CEO.    Kress was concerned about Nvidia's growing financial exposure and wanted to rein in the company's \"circular financing\" commitments to cloud providers, and AI companies like OpenAI and Anthropic.    While early talks had floated backstopping roughly 8 gigawatts of OpenAI compute (worth up to $250 billion), Kress successfully scaled Nvidia’s signed guarantee cap down to roughly 4.25 gigawatts, or up to $125 billion.    In an August meeting, Huang belittled Kress for questioning the deals, calling her \"a deer in the headlights\". To justify why Nvidia needed to financially backstop OpenAI and smaller cloud providers to keep buying its GPUs, Huang admitted that Nvidia was caught in a massive squeeze.     Then he went on to describe the situation the company was in. Major cloud providers were running away from Nvidia, making plans to use alternative chips and lessen their reliance on the firm. Even worse, Huang said, the vast supply of chip wafers and memory Nvidia had committed to buying were coming at him like an 'avalanche.' Per the WSJ            Also See    “RAM Altman” Says The World Needs to Accept “Bad Things Will Happen” for AI to Move Forward      Nvidia has reportedly contacted TSMC to wind down their chip orders.    For PC builders and hardware enthusiast the reported admission is validating, and quite glaring. Jensen Huang, instead of allowing excess TSMC wafer capacity and memory flow back into reasonable priced GeForce products and broader DRAM production, allegedly scrambled to use its own balance sheet to finance data center buildouts so AI startups and absorb Nvidia's over-ordered chips.     https://twitter.com/edzitron/status/2121016077073502669?am8yjnDF2jT83nOiKKgQ&amp;s20     Reports of glut of unsold hardware have been around for a while, but only now mainstream media is catching up to what Nvidia was reportedly doing. Many industry analysts including Ed Zitron reported months ago how Nvidia and other companies have exposed themselves to devastating financial risk, and also made sure to channel stuff META, Google, Amazon, and others by leveraging their FOMO.    Zitron estimated that at least $200 billion worth of Nvidia AI chips are sitting idle in warehouses, purchased by cloud giants that lacked the electrical grid capacity or immediate end-user demand to plug them in.    The bottom line is, AI demand is no way near as high that it would consume this much silicon.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Tech",
        " "
      ],
      "author": "oscarfr",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T13:08:48.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://respawnfirst.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Jensen-Huang-shady-deals.webp",
      "originalUrl": "https://respawnfirst.com/before-resigning-jensen-huang-admitted-he-was-facing-an-avalanche-of-excess-wafers-and-memory-new-report-sheds-more-light-on-the-gpu-ram-crisis/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-11-13-apple-should-have-made-the-macbook-logo-rgb",
      "title": "Apple should have made the MacBook logo RGB",
      "summary": "Get that apple back to its rainbow roots, but for a new generation. | Image: The Verge\t\n\nWhen Apple CEO John Ternus announced the first touchscreen Mac last month, the biggest reaction in the room wasn't for the PROLED MacBook Pro's OLED touchscreen or the thinner and lighter chassis; it was for the illuminated lid logo. It's a throwback to the light-up logos on the old MacBooks, which Apple start",
      "body": "When Apple CEO John Ternus announced the first touchscreen Mac last month, the biggest reaction in the room wasn’t for the PROLED MacBook Pro’s OLED touchscreen or the thinner and lighter chassis; it was for the illuminated lid logo. It’s a throwback to the light-up logos on the old MacBooks, which Apple started phasing out in 2015, and which were completely gone by the 2018 MacBook Air redesign. It’s a nostalgia play, but boy did it work. It’s fun!\nJust like in the old days, the new logo’s brightness is linked to the screen’s: The brighter or dimmer the screen, the brighter or dimmer the logo. But the light-up logo on the old MacBooks was illuminated by the screen’s backlight, and an OLED display doesn’t have one. Thanks to iFixit, we now know it’s lit by four white LEDs and synced with the screen’s brightness by software. But that means Apple could have used RGB LEDs.\nRed, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet, or anything in between? On the lid of your new MacBook Pro? Why not? After all, Apple insists it’s still serious about gaming, and nothing says “serious about gaming” like RGB lighting.\n[Image: The new LED-illuminated lid logo of the PROLED MacBook Pro (left) next to the opaque black logo of other MacBooks (right). https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/268836_MacBook_Pro_touchscreen_OLED_handson_ADiBenedetto_0028.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nI was secretly hoping users would be able to customize the illuminated lid cutout much like the Xbox logo on Microsoft’s Elite Series 2 controllers. But alas, no luck. You can turn off the illumination with some Terminal commands or take control of it with a third-party app. But when it’s lit, it’s only lit with white light. And iFixit’s teardown also showed the difficulty of accessing those LEDs (the screen delaminated and broke in the process). So we’re unlikely to see LED RGB mods for it unless someone’s brave enough to void their warranty and risk a pricey screen repair on their $3,000 laptop.\nThe most fun you can have with the Apple lid logo is to install a free third-party app like LogoLight and have it pulse, breathe, match the beat of any music you’re playing, or flash some Morse code. There’s some fun there, but if you combined these features with a splash of color we’d really be cooking.\n[Image: Apps like LogoLight allow you to customize the lid logo, but not with color. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-12-at-6.09.10-PM.png?quality=90&strip=all]\nMacBooks aren’t gaming laptops, so RGB lighting may seem like a mismatch, but multicolor LEDs could have been a cool Easter egg. Nobody thought Apple would put a touchscreen on a MacBook either, and the iPhone Duo and HomeView also show Apple is in a new age of experimentation during the early Ternus era. Maybe if the light-up lid logo ever comes to future MacBook Airs or a MacBook Neo, Apple could cut loose a little.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Analysis",
        "Apple",
        "Gadgets",
        "Gaming",
        "Laptops",
        "macOS",
        "PC Gaming",
        "Report",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Antonio G. Di Benedetto",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T13:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/268868_Apple_added_LEDs_to_its_logo_on_the_MacBook_Pro_and_now_I_want_RGB_CVirginia.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1032550/apple-macbook-pro-proled-xdr-lid-logo-led-rgb-colors",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-blink-182-to-make-download-debut-as-2027-festival-headliners",
      "title": "Blink-182 to make Download debut as 2027 festival headliners",
      "summary": "Evanescence and Bring Me The Horizon will also top the bill at the rock and metal festival.",
      "body": "Blink-182, Evanescence & Bring Me The Horizon have been announced as headliners of next year's Download Festival.The trio are among more than 100 rock and metal acts set to play the event at Donington Park, Leicestershire from 9 to 13 June.Other acts on the bill include The Prodigy, 5 Seconds of Summer and a special performance by heavy metal legends Mötley Crüe.US pop-punk band Blink-182 will be making their Download debut, while Evanescence will become the festival's second female-fronted headliner.The American rock band were founded by singer Amy Lee in the mid-1990s and had a UK number one with their best-known single Bring Me to Life in 2003.It's the second year in a row a female-led band has headlined the festival, after Linkin Park topped the bill in 2025.The nu-metal group have reformed with Emily Armstrong as their lead singer, after the death of former frontman Chester Bennington, who took his own life in 2017.The choice of Emily upset some fans, who pointed to her alleged ties to the Church of Scientology and past support for convicted rapist Danny Masterson - someone she distanced herself from.Next year's Download will be a first for Blink-182, who emerged from the California skate punk scene in the 1990s.They've had two UK top 10 singles and had their first number one album in 2016, 21 years after their first record.Their drummer Travis Barker is married to Kourtney Kardashian and survived a plane crash in the US that killed four people in 2008.British rockers Bring Me The Horizon are more familiar to Download regulars, headlining for the second time in five years.The four-piece band, formed in Sheffield, announced earlier this year they're celebrating 20 years of the debut album Count Your Blessings by re-recording it.That 2006 record was in the deathcore genre, known for its heavy and aggressive sound, but the band have since moved towards metalcore, pop and electronic rock.Some fans questioned if the original lyrics, which frontman Oli Sykes admitted could be interpreted as misogynistic, were still appropriate.But the 39-year-old lead vocalist told the BBC Radio 1 Rock Show that going back to their roots was \"a lot of fun\" as they \"got to remake the album how we wanted it to sound back then\".Download bills itself as a festival \"spanning the full spectrum of rock, metal, punk, emo, hardcore, alternative and classic rock\".It called this year's line-up \"bigger, better and bolder than ever\" and said it features \"something for everyone, from huge artists to electric newcomers\".But not everyone who commented on the festival's Instagram post was as convinced.Writing about Blink-182, one person said \"the fact they've been posted as some mega headliner at a metal festival blows my mind\". \"Literally not one headliner that's proper metal,\" they added.Fan Madison Travers, who's hoping to go to her ninth Download in a row, says she's \"really happy with the line-up\" - but think's there's more to the festival.\"I don't think it matters about the line-up specifically,\" the 27-year-old tells Newsbeat.\"I think the community there and just the energy all weekend is what we look forward to.\"Other acts announced include Turnstile, Faith No More, Body Count ft. Ice-T and Malevolence - with the festival saying more will be revealed.Some pre-sales are currently available but tickets go on general sale at 10:00 GMT on Sunday.In recent years Download has also featured pop acts and surprise guests, with the Vengaboys an unlikely addition to 2024's line-up.And Dogstar - the band formed by actor Keanu Reeves - performed in 2026. Additional reporting by Perisha Kudhail",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
