      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-how-a-sex-scandal-at-nvidia-is-putting-pressure-on-the-white",
      "title": "How a sex scandal at Nvidia is putting pressure on the White House",
      "summary": "Jensen Huang's personal life may have triggered the crisis, but the questions now reach into the White House itself.",
      "body": "How close is Donald Trump to Jensen Huang?The answer is very.The Nvidia boss was one of the few business leaders with direct access to the US president. He had met Trump repeatedly, travelled with him to Britain and, in May, joined him on Air Force One for his trip to China.Jensen Huang wasn't just another tech billionaire seeking the president's ear. He had become an important voice in one of the biggest arguments inside the administration: how much of America's most advanced technology should be allowed to reach China.Nvidia makes the chips that power the AI revolution. Washington has restricted exports of its most advanced processors to China since 2022, believing they could help Beijing develop its military and technological capabilities.But enforcing those restrictions is difficult. Chips can be bought legally by customers in countries such as Singapore and Malaysia and then diverted to China. Chinese companies can also rent access to computing power through data centres outside the country.Jensen Huang has long argued that the restrictions are counterproductive. They shut Nvidia out of a huge market and encourage Chinese companies to develop their own alternatives. Trump appeared sympathetic to this view, allowing Nvidia to resume sales of certain chips to China last year in return for a share of the revenue, although its most advanced Blackwell chips remain off limits.But that relationship now looks rather more complicated.The allegations surrounding Jensen Huang's reported affair with Alice Huang, of no relation but a former executive at Singapore-based company Megaspeed, have raised the possibility that a foreign intelligence operation was targeting not just a powerful businessman, but someone with unusually close access to the American president. US intelligence reportedly suspects Alice Huang of having links to Chinese intelligence. Beijing has rejected the allegations. Nvidia's board says it has no reason to believe company secrets or national security were compromised. But the affair has opened up a much bigger question: what did the administration in Washington know, and what did it do about it?That question leads directly to Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick.His department was responsible for controlling exports of sensitive American technology. It was also being pressed to act on concerns about Megaspeed, an Nvidia customer suspected of helping divert advanced chips to China. On Thursday, Megaspeed chief executive, Tan Yong Pong, also known as James Tan, said the company \"takes its compliance obligations under US export control laws extremely seriously\" and is \"committed to working constructively and transparently with the US government\". He said the company had co-operated with US Commerce Department inspections.A highly classified memo was shared with Lutnick about the concerns surrounding Alice Huang and Megaspeed. He resisted taking action against the company, a decision that is now under scrutiny. Other senior officials wanted a tougher response. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent was among those who reportedly questioned why Megaspeed had not been placed on the Commerce Department's blacklist.On Friday, Bessent pointed the finger at Commerce for not blacklisting the company, telling CNBC that several government agencies \"were in favour of putting Megaspeed on the entity list for months\".\"But Commerce has the pen... and you will have to ask them about their internal process,\" he added.There have also been questions about why an investigation into the Singapore-based company was stopped after Nvidia intervened. The New York Times reported that Nvidia's general counsel contacted Commerce Department investigators probing suspected chip smuggling by Megaspeed invoking a senior Trump appointee. The department allegedly then froze its investigation and handed the case to the Justice Department.This apparently highlighted a significant disagreement about the direction of Trump's China policy. Huang wanted access to the Chinese market. Other officials were supposedly worried about the national security consequences. Lutnick was caught in the middle.Now the political pressure is building.Members of Congress from both parties want answers. Democrats have launched an investigation into how the Commerce Department vetted Chinese companies approved to buy AI chips. Republicans are also questioning the administration's approach.And Jensen Huang's departure has not eased the pressure.Republican representative John Moolenaar, who chairs the House Select Committee on China, said it was in the national interest for Nvidia and other American AI firms to succeed, but that companies \"should never jeopardise our nation's security\" by giving Chinese firms access to their most advanced chips.Senator Elizabeth Warren, the top Democrat on the Senate Banking Committee, said Huang's resignation did not absolve him of responsibility, and urged Lutnick to explain why export control loopholes remain open.Meanwhile Jensen Huang's team told Republican lawmakers on Wednesday that he plans to cooperate with their request to testify behind closed doors.The White House has not responded to the BBC's request for comment.It has, however, told the New York Post that Trump has complete confidence in Lutnick, describing him as \"the most transformative commerce secretary in modern history\".That is a fairly emphatic show of support, particularly amid reports that officials are weighing whether Lutnick should be forced out.There are some obvious questions. If Lutnick had indeed been warned about the risks, why did his department fail to act? And if other senior officials were indeed pressing for action, why did it take so long?The BBC has contacted the Commerce Department for comment. But there is an even bigger question for Trump.The president cultivated a close relationship with Huang. He was directly involved in decisions about chip sales to China. And, according to reporting in the New York Times, he was briefed on the intelligence concerns.This is becoming difficult to dismiss as the misjudgement of one businessman or one government department.Jensen Huang's personal life may have triggered the crisis, but the questions now reach into the White House itself. How much influence did he have? What did the president know? And who was ultimately responsible for protecting America's most sensitive technology?",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T12:22:30.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/2ab0/live/068591d0-dee5-11f1-9bb0-8de7754c4db5.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c67429m3vem3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-more-women-make-allegations-against-russell-brand",
      "title": "More women make allegations against Russell Brand",
      "summary": "The new claims are in the Times and a Netflix documentary, following Brand's recent conviction for rape.",
      "body": "Warning - this story contains graphic contentMore women have come forward to make allegations about disgraced entertainer Russell Brand, in interviews with the Times and a new Netflix documentary.Brand was jailed earlier this week after being found guilty of one count of rape and three of sexual assault at a trial in London.Allegations against him first emerged after an investigation by the Times, the Sunday Times and Channel 4's Dispatches in 2023.The Times has now reported, external accusations of what it called sexual misconduct and predatory behaviour by a further six women, which the newspaper said could not be reported until the criminal case had ended. He has denied non-consensual sexual activity.The Times report was published on Thursday evening, and a Netflix documentary, titled Russell Brand: Open Secret, was made available on Friday morning.The programme included an interview with a woman referred to as Emily, not her real name, who said she was in a relationship with Brand when she was in her twenties.On one occasion, she said she \"literally begged\" Brand to stop after he refused to wear a condom during sex.Emily, who was portrayed by an actress in the documentary, said: \"I said to him, 'No, Russell, please, let's just use protection.' I kept saying 'No'. I said, 'Stop Russell, stop, please don't, please.'\"I begged him. I literally begged him. I tried to kick him off, I tried to draw my knees back, to kick him in his belly, but he was too strong for me.\"But he \"just pinned my hands back behind my head\" before forcing himself upon her, she alleged.\"When it happens you just feel like, is this... is this rape? Because I'm with this guy [in a relationship],\" she said, according to the documentary.\"I know I said 'No', but you question yourself and you think, did I do something wrong?\"She \"left his as quickly as I could\" and told one friend what had happened, but \"back then women felt shamed\", she said.\"You didn't talk about it. I just felt completely violated and unseen and unheard. I was saying 'No'. I kept saying, 'No, stop, please, stop, stop, no'.\"Another ex-girlfriend, Jordan Martin, claimed in the documentary that Brand sexually assaulted her and smashed her phone in a hotel after she had spoken to an ex to arrange to collect her belongings.\"He snatched my phone out of my hand and broke it apart into pieces,\" she said.Brand then followed her into a different room, she said. \"He didn't say anything. He put his hand down the front of my jeans and he inserted a finger and then he walked away.\"She added: \"I look at myself back then and I feel sorry for myself. And not in a bad or pitying way, I just feel like it wasn't very fair.\"Martin also said Brand was jealous and controlling, and had made her \"brush her teeth until gums bled\".\"He wanted me to taste anonymous,\" Martin said. \"And so he was asking me to brush my teeth, but it just was never enough. And so he's standing over me and I'm brushing and I'm brushing, and then I'm seeing my teeth bleeding.\"The Times and Sunday Times reported that at least five sexual partners said Brand had forced them to brush their teeth, including the fifth complainant in the trial, whom he was convicted of raping in 2009.Among the new claims reported by the paper is one from a woman referred to as Alex, who claimed she had asked Brand to stop having sex after noticing he wasn't using a condom.\"I said, 'No, stop', and I started to sort of try and move. And... that wasn't happening,\" she said.When he continued, she decided \"I'm not going to fight\", she said, because \"who knows what he could do?\"Alex said Brand apologised in person and by text message after the alleged incident.\"I justified it in my head by saying to myself, at least he admitted it,\" she told the Times.A woman called Vicky also claimed she had asked him to stop twice after they started having sex without any build up.\"My body wasn't ready for this and it was painful\", she remembered.\"An uneasy wave hits me and I'm scared. And I say, 'It's painful' and he doesn't stop. And then it gets worse and it's really painful. And I say 'Stop' and he doesn't stop.\" She added that it had felt like he \"just wasn't present\".She claimed he said afterwards: \"Tonight was consensual. Nobody did anything they didn't want to do.\"Another woman named Zoe said Brand was \"annoyed\" after she turned up to his hotel late.\"He basically sort of just grabbed me and manhandled me around a little bit and he put his hand on my throat,\" she told the Times.\"And then he shoved his other hand down my trousers and penetrated me with his finger.\"I didn't resist. I just sort of let it happen.\"On Monday, Brand was sentenced to 10 years and six months in prison following his trial. He is expected to serve seven years.He denied the charges and was acquitted on two further rape charges and one count of attempted indecent assault.Police have said Brand is being investigated over rape allegations made by two more women.The Metropolitan Police have said they are also \"assessing\" information from several people who contacted them during the trial \"to provide information or report a potential criminal allegation\".Brand has been placed inside HMP Wandsworth. BBC News has asked his legal representatives for a response to the latest allegations.If you are affected by any of the issues in this story, help and support is available at BBC Action Line.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T12:14:17.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/29e9/live/8dd88650-df63-11f1-a701-fddedf244c16.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cme3dwydp857o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-how-ferguson-inspired-pochettino-can-succeed-where-carrick-f",
      "title": "How Ferguson-inspired Pochettino can succeed where Carrick failed at Man Utd",
      "summary": "With Mauricio Pochettino set to become Man Utd manager, we analyse if he can clear the tactical hurdles others haven't.",
      "body": "How Ferguson-inspired Pochettino can succeed where Carrick failed at Man UtdImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Maurucio Pochettino (right) was first mentioned as a contender to manage Manchester United when Louis van Gaal was sacked in May 2016ByFootball tactics correspondentWhen Mauricio Pochettino was asked the three Premier League managers he would have loved to have played for, he was unequivocal.\"Sir Alex Ferguson, Sir Alex Ferguson, Sir Alex Ferguson,\" replied Pochettino, who on Friday was named Manchester United manager.In 2023, the Argentine - then at Chelsea, and dressed in full Blues training gear - was being interviewed by Sky Sports. Still he felt a need to express his adoration for Ferguson.Six years prior to that interview, while managing Tottenham, he had shared lunch with the legendary former United manager.\"When I was young, when I started my career as a manager, he was always my inspiration, my reference,\" Pochettino said. \"And when you can share two hours with a person who was the greatest manager in the history of football, you can only enjoy.\"Hiring United managers has been complicated since Ferguson's departure in 2013, and one of the most prominent complications has been a back-and-forth around the club's DNA.For some, winning by any means necessary should take precedence, while others believe the style of play and cultural heritage of United's most successful period is vital to the club's identity.In recent years, United's managerial merry-go-round has alternated between European coaches with their own fixed styles of play, and former players from the Ferguson era.But between his tactics of choice and his adoration for the former United boss, Pochettino may be uniquely placed to bridge the gap between both camps.Michael Carrick's United produced a series of good results to finish last season with a run unmatched by any other team in the league. They looked great, but it was a greatness built on attacking in a transition-heavy manner. Under Carrick, in games they had less than 50% of the ball, they only lost once. In those 13 games, they boasted a win-rate of 62%. This dropped to 36% in games they had the majority of possession.This style of play suited their direct attackers and protected their defenders. At the same time, Casemiro's set-piece prowess provided United a reliable way to break the deadlock against deep defences - something they have struggled with this season. With United a goal up, opponents stepped forward, giving Carrick's men more space. They could play to their strengths again, attacking quickly. It worked.But teams at the top are reluctant to play in this way. Carrick's vision for United was a side that played slick football, with short passes in build-up intuitively, referencing Paris St-Germain's midfield three as a source of inspiration. Building up in this manner, to entice and escape from pressure, before looking to find the forwards quickly, was the plan Carrick was working towards.This didn't come to fruition and trying to take United from a successful counter-attacking side to one that could reliably beat teams while keeping the ball was the same hurdle that undid Ole Gunnar Solskjar and Erik ten Hag. Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Former players under Sir Alex Ferguson, both Ole Gunnar Solskjar and Michael Carrick leaned into United’s historic style when crafting their game-modelsPochettino gives United an interesting solution to this problem.In his own words, he has described his sides as needing \"to be protagonists, always press high, work hard and be dynamic\". He refers to himself as a \"disciple\" of the legendary Argentina boss Marcelo Bielsa, who he played for. If you wanted an immediate comparison in the Premier League to a coach who resembles Pochettino's ideas in possession, it would be worth looking at Liverpool's  Andoni Iraola, who similarly cites Bielsa as his reference.These are coaches who, while taking the initiative, encourage vertical football. For as different as the clubs are, both the fans of United and Liverpool have enjoyed success when their teams have played high-octane football.This summer's World Cup was a strong showcase of Pochettino's style of play and his recent tactical adaptation. Rather than a fixed, positional style of play - akin to the work of somebody like Manchester City's Enzo Maresca - Pochettino's USA had only certain players being tasked with holding their position.This approach posed problems for teams who defended in a zonal manner - defending specific areas rather than individual players.The US midfielders being able to move freely meant they could step into areas of the pitch between the zones that were being defended, which resulted in their players often being unmarked with time and space on the ball.By playing in close proximity to each other in the middle of the pitch, the US found another benefit. Small-space passing combinations helped draw the opposing team up towards the ball and combined with clever off-ball movement to stretch the pitch, allowed the US to find their striker with few defenders around him.Pochettino's men looked to thread through-balls in behind and early after enticing the opposition's press and the speedy Folarin Balogun was able to turn those chances into goals.Image gallerySkip image galleryImage caption, Here is a good example of USA's fluid midfield four under Pochettino. The right-sided midfielder McKennie has placed himself near his three midfield team-mates, leaving the right half-space empty. With a four against three in that area of the pitch, USA are best placed to play quick combinations before looking to find their attackers in space.Slide 1 of 2, Screen grab showing USA’s tactics against Paraguay in the World Cup., Here is a good example of USA's fluid midfield four under Pochettino. The right-sided midfielder McKennie has placed himself near his three midfield team-mates, leaving the right half-space empty. With a four against three in that area of the pitch, USA are best placed to play quick combinations before looking to find their attackers in space.This combination of a fluid midfield, small-space passing and direct through-balls doesn't sound dissimilar to what Carrick was trying to move towards implementing this season. And although that may worry United fans, the similarity in principles will benefit the players. \"My philosophy is to give a freer style with a good organisation,\" were Pochettino's words in 2014. Although philosophically similar, that latter part - a clearer organisation to his freer style - is likely to be what Carrick was unable to reliably offer his players. For fans of top sides, fast-paced, vertical football tends to be accepted when it comes from a place of being proactive - think Ferguson's United or Jurgen Klopp's Liverpool - wanting to keep the ball, but not passing it for passing's sake. Fast-paced, vertical football tends to be frowned upon when it comes from a place of conceding possession and defending your own goal for long periods of the match.The first half of Carrick's stint did appear proactive, while his 4-2-4 press worked well. Leaving four players high was a risk that allowed them to win the ball back and score quickly, but as teams figured this out, United failed to adapt. There also appeared to be a drop in the team's fitness the longer United went from the departure of ex-boss Ruben Amorim, who left United well conditioned, albeit on the back of a schedule without European football.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Here we see United's narrow 4-2-4 defensive shape in the first half against ComoHiring Pochettino will immediately begin the process of turning United into a well-drilled side, capable of applying intense pressure, likely in a co-ordinated manner. Pochettino enjoyed relative success with Spurs, built on this intensity and co-ordination, that differentiated him from the rest of the league. With that unique selling point more common now than a decade ago, how he builds on it will dictate how his tenure unfolds. But watching Brighton this season, he can be assured that an approach founded on intensity off the ball can work in the Premier League in 2026, with Fabian Hurzeler's men second in the league, with the fourth highest average possession, 10 games in.Pochettino, from a footballing point of view, feels extremely aligned to the values of Ferguson's United, and as a result will implement numerous similarities to the things Carrick wanted, but ultimately failed, to deliver. His wealth of experience make him a more proven entity than the departing United manager and if he can get his team fit and pressing high, in typically Pochettino fashion, they should see an upturn in form.Expanding on his managerial approach, while managing Spurs he explained how \"the most important thing [was] to create a good shape for players to be comfortable and to show their real qualities\".Rather than relying on pre-defined pattern, he looks to empower individuals to solve attacking problems. Taking this approach means recruitment of top talent matters more than ever.\"He [gave] me the confidence and licence to go where I wanted on the pitch, where I [could] use my strengths,\" said Cole Palmer, who enjoyed his most productive Premier League season under Pochettino at Chelsea.United have good attackers but they will need to take the same initiative Palmer did, if platformed to their strengths - something Pochettino does well.If they don't, and if Pochettino is to stumble, it will likely be in United's inability to break down stubborn defences when asked to dominate the ball - in what would feel like Groundhog Day, again.",
