      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-13-global-microsoft-outage",
      "title": "Global Microsoft Outage",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/12/business/live-news/microsoft-global-outage-intl-hnk\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50479098\nPoints: 203\n# Comments: 116",
      "body": "Millions affected as Microsoft outage continues\nMillions affected as Microsoft outage continues\n• Making headway: Microsoft said it is seeing “encouraging recovery progress” in an hourslong outage that has affected millions around the world. The tech giant warns some users may still “experience intermittent issues.”\n• What’s been impacted: The outage, which began around noon ET on Thursday, left workers unable to access Outlook email, Teams meeting tools and other staples of modern office life. AI companies said they were facing issues as well. Banks, governments, health services, railways and courts all reported disruptions across Microsoft products. The outage was not caused by a cybersecurity breach, the company has said.\n• Global disruption: Outage reports “reached 74 countries and territories in about an hour,” according to Downdetector. While some workers scrambled to find workarounds, others expressed relief in a respite from the work grind.\nMicrosoft just said it is seeing “encouraging recovery progress” at the same time as some social media users and CNN journalists report receiving emails in their inboxes again.\nIn a statement on X, the company reported “email delivery performance improving across a growing portion of affected Microsoft 356 infrastructure,” but warned some users may still “experience intermittent issues.”\nWhile some social media users reported getting emails again, others said they were still unable to access them.\nSeveral UK police forces told the public Friday to report crimes or issues by telephone rather than through their websites amid the Microsoft outage.\nPolice forces in Merseyside, Greater Manchester and Bedfordshire informed visitors to their websites that online submission forms were disrupted.\nEast Hertfordshire Police said in a statement posted to X that “some online form submissions on our website may be affected” because of the outage.\nIn another Friday post on X, Dorset Police said it was “unable to receive online form submissions.”\nAll police forces urged the public to call 101 if their issue is “time-critical but not an emergency.” (The UK emergency number is 999).\nMicrosoft says it is making “additional progress toward recovery” and that traffic is “being gradually restored” as its engineers scramble to fix the worldwide outage that has left millions without the work tools they use every day.\nIn a statement, Microsoft said it is “seeing improvements in key scenarios, including mailbox access and delayed email processing, though some intermittent issues may continue.”\nOutlook is still down, as Microsoft’s rolling outage – now extending into a second day – continues to affect services for millions of people around the world.\nThe outage, which is also affecting Teams and Copilot, is disrupting government and business operations in the United States and elsewhere, with at least one company postponing a quarterly earnings call.\nMicrosoft executivesCharles Lamanna said in the early hours of Friday that there is currently no indication that the outage is the result “of malicious or cyber activity.”\nEmail down: Outlook remains down and users are also having trouble accessing their Exchange Online mailboxes.\nIn an update at around 4:15 a.m. ET Friday, Microsoft said it would extend its “email timeout window” to 48 hours “to help protect delayed messages while service recovery continues.”\nThe email timeout window is the maximum amount of time that a mail server or email client such as Outlook will spend waiting to receive an email before returning an error.\nAI companies affected: OpenAI said it was facing issues with Microsoft connectors, with some tools, including ChatGPT, not working “as expected.” Meanwhile, thousands of Claude AI users reported issues Friday morning, according to Downdetector. Claude creator Anthropic said shortly after 6 a.m. ET that a fix had been deployed and it was “monitoring for recovery.”\nUS lawmakers: The US House of Representatives said the outage impacted its users and its engineers were working with Microsoft, according to Reuters. Some congressional staffers expressed frustration on X. “Shall we start conducting all official business via DM?” said Sharon Eliza Nichols, communications director for Congresswoman Eleanor Holmes Norton.\nUS cities: City governments across the United States have reported email disruptions and have encouraged residents to call local offices and agencies if necessary.\nSeveral police and sheriff departments said the outage was affecting their email and communication platforms, including Colorado’s Arapahoe Sheriff’s Office, which said the outage had delayed the processing of prisoners’ bail payments.\nThe outages are affecting email services in Miami Beach, Austin, and Jacksonville, though officials assured residents that emergency services were still active.\nIn California, officials from Pasadena, Fremont, Burbank, and Santa Barbara also issued statements warning of downed email use.\nBaltimore County officials said on X that government staff could not send or receive emails and urged residents to contact relevant departments by phone.\nEarnings call delayed: Workhorse Group, a Detroit-based electric vehicle manufacturer, pushed its third quarter earnings call to Monday, citing “business disruptions caused by the global Microsoft outages.”\nCNN’s Gage Goulding contributed reporting.\nSome office workers rely on Microsoft’s suite of applications for practically everything – emails, meetings, AI, creating spreadsheets, and writing and sharing documents. Any of those functions which involve email or Microsoft’s Exchange Online services are impacted, the company said.\nOutlook: Email is the main thing impacted by the outage, leaving users likely unable to access or send any new emails.\nCopilot: Users may also be unable to load chats with or receive responses from Microsoft’s AI tool. Copilot also cannot access the data it normally draws from, like emails, files and calendars.\nTeams: Anything that has already been scheduled and requires Teams — Microsoft’s communications platform — to connect with a user’s calendar is likely impacted. But if users are simply just joining a call or exchanging messages, that is still working.\nWord/Excel/Powerpoint: The basic functions of these apps are still working. However, other functions may be impacted, like if users are trying to print documents over the cloud or share documents with Sharepoint. Users may also not be able to view their most recently used files.\nOther, more specialist Microsoft services – like those which pool a company’s data or allow IT to monitor a company’s devices – may also be affected.\nWhile many workers using Microsoft’s systems have spent Friday rejoicing that they’re forced to take a break — unable to send or receive emails or conduct their usual Teams calls — those using Google services are lamenting in memes shared on social media.\nGoogle systems including Gmail, Google Docs and Google Meet appear to be functioning just fine, or at least well enough to promise users an average working Friday.\nOne video on Instagram shows someone lying in bed with the word “Outlook” above their head. Another sits at a desk working on their laptop with the word “Gmail” above theirs.\nAnother video shows a meme of actor Pedro Pascal appearing to sob uncontrollably under text reading: “When you hear Outlook is down and start planning your afternoon wines… Then remember your workplace uses Google Workspace.”\nSome railway networks are experiencing disruptions to their customer email services and routing schedules because of the Microsoft outage.\nVIA Rail Canada said passengers “may not be able to receive email communications about their trip” due to technical issues with Microsoft services. The railway asked passengers to check their web-based train tracker or subscribe to SMS notifications for information on their journeys.\nGermany’s Deutsche Bahn said Microsoft Outlook is experiencing “severe sluggishness,” causing significant delays in its abilities to send out and receive emails.\n“As a result, the distribution of rerouting schedules is severely restricted. All relevant teams are involved in resolving the issue; current indications point to a server-side problem on Microsoft’s end,” it said in a statement.\nIn the UK, National Rail, which runs passenger rail services in England, Scotland and Wales, said the ongoing outage may be affecting customers’ ability to access train tickets by email.\nIt advised travelers to visit the website or app where tickets were purchased, or to contact the ticket retailer directly.\n“If you are still unable to access your ticket, please contact a member of onboard staff or station staff for advice,” National Rail said on its website.\nRail Delivery Group, which manages National Rail, said there were no current disruptions to operations or train services as a result of the outage.\nCNN’s Nina Avramova contributed to this report.\nMicrosoft said that it would extend its email timeout window to 48 hours “to help protect delayed messages while service recovery continues,” according to an update posted at around 4:15 a.m. ET Friday.\nThe email timeout window is the maximum amount of time that a mail server or email client such as Outlook will spend waiting to receive an email before returning an error.\nMicrosoft said recovery efforts are ongoing and that it’s seeing “early signs of stabilization,” though service capacity has not returned to normal.\nMemes and jokes abounded online as the global outage of Microsoft’s Outlook emails and Teams meeting platform crossed the 12-hour mark.\nOne joke that spread on Threads, X and LinkedIn was the rebrand of Microsoft365 to Microsoft364 – memorializing the one day of the year that the software company went dark.\nOthers reveled at not needing to respond to constant emails and notifications for at least one day.\nAnother account wrote: “Outlook is down. Your email does not find me well. It does not find me at all.”\nOthers on Reddit said they panicked and thought they were laid off when they could not access their work emails.\nAt first, Microsoft said the connection issues were happening “in the Americas,” but over time it became clear the troubles were global.\nMicrosoft’s outage reports “reached 74 countries and territories in about an hour,” according to Downdetector, a website that tracks cyber outages.\nHere’s where we’ve seen reports of outages:\nAustralian schools, courts and government services started their Friday morning with Microsoft Outlook and other apps out of service.\nIn Hong Kong, workers from banks including Morgan Stanley and Goldman Sachs told CNN their work was disrupted, as they were unable to access emails or Teams from the moment they signed on and all through their lunch break. “Sporadically some mails come in but there is probably millions of backlog to process,” one banker told CNN before heading off for lunch break.\nThe disruption has also hit Japan’s government services, Deputy Chief Cabinet Secretary Masanao Ozaki said in a Friday presser. Government employees are responding to the outage by creating physical copies of documents, he added. Government ministries and agencies have been unable to send emails through most of the Friday workday.\nCourts in the Philippines are also being slowed down by the Microsoft disruption, the Supreme Court said. “During this period, responses to emails and other transactions may be delayed,” it added in a post on X.\nIn the UK, a disruption to the National Health Service email system is expected to prevent some patients from getting their drug prescriptions.\nCNN’s Brian Stelter, Hilary Whiteman and Junko Ogura contributed to this report.\nA Microsoft outage causing disruptions to the email system of the UK’s National Health Service is expected to prevent some patients from getting their drug prescriptions Friday.\nThe outage “affects a pharmacy’s ability to communicate with GP practices and some patients,” a spokesperson for the National Pharmacy Association said in a Friday statement.\nGPs are equivalent to family doctors in the US.\nAn NPA spokesperson told CNN in a separate phone call that, while many patients will get their medications, those with more complex prescriptions requiring a pharmacist to contact the prescribing doctor to check or query any details could experience delays.\nThe spokesperson added that Fridays are typically busy days at pharmacies.\n“It’s just a massive pain. It will be slow today,” they said.\nMore than 12 hours after Microsoft first warned of a global outage affecting some of its most popular products, the company said it had “observed marginal success” in fixing the problem.\n“We’re continuing to monitor activity across regions to evaluate server status and determine actions that can be taken to improve capacity,” Microsoft said in a statement posted on its Service Health Status page early Friday.\n“At this point, we have observed marginal success,” the company wrote.\nAround 30 minutes later, Microsoft posted another statement saying the company has seen “positive results for service stability and increased capacity as a result of our latest traffic optimization actions.”\nReiterating what it said earlier Thursday, Microsoft stated it was not aware of any malicious activity causing the outage but will continue to treat the incident “with urgency and priority until full resolution.”\nThe Microsoft global outage is ongoing after the US tech giant first reported problems with its email services on Thursday.\nMore than 430 million people use Microsoft 365 apps and more than 90% of Fortune 500 companies use its Copilot AI chatbot, according to the company’s website.\nMillions worldwide lost access to Outlook email, Teams meeting tools and other essentials of modern office life as the disruption quickly spread.\nThe company reported “marginal success” in repairing the outage in its latest update, early Friday ET. A Microsoft executive confirmed the outage was not the result of a security breach.\nAs Europe woke on Friday, the outage remained widespread.\nHere’s the latest if you’re just tuning in:\nReturn to sender: The outage hit in 74 countries and territories in around an hour, according to tracking website Downdetector. Several airlines reported disruptions to their email support.\nIn the US: City governments countrywide reported widespread email disruptions and encouraged residents to call local offices and agencies if necessary. At least one American company postponed its quarterly earnings call.\nIn Asia Pacific: Some of Japan’s government services switched to paper documents due to the outage and courts in the Philippines experienced delays. Australian schools, courts and government services were also affected. In Hong Kong, staff at Morgan Stanley and Goldman Sachs told CNN their work was disrupted through to their lunch break.\nScams warning: Banks warned customers to beware of scams and phishing attempts as cybercriminals may use the outage to target individuals and businesses.\nCNN’s Helen Regan, Hilary Whiteman, Karina Tsui, Kathleen Magramo and Ross Adkin contributed reporting.\nSeveral airlines are reporting disruptions to their email support and communications due to the Microsoft outage, posting statements to customers on social media.\nIndian budget carrier IndiGo said its “email support services are currently impacted, and we may not be able to receive your emails.” It directed customers to reach out via their website or mobile app.\nAkasa Air, another low-cost Indian carrier, similarly said its “email support services are currently experiencing delays.”\nAnd South African carrier CemAir said it was “experiencing some system disruptions due to the global Microsoft outage.”\nNone of the carriers reported flight disruptions.\nIndian flag carrier Air India said it was “closely monitoring the ongoing Microsoft outage” and has rerouted emails. The airline said its flight operations “remain unaffected” and “there is currently no impact on any of our communication channels.”\n“Consider this your cue to plan that getaway.”\nThe Microsoft outage comes at a time when Europe is pushing for greater tech sovereignty, aiming to run its own servers and reduce dependency on foreign providers.\nEurope lacks the kind of mega tech companies that abound in the US, making it reliant on American firms for its digital services, but there is sentiment in Europe that it should become less reliant on the United States for its tech needs.\nThat sentiment has hardened during US President Donald Trump’s second term, when he has hit the continent with huge tariffs and threatened to take over the Danish territory of Greenland.\nEarlier this year French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu said that government officials will ditch Zoom and other US-owned video conferencing tools and begin using French-owned software. The decision is intended partly to reduce “dependencies on non-European actors,” Lecornu said.\n“The internet in Europe is essentially a US construct, a US system. And Europe doesn’t really have a competitor to any of that,” Neil Shearing, group chief economist at Capital Economics, told CNN earlier this year.\nOn Friday, that question resurfaced as Microsoft’s outage stretched beyond 12 hours, disrupting countries across the globe.\n“That tech sovereignty conversation, while it’s been mostly political, I bet it’s real sharp today,” technology journalist and founder of The Rip Current podcast Jacob Ward told CNN.\nCNN’s Anna Cooban contributed reporting.\nCity governments across the United States have reported widespread email disruptions since Microsoft’s global outage began and have encouraged residents to call local offices and agencies if necessary.\nThe outages are affecting inbound and outbound email services at local government offices across major cities, including Miami Beach, Austin, and Jacksonville, though officials assured residents that emergency services were still active.\nBaltimore County officials said on X that government staff could not send or receive emails and urged residents to contact relevant departments by phone.\nIn California, officials from the cities of Pasadena, Fremont, Burbank, and Santa Barbara also issued statements warning of downed email use.\nSeveral police and sheriff departments said the outage was affecting their email and communication platforms, including Colorado’s Arapahoe Sheriff’s Office, which said the outage had delayed bond payments for prisoners.\nBanks and financial institutions are warning customers to beware of scams and phishing attempts as cybercriminals may take advantage of the global Microsoft outage to target individuals and businesses.\n“Stay alert and protect yourself,” Standard Chartered Singapore said on social media. “A technology outage can create opportunities for scammers.”\nThe Bank of Queensland said that scammers may ask for personal details or One-Time Passwords and warned customers to “hang up immediately” if they receive a suspicious call.\nCustomers have been advised to not click on links or open attachments from unknown sources, and never share PINs or passwords. Other banks have told their customers to be cautious of anyone claiming to be tech support personnel, and to report unauthorized transactions and suspicious messages.\nTechnology journalist Jacob Ward told CNN that the “the ripple effects” from the outage could “go on and on.”\nThe Microsoft outage has caused at least one American company to postpone its quarterly earnings call.\nWorkhorse Group, a Detroit-based electric vehicle manufacturer, pushed their Q3 earnings call to Monday, citing the technology outage.\nEarnings calls are typically held virtually, either via a conference call or video webcast system, allowing company executives to discuss financial results with investors and analysts. Participants dial in by phone or join online.\nWorkhorse postponed its earnings call until Monday, November 16 at 4:30 p.m. ET.\nThe global Microsoft outage was not caused by a cybersecurity breach, a Microsoft executive confirmed late Thursday.\n“We will continue to share more information as soon as we have it, and for now I can confirm that there is no indication this is the result of malicious or cyber activity,” Charles Lamanna, executive vice president of Copilot, Agents, and Platform at Microsoft, said in a post on X.\nEarlier, Australia’s Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke said the company had told him that they “don’t believe they are dealing with a cyber attack.”\n“They believe they are dealing in some ways similar to what we saw with respect to CrowdStrike where there is an update that has gone awry,” Burke added during a press conference in Sydney.\nBurke was referring to the 2024 software glitch at cybersecurity firm CrowdStrike that crashed computers, canceled flights and disrupted hospitals all around the globe, causing billions of dollars in losses.\nAustralia’s spy agency and federal police were among the organizations hit by the Microsoft outage on Friday local time.\nThe Microsoft outage highlights the “inherent dangers” of companies that depend on a handful of tech giants for critical global infrastructure, an expert told CNN.\n“If one of those firms suffers a major outage, it has the potential to dramatically reduce business productivity globally during the outage time,” Computing Professor Toby Murray, of Australia’s University of Melbourne, said.\nMurray added that the outage affecting Outlook and other major services like Microsoft Teams is “incredibly widespread, and its ramifications extend well beyond being able to send and receive work emails,” making it difficult for businesses to operate.\nMark Gregory, Associate Professor at Australia’s RMIT University School of Engineering, also said the outage highlights how dependent businesses and government agencies have become on technology providers.\nit was “unthinkable” that a company like Microsoft could have a global outage after previous incidents.\nGregory was referring to the 2024 software glitch at cybersecurity firm CrowdStrike that crashed computers, canceled flights and disrupted hospitals all around the globe, causing billions of dollars in losses.\nHe highlighted the need for better contingency plans when critical services go offline.\nEn español: últimas noticias de la caída de Microsoft",
      "source": "Hacker News",
