    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-13-nyc-council-passes-ban-on-surveillance-pricing",
      "title": "NYC Council passes ban on 'surveillance pricing'",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://gothamist.com/news/nyc-council-passes-ban-on-surveillance-pricing-that-relies-on-personal-data\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50477155\nPoints: 28\n# Comments: 8",
      "body": "The City Council passed a bill Thursday that bans companies in New York City from using surveillance pricing, which charges different prices based on algorithms or shoppers’ personal data.Surveillance pricing means shoppers see a price based on their personal data like demographics, credit, browsing history or purchasing history. The bill prohibits surveillance pricing both online and in stores, although the practice is mainly used on the web.Speaker Julie Menin described surveillance pricing as an “insidious and predatory corporate practice” that erodes privacy and fairness.“It's a practice that worsens the affordability crisis, and today it's a practice that we are going to be ending,” she said during a press conference ahead of the vote.In one investigation cited by the Council, Target shoppers saw a jump in pricing based on location data showing whether they were inside or outside a store. Shoppers found that a 55-inch SmartTV cost $499 on the Target app when inside a store, and $599 when in the store parking lot.Uber, Lyft, Target, Instacart and airlines are among the companies that have faced scrutiny for alleged surveillance pricing. Many of the companies have denied using the practice.Under the bill, the city would be able to issue fines of up to $1,000 for each transaction that violates the ban.Menin’s proposal follows legislation approved by Albany lawmakers in June that prohibits surveillance pricing online and in stores, with some exceptions.Mayor Zohran Mamdani said in a statement that he supports the ban. He called surveillance pricing “just another way corporations are finding to squeeze working people.”“New Yorkers’ personal data should never be used as a weapon against their own pocketbooks,” he added.Business groups have warned that the ban could hinder discounts or loyalty programs for customers.“If enacted, this law will make it harder for small restaurants with limited marketing budgets to reach customers, putting them at a disadvantage against national chains,” said Josh Gold, a spokesperson for Uber.The company operates Uber Eats, which allows restaurants to offer discounts based on search history and previous orders.Menin said the legislation would not affect discounts.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "jonbaer",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T02:04:39.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://api-prod.gothamist.com/images/362447/fill-1200x650|format-webp|webpquality-85/",
      "originalUrl": "https://gothamist.com/news/nyc-council-passes-ban-on-surveillance-pricing-that-relies-on-personal-data",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-13-zelensky-welcomes-us-bringing-europe-into-russia-ukraine-pea",
      "title": "Zelensky welcomes US bringing Europe into Russia-Ukraine peace talks",
      "summary": "The Ukrainian president says it makes sense for European leaders to be involved as Ukraine hopes to eventually join the EU.",
      "body": "Zelensky has for several years been calling for European delegates to be included in the peace talks, and says it makes sense because of Kyiv's longer-term ambition to join the European Union. On Thursday, Kushner said that the US has been \"working very, very closely over the past several months with Europeans and I think that that's been very important.\"He added that he had stressed to Putin how important it was to have European delegates \"at the table\". \"He's willing to give it a try,\" Kushner added.Ukraine and Europe have long been concerned about the current US government's commitment to European security and its softening towards the Kremlin.Moscow has been dismissive of European involvement, although Europeans have long argued that they are central to any post-war dynamics on their continent.Earlier on Thursday, the EU's foreign policy chief, Kaja Kallas, told the BBC's Newshour the presence of a European delegate showed the Kremlin \"now realised that Europe is a big player\".Kallas also expressed doubts that there was Russian momentum towards a peace deal.Taoiseach (Irish Prime Minister) Micheál Martin told a host of European leaders gathered in Dublin on Thursday that he believed the security of the region had been \"fundamentally reshaped\" by Russia's war.\"Many member states [of the EU] feel an existential threat because of what has happened in Ukraine and the potential, if Ukraine was to fall, for further aggression on the European continent,\" he told reporters.Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine began in February 2022. Approximately one-fifth of Ukraine is now under Russian occupation and Kyiv has refused Putin's demand to give up areas of Ukraine's eastern Donbas that remain under its control.Tensions between the US and Ukraine were on display on Thursday before Witkoff and Kushner's meeting with Zelensky.Earlier, the Ukrainian leader said that the military was facing more \"systematic\" restrictions to Starlink's military-grade satellite service, Starshield, meaning Kyiv was struggling to intercept Russian jet-powered drones.Starshield technology, which was developed by Elon Musk's SpaceX company for government and military use, has played a vital role in facilitating communication on Ukraine's front line.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T02:01:35.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/9462/live/a60e9f80-df2b-11f1-a701-fddedf244c16.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckn80j9jv3jxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
