      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-12-muse-can-now-place-calls-to-us-businesses-if-you-pay",
      "title": "Muse can now place calls to US businesses — if you pay",
      "summary": "Muse's AI can now place outbound calls to U.S. businesses — but it's not free.",
      "body": "After initially testing the feature in September, Meta’s consumer AI agent Muse is now able to place calls to U.S. businesses on behalf of its users. However, only paid subscribers have access to the feature, the company says.\nMeta’s Muse, which now has over 10 million users, the company said last month, has been working to make consumer AI a mainstream product for everyday users. Placing calls is one of the ways it aims to get there, as it could handle another task on people’s to-do lists.\nNotes Alexandr Wang, Meta’s chief AI officer and head of its Superintelligence Labs division, the feature could be used to take care of calls people don’t want to make, like those to their cable company, the DMV, or to a restaurant that doesn’t offer online reservations.\n🚨MUSE FEATURE ALERT!!!🚨lots of you have been asking for this one…muse can make phone calls for you! ☎️📞📲let muse take care of all those calls you don’t want to make. insurance? cable? DMV? dinner res? not your problem anymore.out now to paid subs in the US 🇺🇸 pic.twitter.com/dsyMNmI2Ub— Alexandr Wang (@alexandr_wang) November 12, 2026\nPreviously, only a select number of Muse early adopters were able to try this feature after asking the AI for access. Now, anyone who pays for Muse can make this type of request. Currently, Muse subscription plans start at $20 per month for 500 million Muse tokens per week. Heavier AI users can opt to pay $100 per month for 3 billion tokens.\nMuse is not the only AI agent able to place calls to businesses; the startup Instinct has been offering a similar option, as has Google’s Gemini. However, while the AI tools are now certainly capable of handling the task, the reception on the other end of the line can be mixed. Many businesses and receptionists hang up on calls from AI agents as soon as they identify that it’s not a human on the phone. But with AI now better able to mimic a real human voice, being able to tell the difference between a customer and an AI working on the customer’s behalf will get tougher.\nDespite the challenges, this feature is one that could appeal to consumers who have grown frustrated with the state of customer service over the years. For too long, customers have been forced to navigate complex phone trees, and then sit on hold waiting for a human representative to pick up. If they’re lucky, that person can actually help them, and not transfer them to another department. Now, those consumers could potentially use their AI, with its infinite patience, for this tedious task instead.\nWhile calls remain a paid-only feature for the time being, the recently added option to make a Muse email address will soon roll out to free users, Wang said this week.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nSarah has worked as a reporter for TechCrunch since August 2011. She joined the company after having previously spent over three years at ReadWriteWeb. Prior to her work as a reporter, Sarah worked in I.T. across a number of industries, including banking, retail and software.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Sarah by emailing [email protected] or via encrypted message at sarahperez.01 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Sarah Perez",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-12T22:22:28.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/muse-calls.jpg?w=1104",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/12/muse-can-now-place-calls-to-u-s-businesses-if-you-pay/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-12-grealish-apologises-for-absence-after-breach-of-discipline",
      "title": "Grealish apologises for absence after breach of discipline",
      "summary": "Jack Grealish issues an apology to Everton fans for his recent absence due to a disciplinary issue.",
      "body": "Jack Grealish has issued an apology to Everton fans for his recent absence due to a disciplinary issue.The on-loan Manchester City midfielder missed the Toffees' two games against Newcastle United in the Premier League and Carabao Cup, as well as the win against Coventry prior to the international break, following a \"breach of club discipline\".But he returned to action for the first time on Thursday, featuring in a behind-closed-doors victory against Championship side Derby County.The 31-year-old posted an apology to the club and fans social media, saying he \"can't wait to be back playing in front of you.\"\"Nice to be back out there with the boys today after a good few days training,\" Grealish wrote on his Instagram story.\"I want to apologise to the fans and everyone at the club for not being able to help the team the last few weeks. \"I love playing for this club and can't wait to be back playing in front of you guys in an Everton shirt. See you soon.\"Before Everton's game against Coventry, manager David Moyes was asked if the international break would give Everton the opportunity to resolve the issue with Grealish, to which he said: \"We just need to wait and see how things go.\" Grealish is trying to rebuild his career at Everton, having fallen out of favour at City despite costing the club £100m in 2021.He scored twice in 22 games for Everton last season during a loan spell which was cut short by injury.The midfielder returned to the club for a second loan spell this season but had only made six appearances before falling out of favour.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-12T22:10:13.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/6ba4/live/6d670f60-dedf-11f1-a75a-dbf83836672c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cr3wgp8e2ljxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
