    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-12-where-does-kane-rank-among-all-time-england-greats",
      "title": "Where does Kane rank among all-time England greats?",
      "summary": "Harry Kane is England's all-time top scorer and record appearance maker, but where does he rank among the Three Lions' greatest players?",
      "body": "ByAlex BysouthBBC Sport senior journalistHarry Kane is now England's most-capped men's player as well as his country's record goalscorer, but harder to quantify is where these achievements place him among the Three Lions' greatest ever players.This is a tricky debate with so many variables and a timescale as old as the game itself and, to my mind, not merely a determination of a player's individual ability but also a measure of their impact upon the nation's psyche.Ballon d'Or winners Stanley Matthews, Kevin Keegan and Michael Owen would surely have a shout if the criteria was such, not to mention genius technicians such as Glenn Hoddle, Chris Waddle and John Barnes.Likewise, if any England boss managed to extract the best from Paul Scholes, Steven Gerrard and Frank Lampard they would be surely making a classic late dart from midfield. But to qualify as one of the greatest England players of all time, you need the above ability combined with an impactful overall contribution - those memorable moments and an element of longevity - in a Three Lions shirt.It might be controversial then to leave out Geoff Hurst, who produced the most recognisable performance in England history, while the man he replaced, Jimmy Greaves, would be a shoo-in were it not for injury earlier in the tournament.It is also hard to rank the contribution of those whose best football came outside of major tournaments and before the dawn of continental competitions - see Eddie Hapgood, Tom Finney, Nat Lofthouse, Dixie Dean et al.There are only 10 spots, after all, so here we go.10. David BeckhamSpice Boy. Celebrity. Galactico. There is no argument David Beckham is one of the most iconic England players of all time, but he backed it up on the pitch, too.Beckham was the face of the Three Lions for a generation, and to his credit managed to overcome the vicious fallout of his red card against Argentina in 1998.At times he seemed to single-handedly carry England, most notably scoring a 93rd-minute free-kick against Greece to qualify for the 2002 World Cup.Beckham's former team-mate Gary Neville called him the \"most influential player out of England in transforming football\".It's true. On top of 115 caps, Beckham was part of Manchester United's 1999 Treble-winning outfit - finishing Ballon d'Or runner-up - before joining Real Madrid.His profile was truly global with spells at LA Galaxy, AC Milan and Paris St-Germain.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, David Beckham scored 17 goals for England, including five free-kicks9. Paul GascoigneThere's arguably no England star fans forged an emotional attachment with more than Gazza - crying with him in 1990, rejoicing in his spectacular effort against Scotland and desperately willing him to connect during that Euro 96 semi-final defeat by Germany.Gascoigne won 57 caps and scored 10 goals. At his peak, he was captivating - transcending football as a cultural phenomenon during the summers of '90 and '96, having joined a star-studded Serie A between those tournaments.Creative, unpredictable, vulnerable. He was a loveable but flawed genius.He suffered long-term injuries as England missed out on Euro 92 and the World Cup in the USA, but helped the Three Lions qualify for France 98 before being dropped from the final squad, famously wrecking boss Glenn Hoddle's office.Figure caption, Gascoigne, Poborsky , McCoist - Goals that defined Euro 968. Billy WrightGoalscorers often grab the headlines but I've tried to credit players across positions and eras. From a defensive point of view Ashley Cole, arguably England's best-ever full-back, was close to making it.But instead it's the great Billy Wright, the first men's player to surpass a century of international caps, including a remarkable 70 successive appearances over eight years.Wright captained his country on 90 occasions, a joint-record recently broken by Kane, taking in three World Cups with impressive longevity for his era.He was a true one-club man, winning three league titles, the FA Cup and becoming unofficial \"world champions\" with Wolves in 1954 - helping inspire the birth of the European Cup - while finishing Ballon d'Or runner-up to Alfredo di Stefano in 1957.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Billy Wright captained England 90 times - a record shared with Bobby Moore until Harry Kane recently surpassed the pair7. Jude BellinghamIt feels a little premature to include a 23-year-old with the bulk of his England career ahead of him, but that shows the impact Jude Bellingham has already made for England and Real Madrid.A Champions League winner, the midfielder has also appeared at four major tournaments for the Three Lions, becoming the youngest player to do so at the delayed Euro 2020, aged 17.But his role in helping carry England to the final two years ago and a third-place finish at last summer's World Cup really make him stand out.Bellingham has delivered those moments a nation collectively remembers. The last-gasp overhead kick against Slovakia in 2024, a quickfire double at the Azteca against Mexico this summer and 93rd-minute quarter-final winner against Norway.His seven goals at this World Cup were more than any previous Three Lions player in a single edition, the solo effort against France a thing of beauty.The former Birmingham and Dortmund man doesn't turn 24 until June but has already collected 61 England caps and netted 14 times. As Roy Keane put it, Bellingham has the potential to be one of the \"greatest of all time\".Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Ten of Bellingham's 14 England goals have come at major tournaments6. Wayne RooneyIn terms of natural ability, Wayne Rooney is among England's greatest ever talents.Bursting on to the scene at Euro 2004 as an electric 18-year-old, having become England's youngest player and scorer the previous year, convinced fans and media the teen sensation could carry the Three Lions to glory in Portugal.He bounced World Cup winner Lilian Thuram around in England's opener against France in a glimpse of his precocious strength, before scoring twice against both Switzerland and Croatia.Who knows how far England would have gone had Rooney not been injured against Portugal in the quarter-final?He was unable to replicate the impact of his first Euros - a red card at World Cup 2006, suspension for the Euro 2012 group stage, seven goals across six major tournaments - but was an all-rounder who brought more than just goals.Rooney also won it all with Manchester United, becoming the club's all-time top scorer, and finished his 15-year England career with 53 goals from 120 caps.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Rooney burst on to the scene as an 18-year-old at Euro 20045. Gordon BanksThere has to be a place for a goalkeeper, and Gordon Banks is the safest pair of hands.A World Cup winner in 1966, his save from Pele four years later against Brazil is one of the most iconic of all time. Pele called him a \"goalkeeper with magic\".Banks spent the majority of his career at Leicester and Stoke, winning a League Cup with each, and earned 73 England caps overall between 1963 and 1972.His incredible athleticism and reflexes saw the shot-stopper named Fifa's best goalkeeper for five successive years between 1966 and 1971.Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Banks and Pele became good friends following the goalkeeper's iconic save in 19704. Gary LinekerGary Lineker's early club career did not deliver the silverware as his goals deserved, netting 40 times but finishing league and FA Cup runner-up in his one season at Everton before joining Barcelona.He scored a Clasico hat-trick and won the Copa del Rey and Cup Winner's Cup, however, before adding an FA Cup with Tottenham in 1991.Lineker netted 283 club goals (and famously no cards) in total across spells with Leicester, Everton, Barcelona, Spurs and Nagoya Grampus Eight.But as a player Lineker is most renowned for his contribution in an England shirt, winning the Golden Boot as they reached the World Cup quarter-finals in 1986 - finishing Ballon d'Or runner-up that year - before netting another four as the Three Lions reached the semi-finals in 1990.Lineker equalised against West Germany before England crashed out on penalties.However, having missed a spot-kick against Brazil, he finished one short of Bobby Charlton's elusive goal record when his international career ended after Euro 92.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Gary Lineker scored 48 goals in 80 games for England3. Harry KaneHarry Kane is undoubtedly England's most important and influential player of a generation - it is hard to imagine how this Three Lions side functions once the skipper hangs up his boots.The Bayern Munich striker, complete with Ballon d'Or, has been scoring goals for his country for almost 12 years, the first just 79 seconds into his debut after coming off the bench against Lithuania in March 2015.Rooney's goalscoring benchmark is long in his rear view mirror - you wouldn't bank against Kane doubling his nearest rival's figure once his England days are over.They have come on the biggest stage, too - 21 in 36 appearances from three World Cups and European Championships apiece, winning the World Cup Golden Boot in 2018.Kane has come closer to winning a major tournament than any England men's captain since Bobby Moore - losing two World Cup semi-finals and back-to-back Euros finals. Team-mate Bellingham called him the \"greatest player that has ever played for England\".\"I think we'll appreciate him even more in another 10, 15, 20 years when you look back and see what he has achieved,\" said ex-England goalkeeper Paul Robinson.2. Bobby MooreIn many ways, Bobby Moore would be the complete modern-day defender. Cool as you like under pressure, elegant in possession, a great passing range off both feet and, as his West Ham and England team-mate Geoff Hurst said: \"Nobody out-ran him and nobody out-jumped him.\"Moore's positioning and timing was perfect, that iconic tackle on Jairzinho at the 1970 World Cup the finest example of his poise and precision when defending.He was also a composed and influential leader. A player built for the big occasions. England boss Sir Alf Ramsey called him: \"My lieutenant on the field, a cool, calculated footballer I could trust with my life.\"The Barking-born centre-back won the FA Cup and European Cup Winners' Cup with West Ham but his finest moment was leading England to glory in 1966. He was only 25 at the time, having become his country's youngest captain three years earlier.In 1970, after the Bogota bracelet incident, Moore was imperious as England reached the quarter-finals before bowing out to West Germany.It was, however, the final group game against Brazil, the most exciting side on the planet, that produced some of the most recognisable and renowned images in sporting history - not only Moore's tackle and Banks' save but the defender swapping shirts with Pele after the final whistle.\"The greatest defender I ever played against,\" was the Brazilian's verdict.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Bobby Moore is the only man to captain the Three Lions to World Cup glory1. Sir Bobby CharltonFor more than a generation, Sir Bobby's England goalscoring record remained unmatched. It took 55 years from his 49th and final strike against Colombia in 1970 until Rooney surpassed him with his half century in 2015.Charlton was, as Alan Shearer put it, \"not only a great goalscorer but a scorer of great goals\" and played a pivotal part of the Three Lions' World Cup success in 1966, netting three times on the way to the final.A scampering run from the halfway line followed by a trademark long-range strike into the corner at Wembley gave England's campaign lift-off against Mexico, before he scored twice against Eusebio's Portugal in the semi-finals.Manager Ramsey called the buccaneering midfielder his \"lynchpin\" as England triumphed over West Germany in the showpiece and those performances helped Charlton win the Ballon d'Or later that year.He'd be runner-up in successive years after that, too.Charlton won 106 England caps and was a star for Manchester United, with Rooney also the one to pip his club-record 249 goals for the Old Trafford outfit.A survivor of the Munich air disaster on 6 February 1958, which killed 23 people including eight players and three members of the club's staff, Charlton won three league titles, an FA Cup and most poignantly the European Cup in 1968.\"England's greatest ever player,\" Lineker previously told BBC Radio 5 Live.\"He was unique. Wherever you go in the world, even if they didn't speak the language, they knew two words - Bobby Charlton.\"Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Charlton's England goal record stood for more than half a centuryMore on this story",
