    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-12-duran-duran-drummer-quits-band-due-to-tinnitus",
      "title": "Duran Duran drummer quits band due to tinnitus",
      "summary": "Roger Taylor says his condition has been getting steadily worse, and he must now \"listen to my body and my doctors\".",
      "body": "Duran Duran drummer Roger Taylor said he will leave the band due to his worsening tinnitus.Taylor has been the chart-topping group's drummer since they formed in Birmingham in 1978, which he described as \"the joy of my life\", but said he had \"reached the point where I must now listen to my body and my doctors\".The 66-year-old said his condition has been getting steadily worse, leading to him making one of his hardest decisions to quit the band.The group, made up of Simon Le Bon, keyboardist Nick Rhodes and guitarist John Taylor, said his decision came after \"careful consideration and medical advice\", which said that \"protecting his hearing and long-term health must now come first\".Taylor, who left the band in 1985 before returning in 2001, said: \"Playing drums in this band has been the joy of my life, and I'll never be able to properly thank the fans for the love they've shown us over the years.\"But the truth is that my tinnitus has been getting steadily worse,\" he said.He said deciding on the timing had been difficult but he was pleased to be \"going out on a real high\".\"We've just returned from a hugely successful UK and European tour, which followed our second headline show at BST Hyde Park, our induction into the Rock & Roll Hall Of Fame, and a return to the studio with Nile Rodgers.\"I couldn't have asked for a better final chapter, with so many special moments that I'll treasure forever. \"I move on with enormous pride, and with nothing but love for Simon, Nick and John,\" he said.Taylor's band members added that he had helped define the sound of the group, performing on 10 hit albums, their two number one singles - Is There Something I Should Know? and The Reflex - and the band's 1985 James Bond theme A View To A Kill.\"Roger is our brother,\" they said.\"His drumming helped define the sound of Duran Duran from the very beginning and having him back with us since 2001 has been incredibly important to us all.\"We completely support his decision and wish him health and happiness.\"He will always be part of this family, while we continue exploring new and exciting projects with the band.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-12T19:00:15.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3306/live/40459310-debe-11f1-8fb5-a771a656a227.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crwy499k504do?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-11-12-man-jailed-for-appalling-catalogue-of-coercive-control-and-a",
      "title": "Man jailed for 'appalling' catalogue of coercive control and abuse against partner",
      "summary": "John Gizzi, 55, frequently assaulted Natasha Farah, 32, and sent her abusive messages.",
      "body": "Warning: This article contains details and references to suicide and abusive behaviourA man subjected his partner to an \"appalling\" catalogue of abuse, including beating and abusive messages, which the judge said was \"one of the worst\" cases of coercive control the court had seen.John Gizzi, 55, from St Asaph, Denbighshire, was jailed for four years and 11 months after pleading guilty to coercive and controlling behaviour towards Natasha Farah, 32, and for intentional strangulation of her sister.There were shouts of \"scumbag\" and \"rot in hell\" from the public gallery towards Gizzi who appeared at the hearing via video link from prison.Farah's mother said she would campaign in her daughter's name for sentences around coercive and controlling behaviour to be longer.At Mold Crown Court, some people called out that the sentence was too short, until they were asked to be quiet by a court usher.The court heard that a catalogue of physical and verbal abuse of Farah by Gizzi was unearthed after she made a suspected suicide attempt that left her with severe brain damage.In his sentencing remarks, Judge Mills said Farah and her family and friends had faced an \"appalling tragedy\" and said the case of coercive control was one of the worst the court had seen, telling Gizzi he had subjected her to \"appalling\" behaviour.Judge Mills said Gizzi had used \"dreadful misogynistic language\" towards Farah which was \"part of the process of humiliation\" and \"destroying\" her self worth.Farah's mother, wearing a T-shirt saying Justice for Natasha, read a lengthy victim impact statement, saying she wanted to detail the \"sheer devastating\" impact Gizzi's behaviour had had and the \"pain, grief, anger, torture\" he had inflicted.She described her daughter as \"the most beautiful, funniest, kindest soul in the world\" and told Gizzi, who was appearing by video link, that he was a \"smiling devil monster\".Judge Mills said Natasha was often seen to have bruises, and was once assaulted by Gizzi with a dumbbell.\"You would accuse her of sleeping with other men… assaulted her frequently… sometimes in front of other people… you assaulted her in front of her child,\" he said.The judge also said Gizzi had assaulted her on Valentine's Day and Mother's Day.\"She would plead with you to desist,\" he added. He added that a domestic abuse protection order that had been sought did not go ahead because Natasha had asked the magistrates not to proceed, and she \"had a note which appeared to tell her what to say\".He said that when Farah's sister Samantha had challenged Gizzi, his response had been violent. \"You gripped her neck with your hands, she fell to floor... there were marks on her neck.\"Earlier the court heard further details of what had happened prior to 7 April when Farah was found having attempted suicide at her flat in Rhos on Sea by Gizzi, who phoned the emergency services. Elen Owen, prosecuting, the told the court it was not the Crown's case that Gizzi was responsible, but the discovery of Farah led to an investigation that unearthed a catalogue of physical and verbal abuse of her by Gizzi. Owen outlined a long list of attacks and abuse inflicted by Gizzi on Farah between March 2025 and April 2026. It included an attack on her at a gym the pair used when she arrived to ask for some keys, which Gizzi threw at her and hit her in the face. Another man stepped in to protect her. Farah told a friend of a second physical assault at the same gym.He repeatedly followed her, attacked her in her car and left countless messages using abusive language. In September 2025, Farah was found after another apparent suicide attempt, and a call to the emergency services recorded Gizzi physically and verbally abusing Farah's mother, Denise.The police were informed but Denise later withdrew the complaint.Some of his abuse was caught on public CCTV cameras, and following one such incident in January 2025, Gizzi was arrested. Police tried to get a domestic protection order but Farah decided not to co-operate and magistrates declined to make the order.Her sister Samantha witnessed repeated abuse and was herself attacked by Gizzi at a pub in Colwyn Bay - that led to a charge of strangulation, one of three Gizzi pleaded guilty to at a hearing last month.The former drug gang boss also pleaded guilty to refusing to give police the pin so they could access his phone.Messages found on it included a stream of abuse and messages from Farah saying he had made her feel \"worthless\" and had \"broken\" and \"crushed\" her. Referring to the assaults on Boxing Day and Valentine's Day, Farah told Gizzi: \"I don't have one good memory with you\". He responded with an abusive message. In a message where she referred to him kicking her leg, he replied: \"I couldn't reach your face\".Judge Mills told the court that he must recognise the events of 7 April that had left Farah \"in an unresponsive state with serious brain damage\"  but said there was no offence in the indictment making Gizzi directly responsible.He added that he had \"no doubt\" that if \"circumstances were to change or further evidence came to light\" that would be investigated by North Wales Police but it was \"not a matter for today\".The judge told Gizzi he was \"never going to be forgiven for what people believe you did to her\" and said that would \"follow\" him through his life.  He added, to applause from the public gallery, that people who had been afraid of him \"were no longer\".Outside court, friends and family of Farah gathered to express their anger at Gizzi's sentence, with her mother Denise saying she would continue to campaign in her daughter's name for sentences around coercive and controlling behaviour to be longer. She described violence against women and girls as an \"epidemic\".Gizzi also received a concurrent four-month sentence for failing to supply the pin number for his mobile device to the police after his arrest.For support and more information on emotional distress, help is available on the BBC Action Line.",
