      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-12-jensen-huang-s-exit-puts-nvidia-s-support-for-open-source-in",
      "title": "Jensen Huang’s exit puts Nvidia’s support for open source in question",
      "summary": "Jensen Huang’s resignation raises questions about Nvidia’s commitment to open source AI, including its Hugging Face acquisition, open-weight models, and advocacy against restrictions, as the company navigates a leadership transition.",
      "body": "The open source community just lost one of its strongest advocates.\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang resigned Thursday after he admitted to an affair with an alleged Chinese spy, raising concerns about U.S. national security and the motives behind the executive’s support for softer chip export controls on China.\nHuang has been a staunch supporter of open source AI, framing the ecosystem as good for safety, innovation, diffusion, and sovereignty. And as frontier AI labs increasingly move to reduce their reliance on Nvidia and build their own chips, supporting open source is also a good way for the company to diversify its customer base by selling chips to nation-states, enterprises, and any organization that wants to own its own models.\nStill, Huang’s resignation raises questions about whether Nvidia will carry on his legacy by continuing to support the open model community.\nJensen no longer being CEO would be very very bad for the open model cause in the US.— Nathan Lambert (@natolambert) November 11, 2026\nHuang’s departure also comes as Nvidia has yet to finalize a deal to acquire open source platform Hugging Face for $13 billion. Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue said in a post on X that the company would continue to fight for open source, whatever happens next.\nNvidia also agreed to pay $6 billion for a non-exclusive license for open AI coding startup Poolside’s technology.\nWithin Nvidia, Huang pushed for the development of the Nemotron family of open-weight AI models built for long-running agentic AI systems and reasoning workloads — potential competitors to closed frontier models. One company insider told TechCrunch that they don’t expect a change in leadership to have an impact on Nemotron development.\nHuang also backed companies that would make American open models more competitive, investing in startups like Reflection AI and Thinking Machines Lab.\nHuang’s influence extended beyond Nvidia’s investments and product roadmap. He also emerged as one of the most prominent voices pushing back against Washington’s efforts to restrict the open AI ecosystem.\nEarlier this year, Huang wrote an open letter signed by 25 companies, including Microsoft, Meta, IBM, and Palantir, urging the U.S. government not to over-restrict open-weight AI models, many of which come from China.\nThe impetus for that letter was Washington’s debate over how the U.S. should respond to allegations that Chinese AI labs are stealing intellectual property from American counterparts like Anthropic and OpenAI, both of which have reportedly appealed directly to Washington on the matter, urging stronger protections.\nThat combination of Huang’s personal visibility and Nvidia’s market power made him the strongest counterweight to arguments from closed-model providers like Anthropic and OpenAI, which have advocated for greater restrictions on advanced AI models due to safety reasons.\nWithout Huang, the concern among open source community members is that whoever takes over will be more cautious. Nvidia has named CFO Colette Kress as interim CEO. It’s not clear how Kress will approach the Hugging Face deal or support for the open model ecosystem, but founder-driven priorities tend to get a downgrade when leadership changes.\nHuang’s exit could also strengthen the arguments of those in Washington who are arguing for tougher restrictions on Chinese AI companies, particularly as the scandal surrounding his relationship with Alice Huang, the Megaspeed executive and alleged spy, puts renewed scrutiny on his efforts to loosen chip export controls.\nRestrictions on open-weight models from China could kneecap competition for American companies, the most powerful of which have kept their models closed.\nStill, supporting open source isn’t simply a passion project Huang pursued out of idealism. It’s increasingly central to Nvidia’s business strategy.\nAs some of Nvidia’s biggest customers, including Google and OpenAI, develop their own chips, Nvidia has an incentive to cultivate a much broader ecosystem of AI developers and customers.\nMore players training, fine-tuning, and running models mean more GPUs sold, and less dependence on a handful of frontier labs.\nHuang may be gone, but Nvidia still needs an open AI ecosystem. The question now is whether that commitment can survive without the man who made it a priority.\nTechCrunch has reached out to Nvidia for comment.\nThis article has been updated with information from an Nvidia employee.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nRebecca Bellan is a senior reporter at TechCrunch where she covers the business, policy, and emerging trends shaping artificial intelligence. Her work has also appeared in Forbes, Bloomberg, The Atlantic, The Daily Beast, and other publications.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Rebecca by emailing [email protected] or via encrypted message at rebeccabellan.491 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Rebecca Bellan",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-12T18:39:23.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-2290097570.jpg?w=1024",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/12/jensen-huangs-exit-puts-nvidias-support-for-open-source-in-question/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-12-rust-1-100",
      "title": "Rust 1.100",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://blog.rust-lang.org/2026/11/12/Rust-1.100.0/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50472038\nPoints: 750\n# Comments: 278",
      "body": "The Rust team is happy to announce a new version of Rust, 1.100.0. Rust is a programming language empowering everyone to build reliable and efficient software.\nIf you have a previous version of Rust installed via rustup, you can get 1.100.0 with:\nIf you don't have it already, you can get rustup from the appropriate page on our website, and check out the detailed release notes for 1.100.0.\nIf you'd like to help us out by testing future releases, you might consider updating locally to use the beta channel (rustup default beta) or the nightly channel (rustup default nightly). Please report any bugs you might come across!\nReporting unused dependencies in Cargo\nAfter 10 years and 5 failed stabilization attempts, the never type is finally stable! This also completes the project goal for this work.\nThe never type, spelled ! in code, is the canonical uninhabited type. It represents the type of diverging computations – computations which never resolve to any value – where any code that follows is unreachable. Expressions that terminate control flow, such as return, break, and std::process::exit, also have the type !.\nSince having a value of the never type is impossible, Rust permits a never-to-any coercion, allowing to turn ! into any other type (this is what distinguishes it from any other uninhabited types). This can be used, for example, to not return a value from one of the branches of a match or an if:\nlet value = match option {\n// `x` has type `u32`\n// `return` has type `!`, which is then coerced to `u32`,\n// allowing the `match` to pass type checking.\nWhile the never type could already be used and observed this way, and even named in the return type of functions (-> !), this release allows naming the never type anywhere:\nfn infallible_unwrap<T>(res: Result<T, !>) -> T {\n// ^^ writing `Result<_, !>` was not allowed before\nAdditionally this release changes Infallible to be an alias to the never type. That makes all old APIs that used Infallible be compatible with using !. This was the plan ever since the introduction of Infallible and is one of the reasons why the never type stabilization was so hard (waffle gave a talk about some of the troubles of the stabilization).\nUnfortunately, to allow changing Infallible, we had to introduce a breaking change. This release changes the \"never type fallback\" from () to ! in editions before 2024 (i.e. 2015, 2018, and 2021), similarly to what has already been done in edition 2024. This should largely be unnoticeable, but can affect code in some rare cases (for example when ignoring a result of a function returning a generic parameter). Note that this change might also make the compiler detect more code as unreachable.\nWe tried to minimize the impact of this breaking change as much as possible, by providing future compatibility warnings far in advance and releasing fixes to public crates that have been broken by this. If a dependency is failing due to issues with the never type, consider updating it, as a fix might have already been released for it.\nThis release brings about the stabilization of the core parts of allocator_api, enabling library authors to define custom allocators that can be used together with certain standard library types - currently Vec and Box. These are enabled through the newly-stabilized Allocator trait.\nThis is the foundation of the Allocators 1.0 project goal. A lot remains to be done still, including expanding the API surface usable with custom allocators and stabilizing their use on more collections and pointer types; with the core trait and design out of the way though, expect to see more being stabilized soon!\nDropGuard: run a closure on every exit path\nThe Drop trait in Rust makes it possible to run code when a variable goes out of scope, regardless of whether we return, ?, or panic. Drop being a part of types means that in Rust you never have to close a socket.\nImplementing Drop can however feel a little inconvenient when all you want is to run some code unconditionally when a function exits. This is why we’re introducing core::mem::DropGuard: a new helper type that runs a closure when dropped.\nDropGuard takes two arguments: a variable and a closure. It implements Deref and DerefMut so you can keep working with the variable as normal. But when dropped it runs the closure and consumes the variable.\nDropGuard also implements a dismiss method which returns the variable from the guard without running the destructor. This makes it possible to quickly implement all-or-nothing transaction-like APIs with a rollback. Here is an example implementing a parser which uses a guard to roll back mutations in case of failure:\n/// Attempt to parse all `strings` and push them into `nums`.\n/// If parsing any of them fails, `nums` remains unchanged.\nfn parse_all(nums: &mut Vec<u32>, strings: &[&str]) -> Result<(), ParseIntError> {\nlet mut nums = DropGuard::new(nums, move |nums| {\nnums.truncate(len);    // ← 1. Reset the vec on failure\nfor s in strings {\nnums.push(s.parse()?); // ← 2. If `?` returns an error, roll back\nDropGuard::dismiss(nums);  // ← 3. Parsing successful, dismiss the guard\nAs Rust's unsafe code guidelines are still being decided, it became clear that it would be very useful to decide what exactly it means to create zero-sized types in an unsafe way, and what we should recommend for people to do this.\nThis question is answered in Rust 1.100 with a single function: std::mem::conjure_zst.\nInstead of transmuting (), MaybeUninit::uninit().assume_init(), or various other incantations, this is the dedicated way to create a value of a zero-sized type when you know that doing so is safe to do. If an inhabited type has no size, we know exactly what value it must be: nothing.\nOf course, APIs often rely on the inability to construct such types for safety, and that's why this function is unsafe. For example, PyO3's Python<'py> is a zero-sized marker token that represents holding the Python GIL (global interpreter lock). Many of Python's APIs require holding this lock to be safe, but conjuring a token from thin air would allow safe PyO3 calls to violate that precondition.\nReporting unused dependencies in Cargo\nBuilding dependencies that are no longer used or are only needed for specific platforms can slow down builds.\nRustc has the unused_crate_dependencies lint for this but it has false positives when used with Cargo because dependencies may be shared between multiple build targets, e.g. dependencies.tracing-subscriber being available for every bin in a package when only some may use it.\nCargo now reports unused dependencies to users, e.g.\n23 | tracing-subscriber.workspace = true\n= note: `cargo::unused_dependencies` is set to `warn` by default\nhelp: consider removing the dependency on `tracing-subscriber`\nA limitation of this warning is that it will only appear when building every build target that may use an entry in a dependencies table.\nFor example, running cargo check --bin foo won't show warnings for unused [dependencies] because another bin may be present that uses them.\nNo warning will be shown for unused dev-dependencies because there isn't a way to ensure every development build target is built.\nThis warning is being introduced as part of a new linting system for Cargo, controlled by the [lints.cargo] table in Cargo.toml, completing this project goal. See also the other lints that have been introduced.\nCargo builds all libraries and binaries in the build-dir and then uplifts final artifacts to the target-dir. At this time, these share a common parent directory.\nDifferent build configurations, like --features, can lead to different copies of libraries and binaries in the build-dir.\nThese copies are kept unique by a build-configuration hash but this hash was not always applied due to platform constraints.\nThis can lead to extra rebuilds, missed rebuilds, confusion over what binaries or libraries are used, and so on.\nThese problems get exacerbated when sharing a build-dir across git worktrees.\nThe build-dir now consistently applies the build-configuration hash to all content by organizing the directories around their build step, rather than their file type, and moving the hash from a file's name to the parent directory.\nThis work began with the build directory layout project goal in 2025, and its stabilization is a milestone toward a new project goal, unblocking future improvements such as cache garbage collection, cross-workspace cache de-duplication, cross-workspace caching, and fine grain locking.\nWe previously published a call for testing for the changes that are now taking effect.\nWhile this is an internal change, this may break build processes and tests that had workarounds for missing or incomplete features.\nIn some cases, the workaround is no longer needed.\nSee cargo#17287 for possible solutions and to add any new use cases.\nCargo min-publish-age a.k.a. dependency cooldowns\nMalicious versions of packages are often caught within hours or days after being published, by either automated scanners or by the community.\nRust 1.100 stabilizes Cargo's min-publish-age aware dependency resolution, which lets you wait for that window before it picks up newly published versions.\nThis practice is also known as dependency cooldowns.\nYou can opt in via .cargo/config.toml:\nWhen selecting versions, Cargo now skips any version published more recently than that, and tells you what it held back:\nLocking 2 packages to highest compatible version as of min-publish-age\nAdding cc v1.11.0 (available: v1.18.0, published 3 hours ago)\nAdding clap 4.6.7 (available: 4.6.9, published 2 days ago)\nWhen a new version is needed right away, you can override the check for a single update:\n$ CARGO_RESOLVER_INCOMPATIBLE_PUBLISH_AGE=allow cargo update clap --precise 4.6.9\nUpdating clap 4.6.7 -> clap 4.6.9 (published 2 days ago, minimum age 3 days)\nThe newer version will be recorded in Cargo.lock and kept from then on until a later cargo update re-resolves it.\nA few things to keep in mind:\nThis depends on the registry recording when each version was published.\nVersions without a pubtime field in the index are never held back.\ncargo install ignores the minimum publish age, for both the installed crate and its dependencies.\nPin a version with cargo install foo@1.2.3 if needed.\nCooldowns reduce the risk from some supply chain attacks but are not a complete defense on their own.\nPlease keep reviewing dependency updates as you otherwise would.\nFurther work to protect the ecosystem is planned under the crates.io supply chain hardening project goal, including quarantine support and automated detection of suspicious releases.\nimpl PartialEq<Cow<'_, [U]>> for Vec<T, A>\nimpl PartialEq<Cow<'_, [U]>> for &[T]\nimpl PartialEq<Cow<'_, [U]>> for &mut [T]\nimpl<A: Allocator> Allocator for &A\nimpl<T: Allocator, A: Allocator> Allocator for Box<T, A>\nimpl<A: Allocator> Allocator for &mut A\nimpl<T: Allocator, A: Allocator> Allocator for Arc<T, A>\nimpl<T: Allocator, A: Allocator> Allocator for Rc<T, A>\nCheck out everything that changed in Rust, Cargo, and Clippy.\nMany people came together to create Rust 1.100.0. We couldn't have done it without all of you. Thanks!",
