      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-12-i-m-making-llms-work-on-small-and-old-devices",
      "title": "I'm making LLMs work on small and old devices",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://github.com/hyperionaidev/smolmaxxing/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50468888\nPoints: 245\n# Comments: 86",
      "body": "Run the biggest language model you can on the smallest hardware you own.\nTV boxes, old tablets, single-board computers and routers all have a CPU, some RAM and flash storage that sit idle most of the day. smolmaxxing finds the biggest model each of them can really run, then pushes it further with custom kernels, careful memory use and weights streamed from flash. Every number in this repo was measured on the device itself.\n📺 Xiaomi TV Box S (3rd Gen)\n1.9 GB (about 1 GB usable)\n1 GB (about 700 MB usable)\nBonsai-1.7B (1-bit) at 24-26 tok/s\nLing-mini-2.0, 16B, at 5.1 tok/s\nLing-mini-2.0, 16B, at 2.6-4.5 tok/s\nBoth devices run 8B and 16B mixture-of-experts models on about 1 GB of RAM by keeping only part of each model in memory and reading the rest from flash as it's needed.\nMore devices (phones, single-board computers, routers) are coming. The techniques aren't tied to one box: the 1-bit kernel needs only basic NEON, and the flash streaming works on any Linux or Android device with more model than RAM.\nAll of it is a patch on llama.cpp. The main ideas:\nKernels built for the chip. A 1-bit lookup-table kernel, repacked 2-bit and ternary formats for the Cortex-A55, and versions of them for the A7, which has no dot-product instructions.\nLess RAM per model. Most of a small model's vocabulary is rarely used, so we drop those output rows. On the TV, a \"headless\" mode also stops the launcher and Google services while a model runs.\nExperts paged from flash. A mixture-of-experts model only uses a few experts per token. We keep the popular ones in RAM, read the others from flash on demand and prefetch the next layer's. This is how 8B and 16B models run on 1 GB.\nStreaming for dense models bigger than RAM, at the flash's full speed.\ndocs/how-it-works.md has every technique with its measured gain.\nThe quickest start is a ready-made model file from Hugging Face. They need the patched llama.cpp: stock llama.cpp can't load them.\nTV box or another ARMv8.2 Android device: docs/tv-box-setup.md\nJailbroken iPad on iOS 12: ipad-air/README.md\nAll model files and how to rebuild them: docs/models.md\ndevice #1: every model run on the TV box, speed and quality\ndevice #2: the iPad Air results, setup and scripts\nthe kernels, memory tricks, expert pager and streaming engine\ndownloads for both devices, rebuild targets and checksums\nwhat we learned, including what didn't work\nthe full lab notebook: 160 experiments, failures included\nevery change to llama.cpp for the TV box (the iPad's patch is in ipad-air/patches/)\nmodel rebuilding, GGUF rewriting, benchmarks and kernel checks\nthe live token-stream chat page and its launcher\ndevice #2: iOS 12 build, smolchat, deploy and benchmark scripts\nevery chat benchmark (results.jsonl) and the raw logs\nvocabulary lists and evaluation texts\nthe first experiment: a 260K-parameter story model\nzig wrappers for cross-compiling static aarch64 binaries\nMIT. The llama.cpp patches are derivatives of llama.cpp (MIT). Model weights keep their own licenses, and the evaluation texts are Wikipedia excerpts (CC BY-SA 4.0).\nBuilt on llama.cpp and llama2.c. Models from Qwen, unsloth (GGUFs and importance matrices), PrismML (Bonsai), inclusionAI (Ling-mini-2.0), tablinum (Moon-Beam), bartowski and mradermacher (GGUFs), Google (Gemma) and stories260K. The iPad is jailbroken with Amethyst. The banner uses Archivo (SIL OFL) and is rendered by tools/banner/render.sh. The 🤏 logo is the pinching-hand emoji from Google's Noto Emoji (Apache 2.0).",
