      "id": "bbc-2026-11-12-what-in-the-world",
      "title": "What in the World",
      "summary": "Home News Sport Weather iPlayer Sounds Bitesize CBeebies CBBC Food Home News Sport Business Technology Health Culture Arts Travel Earth Audio Video Live Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsWorld Service,·12 Nov 2026,·8 minsAvailable for over a yearIt’s n…",
      "body": "Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live          Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsWorld Service,·12 Nov 2026,·8 minsAvailable for over a yearIt’s not unusual to receive an email that your personal information has been accessed through a data breach or cyber attack these days. This happened to millions of ASOS customers in October. Or you might have logged onto a site and been told to change your password because it’s been found in a hack.\nLoads of us are talking about how to protect yourself online, especially in cases where companies are responsible for handling your sensitive information.\nBBC’s Cyber Correspondent Joe Tidy tells us about some of the methods cyber criminals use to carry out their hacks. We also get Tejal Reddy, a software engineer and cyber-security content creator to answer some of your online safety questions and concerns.\nWhatsApp: +44 330 12 33 22 6\nProducers: Mollie Perella and Chelsea Coates\nEditor: Emily HorlerProgramme WebsiteMore episodes",
