      "id": "bbc-2026-11-12-poundland-gets-more-time-to-secure-a-rescue-deal-as-emergenc",
      "title": "Poundland gets more time to secure a rescue deal as emergency talks continue",
      "summary": "A deadline to secure a buyer was set to expire at midnight, but the retailer now has an extra 10 days to find a buyer.",
      "body": "Poundland has avoided immediate collapse after a deadline was extended to find a rescue deal.A deadline to secure a buyer for the retail chain was set to expire at midnight, but a court has given Poundland an extra 10 days to find a buyer.The discount chain, which has 11,000 employees, has been edging towards administration after being blocked by owners Gordon Brothers from accessing cash in its bank accounts.A committee of MPs questioned Gordon Brothers following accusations that the investment firm hoped to profit from selling off Poundland's 600 shops and stock piecemeal.Gordon Brothers - which bought the chain for just £1 last year - is accused of forcing that outcome by restricting Poundland's access to available cash needed to restock shops, and pay wages and rent, ahead of the crucial Christmas trading period. The investment firm, which specialises in buying and restructuring distressed companies, has denied the claims.However, pressure remains on Poundland's owners after a takeover bid was rejected. The decision prompted calls for answers from politicians, including Prime Minister Andy Burnham.About £50m had been frozen by Gordon Brothers, the BBC understands, meaning Poundland bosses were unable to restock shelves across its 600 shops.The BBC understands that Gordon Brothers agreed to release a further £11m today from Poundland's accounts, on top of £10m announced earlier, as the firm seeks to find a new buyer.Gordon Brothers said it was in talks with three parties for a sale of the whole business, and it had received \"several offers from established high street retailers for significant parts of the business\".Views on the chain's performance differ between those running operations and the owners. Poundland boss Williams told staff last week that like-for-like sales, which exclude new store openings, were up 11.4%. The sales period included Halloween, but Williams said this was £1.5m ahead of budget.He added that the business made a profit of £18.1m in the year to September, which was a \"strong recovery\" from the previous year when there were losses of £68m.However, Gordon Brothers has painted a different picture, saying the chain remains \"challenged and vulnerable to any potential downturn in future trading\". It has argued that the best way of securing jobs may be to split up the business and sell chunks of its 600 sites to other retailers.There has been interest from the likes of Poundstretcher, Tesco and B&M in picking up Poundland shops, which could mean continuing employment for workers at those sites.The main issue is whether a piecemeal sale would protect more jobs than a buyer willing to keep Poundland as a national chain.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-12T14:29:59.000Z",
