    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-11-12-nvidia-s-jensen-huang-resigns-chipmaker-names-cfo-colette-kr",
      "title": "Nvidia’s Jensen Huang resigns, chipmaker names CFO Colette Kress as interim CEO",
      "summary": "Nvidia gets a new chief executive, days after former CEO Jensen Huang admitted to a romantic affair with an alleged Chinese spy.",
      "body": "Jensen Huang has resigned from his position as president and chief executive of Nvidia, the company said on Thursday, just a few days after he admitted to a romantic affair with an alleged Chinese spy, sparking a U.S. national security crisis.\nIn a regulatory filing, Nvidia said its board accepted Huang’s resignation on Wednesday.\nNvidia said its current chief financial officer and executive vice president, Collete Kress, will take over as interim CEO until the company finds a permanent successor. Kress will continue to serve as the company’s CFO.\nNvidia’s shares were down more than 11% in premarket trading on Thursday. Since Friday, the company’s stock has fallen dramatically, resulting in a market cap loss of more than $300 billion.\nThe potential implications of Huang’s departure for the AI and data center industries are vast, given how active Huang, and by extension Nvidia, has been in furthering the AI boom. In particular, it raises questions about the future of Nvidia’s sprawling AI investments, which have helped bankroll everything from startups developing new technologies to the enormous data centers needed to run them.\nUnder Huang, Nvidia deftly used its financial muscle to support companies buying its chips, effectively helping to finance the market for its own products, as well as lobbying Washington to relax AI rules .\nNvidia’s board has also appointed Harvey Jones, a managing partner of Square Wave Ventures and former CEO of Synopsys, as the company’s first-ever chair of the board of directors. Jones has been on Nvidia’s board since 1993.\n“Jensen’s contributions to NVIDIA are profound. NVIDIA is one of the most consequential technology companies in the world. The Board and the company thank Jensen for his immeasurable contributions to the company and the industry,” the company said in a statement.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nRam is a financial and tech reporter and editor. He covered North American and European M&A, equity, regulatory news and debt markets at Reuters and Acuris Global, and has also written about travel, tourism, entertainment and books.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Ram by emailing [email protected].",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Ram Iyer",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-12T14:10:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/GettyImages-2205210966.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/12/nvidias-jensen-huang-resigns-chipmaker-names-cfo-colette-kress-as-interim-ceo/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-11-12-nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-resigns-after-reports-of-extramarita",
      "title": "Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang resigns after reports of extramarital relationships",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-resigns-reports-extramarital-relationships-rcna608402\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50467191\nPoints: 11\n# Comments: 2",
      "body": "Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has resigned in the wake of reports that he had multiple extramarital relationships, including one with a  suspected agent of China and another with an executive who reports to him — a stunning turn of events for the head of a company at the heart of the global AI investment boom.  In a statement, Nvidia’s board said: “Colette Kress, current Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer of NVIDIA, has agreed to serve as Interim Chief Executive Officer.” The company also named its first chairman. That role will be filled by Harvey Jones, who is a former CEO of major chip company Synopsys. The addition of a chairman is a major governance overhaul at the company that for decades relied solely on Huang. Nvidia’s board met Wednesday night to address Huang’s future at the company, a source familiar with the matter told NBC News, adding that the White House was aware of the meeting. Nvidia shares plunged more than 11% after the board’s announcement. Huang, 63, had been with Nvidia since he co-founded the Silicon Valley chipmaker in the early 1990s, and he presided over the company during its meteoric rise amid the AI boom of the past several years. In Collette Kress, Nvidia’s board selected a steady hand to steer the company in its most uncertain period ever. Kress has been the company’s chief financial officer since 2013. She is a familiar face to Wall Street, regularly appearing at conferences and discussing the intricacies of the company’s sprawling operations. Before Nvidia, Kress worked at Cisco, another major technology company, and Microsoft. Kress, a highly regarded executive, is widely trusted by the Wall Street community. Huang long leaned on her during financial conferences and the company’s earnings calls to debrief the investing public about the latest developments with the company’s operations. Nvidia’s board said that it would search for a permanent CEO and consider internal and external candidates. Kress’ ascension to the CEO suite comes after the second largest public company in the world was hit with multiple scandals involving its founder.  On Wednesday, The New York Times, citing five people who worked at Nvidia, reported that Huang had an extramarital relationship with a married healthcare executive who reported to him, which appears to run afoul of Nvidia’s code of conduct. NBC News has not independently verified the claims. Nvidia and the executive did not respond to multiple requests for comment Wednesday about the Times’ report.  The report concerning the alleged relationship followed reports that Huang had an affair with Alice Huang, a businesswoman believed to be a spy for China. The two Huangs are not related. Secretary of State Marco Rubio circulated a memo to officials in the Trump administration this spring outlining how U.S. intelligence showed that Huang was in a romantic relationship with a business executive who was an asset of China’s spy service, according to a person familiar with Nvidia and a former national security official with knowledge of the matter. The New York Times and the Financial Times, citing people familiar with the matter, first reported on the memo last week. Alice Huang held a senior position at Megaspeed, a Singapore-based company that was buying advanced chips from Nvidia. Megaspeed and other related firms remain under U.S. criminal investigation of allegations they smuggled American-made chips into China.  Alice Huang’s precise role and relationship with China’s spy service is unclear. It is possible she was not a career spy for China’s spy agency, the Ministry of State Security, but was asked to cooperate and share information based on her access to Jensen Huang, according to the former national security official.  On Sunday, Nvidia’s board announced it was investigating whether Huang had fallen victim to a foreign intelligence plot. It said Tuesday that it had hired a former acting U.S. attorney and his law firm to conduct the investigation.  Huang apologized in a memo to employees and said he supported the independent investigation. “I was unfaithful to my wife,” he wrote. “My actions hurt her and our children. There is no excuse for what I did. I am deeply sorry.” Multiple Washington officials have called for greater scrutiny of Nvidia and for Huang to testify about his relationship with the suspected Chinese agent in the wake of the reports. China has denied the allegations, calling them “purely unsubstantiated and directed at smearing China.” Even before Wednesday’s Times report, Nvidia shares had plunged 11% this week, wiping out nearly $500 billion in paper value. Huang’s departure leaves a host of unknowns at the company, the valuation of which has soared to more than $1 trillion amid insatiable demand for its GPU chips. Those specialized processors underpin the AI applications that have powered the stock market and economic growth writ large to new heights.  For years, Huang has effectively been the chief ambassador for the artificial intelligence industry, appearing regularly alongside heads of state and other CEOs as the industry spends trillions of dollars investing and building AI models and data centers. Nvidia acknowledged Huang’s contributions to the sector in its Thursday announcement. “Jensen’s contributions to NVIDIA are profound,” a statement said. “The Board and the company thank Jensen for his immeasurable contributions to the company and the industry.” After coming up with the idea for Nvidia at a Denny’s restaurant in Silicon Valley with co-founders Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem, Huang built the company into a $4 trillion global chip behemoth over the following 30-plus years. Before Wednesday’s report of a second affair, many high-profile figures across Silicon Valley, Wall Street and Washington had come to Huang’s defense. President Donald Trump told reporters Sunday that Huang had “done an amazing job for our country.” “I think he’s an honorable guy,” Trump said. “I think he probably has an excuse for it. You have to hear it.” Shaun Maguire, a partner at the venture capital group Sequoia, said on X that Huang is “one of the greatest entrepreneurs of this generation,” adding that “two mistakes don’t make a right, and I believe Jensen is irreplaceable for America’s AI leadership.” SpaceX and Tesla CEO Elon Musk responded under Maguire’s post, writing: “I agree.”  Before the decision, Musk said in a response to a post Wednesday night that it “would be an honor” to hire Huang.",
